window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("serving-inference", {"topics":[{"key":"serving-inference/model-serving","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"A trained model is a file of weights. Nothing about it answers a request. Someone has to put the weights\non a device, tokenize input, run the forward pass, sample, detokenize, stream the result back and do all\nof that for hundreds of users at once without one user's 20k-token document starving everyone else.\nThat set of responsibilities is \"serving\", and for LLMs it is its own discipline because the obvious\napproach fails on three fronts.\n\nThe obvious approach is to treat the model like any other prediction service: one request in, one\nforward pass, one response out, scale horizontally. Classic ML serving works this way because a\nclassifier's cost is fixed per request. An LLM request is not a forward pass; it is a loop of forward\npasses, one per generated token, and you do not know in advance how many there will be. A request that\nasked for a one-word answer and one that asked for a 2,000-word essay arrive looking identical.\n\nSecond, the loop is stateful. Each step needs the KV cache of every earlier token in that request, so the\nstate lives on the GPU for the life of the request and grows every step. Memory, not compute, usually\ncaps how many requests a card can hold.\n\nThird, GPUs are efficient only when busy with large matrix multiplications. A single request in decode\nreads all the weights to produce one token: for an 8B model in fp16 that is ~16 GB moved per token, so a\nGPU with ~3 TB/s of memory bandwidth cannot exceed roughly 200 tokens/s for that request no matter how\nmany FLOPs it has, and its arithmetic units sit almost idle. Put 32 requests in the same step and the\nweights are still read once, so the same ~16 GB now yields 32 tokens. Only batching many requests into\neach step recovers that efficiency, and batching variable-length, variable-lifetime\nloops is a scheduling problem the one-request-one-pass model never had to solve. The whole module exists\nto work through those three facts; this lesson is the map.","how":"### The layers of a serving stack\nFrom the outside in, a production LLM deployment looks like this:\n\n1. **API layer.** An HTTP server speaking a chat/completions protocol (most self-hosted engines expose an\n   OpenAI-compatible endpoint). It handles auth, rate limits, request validation, streaming via SSE and\n   cancellation when the client disconnects.\n2. **Router.** When there are several replicas, something picks one. Simple round-robin works until you\n   care about prefix-cache locality or model-per-tenant isolation, at which point routing becomes\n   content-aware.\n3. **Engine.** The process that owns the GPU(s): vLLM, SGLang, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, llama.cpp and others.\n   It loads weights (optionally quantized, optionally sharded across GPUs with tensor parallelism), owns the\n   KV-cache memory pool, and runs the scheduler.\n4. **Scheduler.** Each iteration it decides which waiting requests to admit for prefill and which running\n   requests get a decode step, subject to a token budget and free KV-cache memory. This is the heart of\n   the system; continuous batching and paged memory live here.\n5. **Model executor and kernels.** The forward pass itself: attention kernels that read the paged cache,\n   fused matmuls, CUDA graphs to cut launch overhead, sampling on-device.\n\n### The request lifecycle\nA request arrives, is tokenized, and waits in a queue. When the scheduler admits it, its prompt is run in\none **prefill** pass, which is compute-heavy and populates the KV cache. The first token comes out of that\npass; the time it took is the **time to first token (TTFT)**. The request then joins the running batch and\nreceives one **decode** step per iteration alongside every other running request; the spacing between its\ntokens is the **time per output token (TPOT)**. It leaves the batch when it emits an end-of-sequence token,\nhits `max_tokens`, or the client cancels, and its cache blocks are freed for the next request. Because\nrequests join and leave at iteration granularity rather than waiting for a whole batch to finish, the GPU\nstays full; that is continuous batching.\n\n### Why memory arithmetic comes first\nWeights are a fixed cost; the cache is variable. For an 8B-parameter model in fp16, weights are about\n16 GB, and each token of context costs a model-specific amount (around 128 KB for a GQA model with\n8 KV heads and 32 layers). The engine reserves a fraction of VRAM (vLLM's default is 90%) and gives what\nis left after weights to the cache pool. Free cache tokens divided by typical sequence length is the real\nconcurrency limit, and it is why \"how many users can one H100 serve\" has no answer until you know\nsequence lengths. The KV-cache memory estimation lesson does this arithmetic in full.\n\n### Where the trade-offs live\nEvery serving knob trades latency against throughput. A bigger batch raises tokens per second for the\nwhole GPU but lengthens each user's TPOT, because each decode step now reads more cache. A larger\n`max_model_len` admits longer documents but reduces how many requests fit. Quantizing weights or cache\nfrees memory and bandwidth at a quality cost you must measure. Speculative decoding spends extra compute to\ncut per-token latency. The point of the rest of this module is to make each of those trades deliberately.\n\n### Startup, cold start and scaling\nBefore an engine serves its first token it must load tens of gigabytes of weights (from local disk,\nnetwork storage or a model hub), profile a worst-case forward pass to size the cache pool, and usually\ncapture CUDA graphs. Depending on model size and storage this takes from tens of seconds to several\nminutes, which is why LLM replicas cannot be autoscaled like stateless web pods: a spike is over before a\nnew replica is warm. The usual answers are a warm floor of replicas sized for normal peak, weights\npre-staged on local NVMe or baked into the image, and scaling on queue depth or cache utilization (which\nlead) rather than CPU or request count (which lag or mislead).\n\n### Managed API versus self-hosting\nCalling a provider's API is serving outsourced: the same machinery runs on their side, and their pricing,\nrate limits and prompt-caching rules are the visible surface of it. Self-hosting buys control (data\nresidency, custom models, predictable cost at high utilization) at the price of owning all of the above.\nThe decision is covered in the infrastructure and economics module; what matters here is that the\nmechanics are identical, so understanding them makes you better at both.","example":"The smallest realistic stack: an OpenAI-compatible engine on one GPU, and a client that streams. The\nserver flags are the ones that encode the memory and concurrency decisions discussed above.\n\n```python\n# Terminal (one GPU, a GQA model that fits in fp16):\n#   vllm serve meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct \\\n#       --max-model-len 8192 --gpu-memory-utilization 0.9 \\\n#       --max-num-seqs 128 --enable-prefix-caching\n# The startup log prints how many KV-cache blocks were allocated; that number\n# times the block size is the pool of context tokens the scheduler can hand out.\n\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\n\nt0 = time.perf_counter()\nfirst = None\nn = 0\nstream = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a terse assistant.\"},\n              {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Explain continuous batching in 3 sentences.\"}],\n    max_tokens=200,\n    stream=True,                                   # SSE: one chunk per sampled token\n)\nfor chunk in stream:\n    if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n        if first is None:\n            first = time.perf_counter() - t0      # time to first token\n        n += 1\ntotal = time.perf_counter() - t0\nprint(f\"TTFT={first*1000:.0f} ms, chunks={n}, \"\n      f\"TPOT≈{(total-first)/max(n-1,1)*1000:.1f} ms/chunk\")\n# Run two copies of this script at once: TTFT barely moves, TPOT rises slightly,\n# and the server's tokens/sec roughly doubles — the signature of a batched decode loop.\n```","tradeoffs":["Batching more requests per step raises GPU throughput but lengthens each request's per-token latency; flips in favour of smaller batches when the product is interactive and TPOT is the user-visible metric.","Allowing long `max_model_len` serves document workloads but shrinks concurrency because every admitted request may claim that much cache; flips when p99 prompt length is far below the cap and admission uses actual lengths.","Self-hosting gives data control and a lower cost per token at high, steady utilization; a managed API wins when traffic is bursty or small, because idle GPUs cost the same as busy ones.","Streaming tokens improves perceived latency and lets clients cancel early, but it complicates proxies, retries and observability; it stops being worth it for short structured outputs consumed by machines."],"pitfalls":["Capacity-planning from weight size alone and forgetting the cache; symptom is the engine admitting far fewer concurrent requests than expected, or preempting them mid-generation under load.","Measuring latency with a single request and shipping; symptom is TPOT doubling the first time real concurrency arrives, because decode steps now read many caches per iteration.","Putting a generic HTTP load balancer in front of replicas with prefix caching enabled; symptom is a fleet-wide prefix hit rate far below what one replica achieves, because turn *n* of a conversation lands on a GPU that never prefilled turns 1 to *n−1*.","Treating `max_tokens` as cosmetic; symptom is a few runaway requests holding cache blocks for minutes and starving the queue, visible as TTFT spikes unrelated to request rate.","Ignoring client disconnects; symptom is GPU time spent generating tokens nobody reads, showing up as throughput that does not translate into completed requests.","Serving an instruct model with a chat template other than the one it was trained with (often a hand-built prompt string); symptom is no error at all, just answers that ramble, ignore the system prompt or fail to stop, because the model never sees the role markers it expects.","Autoscaling replicas on CPU utilization or request count; symptom is new replicas becoming ready minutes after the queue already blew through the TTFT budget, then idling after the spike."],"when":"You meet serving the moment a model leaves a notebook. Use this lesson as the map: when a question is\nabout *why* a number looks the way it does, find the layer it belongs to (API, router, scheduler, memory,\nkernels) and go to the deep lesson for that layer: prefill vs decode for the latency split, KV-cache\nmemory estimation for capacity, continuous batching and paging for scheduling, engines for which software\nto run. Measure TTFT, TPOT, tokens per second per GPU and cache utilization from day one, because every\nlater decision is made against those four. In interviews, the expected depth is to explain the request\nlifecycle end to end and to name which resource (memory, bandwidth, compute) binds at each phase.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Stands up an engine with an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, sets memory and concurrency flags from measured sequence lengths, and instruments TTFT and TPOT before tuning anything.","architect":"Owns the managed-versus-self-hosted decision, the GPU tier and replica count, and the routing design that keeps tenant isolation and cache locality from fighting each other.","fde":"Deploys the stack inside a customer's VPC, explains why their latency differs from the demo, and translates their document sizes and user counts into a concrete GPU footprint."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs an 8B model on one 80 GB GPU and reports that p50 latency is fine but p99 TTFT\nspikes to several seconds every few minutes with no change in request rate. Walk through where in the\nserving stack you would look, and why.","a":"TTFT is dominated by queueing plus prefill, so periodic spikes with flat request rate point at\nadmission, not load. First suspect is a few very long prompts: a 20k-token prefill is compute-heavy and,\nwithout chunked prefill, blocks a whole iteration so every waiting request's TTFT jumps. Second is memory:\nif long-running generations fill the KV-cache pool, new requests wait for blocks to free, and the engine\nmay preempt running sequences, which shows as both TTFT and TPOT spikes. Check the engine's metrics for\nqueue depth, cache utilization and preemption count at the spike times. Fixes, in order: cap `max_tokens`\nand prompt length per endpoint, enable chunked prefill so long prompts are interleaved with decode steps,\nand if cache utilization is pinned at 100%, either lower concurrency, quantize the cache, or add a replica."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is serving an LLM fundamentally different from serving a classification model?","options":["LLM weights are larger, so they must be loaded lazily from disk per request","Each LLM request is a stateful loop of unknown length whose working set grows while it runs","LLM requests cannot be batched, so each one needs a dedicated GPU","Classifiers return probabilities while LLMs return text, which changes the serialization layer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A classifier costs one fixed forward pass; an LLM request runs one forward pass per generated token, holds a growing KV cache on the GPU throughout, and finishes at an unpredictable time. Batching is not only possible but essential, which is the opposite of the third option."},{"q":"Which single quantity most directly determines how many concurrent requests one GPU can hold?","options":["Peak FLOPS of the GPU divided by model parameter count","Number of CPU cores available to the tokenizer","Free VRAM after weights, divided by per-request KV-cache size at typical sequence length","Network bandwidth between the API server and the engine"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrency is a memory question: each running request pins its KV cache for its whole lifetime. FLOPS affects how fast steps run, not how many requests fit, and the tokenizer and network are rarely the binding resource."},{"q":"A team doubles concurrent users on the same GPU and sees per-token latency rise while total tokens/sec also rises. What is happening?","options":["The GPU is thermally throttling under the extra load","The tokenizer has become the bottleneck","Decode is bandwidth-bound; larger batches amortize weight reads but each step now reads more KV cache","Prefix caching is evicting entries faster than it can fill them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Batching lets one weight read serve many requests, so aggregate throughput climbs. But each decode step also reads every running request's cache, so step time grows and every user's TPOT lengthens. That is the expected shape of the throughput-versus-latency curve, not a fault."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/inference-optimization","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding"],"why":"A GPU has a fixed ratio of arithmetic throughput to memory bandwidth. An H100 can do on the order of a\nthousand teraFLOPs of dense fp16 math but move only a few terabytes per second from HBM, so it needs\nroughly a few hundred floating-point operations per byte loaded to keep its arithmetic units busy. Each\ntransformer matmul applied to a batch of *B* tokens performs about 2·B FLOPs per weight element while\nreading that element once, so its arithmetic intensity is about *B* FLOPs per byte. A single-request\ndecode step has *B* = 1. The GPU spends essentially all of its time streaming weights and cache through\nmemory and almost none computing. That is a tensor-core utilization well under one percent (an 8B model\nat batch 1 does ~16 GFLOPs in a ~5 ms step, a few TFLOP/s against a peak near a thousand), and it is the\ndefault state of a naive LLM server.\n\nThe naive response, \"buy a bigger GPU\", fails because a GPU with more FLOPs and similar bandwidth makes\ndecode no faster. The second naive response, \"run more requests at once\", is actually correct, but it\nruns into memory: each concurrent request pins a KV cache, and the pool fills long before the batch is\nlarge enough to be compute-bound. Every optimization technique is a way around one of those two walls:\nmove fewer bytes per token, do less work per request, or fit more requests into the same bytes. The\ndiscipline is matching the lever to the wall you are actually hitting, which is why measurement precedes\ntuning.","how":"### Start from the roofline\nFor a decode step at batch size *B* with weights of *W* bytes and a total KV cache of *C* bytes across the\nbatch:\n\n```\ntime_per_step ≈ max( (W + C) / bandwidth ,  2·B·params / FLOPs )\n```\n\nThe left term is the bandwidth floor and dominates until *B* reaches the GPU's ridge point (a few hundred\ntokens per step on current hardware). Prefill processes the whole prompt as one batch of *B* = prompt\nlength tokens, so it sits on the right-hand, compute-bound side. Each lever below shrinks one term.\n\n### Levers that move fewer bytes (help decode)\n- **Weight quantization** (int8, fp8, 4-bit formats such as AWQ or GPTQ): halves or quarters *W*, so the\n  batch-1 bandwidth floor drops proportionally. It also frees VRAM for more cache. Quality must be\n  evaluated per model and task, because the loss is not uniform.\n- **KV-cache quantization** (fp8 or int8 cache): shrinks *C*, which matters once the batch is large enough\n  that cache reads rival weight reads. Long-context, high-concurrency workloads benefit most.\n- **Architecture choices**: grouped-query attention and multi-head latent attention cut cache bytes per\n  token by sharing or compressing K/V. You pick these by picking the model, not by tuning.\n\n### Levers that do less work per request\n- **Prefix caching**: skip prefill for a prompt prefix already in cache. Cuts TTFT and compute for shared\n  system prompts, few-shot blocks and multi-turn chat.\n- **Prompt and output discipline**: shorter prompts cut prefill compute linearly; a sensible `max_tokens`\n  and stop sequences stop decode from generating tokens nobody uses.\n- **Speculative decoding**: a small draft model (or a lightweight head) proposes several tokens; the large\n  model verifies them in one forward pass. Verification of *k* tokens costs barely more than one decode\n  step because that step was bandwidth-bound anyway, so accepted drafts come almost free. If each drafted\n  token is accepted with probability *α* and *k* are drafted, the expected tokens produced per target\n  pass is (1 − α^(k+1)) / (1 − α): with α = 0.8 and k = 4 that is about 3.4 tokens per pass instead of 1,\n  minus the draft's own cost. The output distribution is unchanged, because rejection sampling corrects\n  every draft to the target model's distribution. It helps when the batch is small and the draft is\n  accurate (acceptance falls on creative or unusual text); at large batch the GPU is already busy and\n  it can hurt.\n- **Constrained decoding**: masking logits with a grammar or JSON schema removes retries for invalid\n  output, an optimization at the request level rather than the kernel level. Computing the mask is CPU\n  work per step, so a slow grammar engine can itself become the bottleneck at high batch.\n\n### Levers that fit more work into the same bytes\n- **Continuous batching**: keep the running batch full by admitting and retiring requests every iteration.\n  Each weight read then serves many tokens, pushing *B* toward the ridge point.\n- **Paged KV cache**: eliminate fragmentation so the memory pool holds more sequences, which is what makes\n  large batches reachable.\n- **Chunked prefill**: split long prompts into chunks interleaved with decode steps so prefill's compute\n  and decode's bandwidth demand overlap rather than queue.\n- **Disaggregated prefill and decode**: run the phases on separate GPU pools and ship the KV cache\n  between them, so long prefills stop stalling decode batches and each pool is sized for its own bound.\n  It pays at large scale with mixed traffic; below that, chunked prefill gets most of the benefit.\n- **Tensor parallelism**: shard weights across GPUs so each reads a fraction of *W* per step; adds\n  all-reduce communication per layer, so it pays off when a model does not fit or when per-GPU bandwidth is\n  the limit.\n\n### Levers at the kernel level\n- **Fused attention kernels** (FlashAttention and its successors) avoid materializing the full attention\n  matrix, cutting memory traffic in prefill and enabling long contexts.\n- **Fused elementwise ops and CUDA graphs** remove per-kernel launch overhead, which at batch 1 can be a\n  surprisingly large fraction of a step.\n- **Compiled or engine-specific runtimes** (TensorRT-LLM style) specialize kernels to the model shape.\n\n### Levers outside the engine\nRoute easy requests to a smaller model, cache whole responses for repeated queries, distill a task-specific\nsmall model, or precompute embeddings. These often beat any engine knob because they cut the number of\ntokens that touch an expensive GPU in the first place.\n\n### The order of operations\nMeasure TTFT, TPOT, tokens/sec per GPU, batch size per step and KV-cache utilization. If batch size is\nsmall and the pool is far from full, you are latency-bound per request: quantize weights, add speculative\ndecoding, trim prompts. If the pool is full, you are memory-bound: quantize cache, enable prefix caching,\nchoose a GQA model, add GPUs. If prefill dominates and TTFT is the complaint, enable chunked prefill,\nprefix caching and shorter prompts. Changing a knob without knowing which regime you are in is how teams\n\"optimize\" into a slower system.","example":"A tiny roofline calculator that reproduces the reasoning above. The hardware numbers are rounded, publicly\nquoted figures and are only meant to show the shape of the result, not to predict a benchmark.\n\n```python\ndef decode_step_ms(params_b, batch, ctx_tokens, kv_bytes_per_token,\n                   weight_bytes=2, bw_tb_s=3.0, tflops=900):\n    \"\"\"Lower bound on one decode iteration: bandwidth floor vs compute floor.\"\"\"\n    W = params_b * 1e9 * weight_bytes                # bytes of weights read per step\n    C = batch * ctx_tokens * kv_bytes_per_token      # bytes of KV cache read per step\n    t_bw = (W + C) / (bw_tb_s * 1e12)\n    t_fl = 2 * batch * params_b * 1e9 / (tflops * 1e12)\n    bound = \"bandwidth\" if t_bw >= t_fl else \"compute\"\n    return 1000 * max(t_bw, t_fl), bound\n\nKV8B = 2 * 32 * 8 * 128 * 2        # 8B GQA model, fp16 cache: ~128 KB per token\nfor batch in (1, 8, 64, 256):\n    ms, b = decode_step_ms(8, batch, ctx_tokens=2048, kv_bytes_per_token=KV8B)\n    print(f\"fp16 batch={batch:4d}: {ms:6.2f} ms/step, {batch/ms*1000:8.0f} tok/s  ({b}-bound)\")\n\nms4, _ = decode_step_ms(8, 1, 2048, KV8B, weight_bytes=0.5)   # 4-bit weights, fp16 cache\nprint(f\"4-bit batch=1: {ms4:.2f} ms/step  -> weight bytes were the floor, so ~3-4x faster\")\n# Illustrative output shape: ~5.4 ms at batch 1 (~180 tok/s); by batch 256 the step is\n# ~5x longer while aggregate tok/s is ~50x higher, and the growth is all KV-cache traffic.\n# Note the roofline is not a capacity check: 256 x 2048 tokens of cache is 64 GiB, which would\n# not fit beside 16 GB of weights on an 80 GB card; the memory-estimation lesson covers that.\n```\n\nRead it as: at batch 1 the step time is almost entirely weight traffic, so quantizing weights is the lever;\nat batch 256 with 2k contexts the cache reads already exceed the weight reads, so cache size (GQA, cache\nquantization) is the lever, and the compute term only takes over at larger batches with short contexts. The same script with `ctx_tokens=32000` shows cache reads overtaking weight reads at modest\nbatch sizes, which is when KV quantization and GQA start to pay.","tradeoffs":["Weight quantization cuts bytes per step and VRAM but costs some accuracy; it flips against you when the task is sensitive (math, code, long reasoning) and no evaluation has been run on it.","Speculative decoding lowers per-token latency at small batch by spending extra compute; at high utilization the GPU has no spare compute and it can reduce throughput.","Larger batches raise throughput per GPU but lengthen every user's TPOT; the right point depends on whether you sell tokens per dollar or seconds per answer.","Tensor parallelism spreads bandwidth across GPUs but adds inter-GPU communication every layer; it stops helping once the model fits comfortably and the interconnect is slow.","Routing to a smaller model or caching responses can beat every engine knob, but only if you have an evaluation that proves the cheaper path meets the quality bar."],"pitfalls":["Tuning for throughput on a batch benchmark and shipping to an interactive product; symptom is good tokens/sec dashboards alongside user complaints about slow typing.","Enabling speculative decoding under heavy load; symptom is throughput dropping while per-request latency barely improves, because verification steals compute from the batch.","Quantizing to 4-bit without a task-specific eval; symptom is a regression that only appears on the hard tail of the evaluation set, weeks after deployment.","Chasing kernel-level gains while prompts carry thousands of tokens of boilerplate; symptom is prefill dominating TTFT no matter what engine flags change.","Reading vendor peak FLOPS as a throughput promise; symptom is utilization in the single digits and a purchase order for faster GPUs that do not help decode.","Load-testing with a prompt/output length mix that differs from production (say, 500 in / 100 out when real traffic is 6k in / 800 out); symptom is a lever that won in the benchmark doing nothing, or harm, in production, because the benchmark sat in a different regime."],"when":"Reach for optimization when a measured number misses a target: TTFT above the budget, TPOT making text\nfeel slow, or cost per million tokens above plan. Classify the regime first (bandwidth-bound decode,\ncompute-bound prefill, or memory-capacity-bound concurrency) and pick the lever that attacks it. Do not\noptimize a workload you have not profiled, and do not stack levers blindly: quantization plus speculative\ndecoding plus aggressive batching interact. In day-to-day work this is a loop of change one flag, re-run\nthe load test, compare TTFT/TPOT/throughput and a quality eval. In interviews, the expected answer to \"how\nwould you make this faster\" starts with \"which bound are we hitting\", not with a technique.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the load test, reads batch size and cache utilization from engine metrics, and changes one flag at a time with a quality eval in the loop.","architect":"Sets the latency and cost targets that define which regime matters, chooses GQA models and GPU tiers accordingly, and decides where routing to smaller models is acceptable.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's slow deployment by classifying the bottleneck from their metrics, then proposes the cheapest lever rather than the most impressive one."},"check":{"q":"A team serves a 70B model across two GPUs with tensor parallelism. Batch size per step averages 3,\nthe KV-cache pool is 30% used, and users complain about slow token streaming. Which optimizations would\nyou try first, which would you avoid, and why?","a":"Batch 3 with a nearly empty cache pool means they are not memory-capacity-bound; they are\nbandwidth-bound per step, reading 140 GB of fp16 weights for three tokens. The first lever is weight\nquantization (fp8 or 4-bit with an eval), which cuts bytes per step directly; the second is speculative\ndecoding, which is ideal at low batch because the verification step is nearly free. Checking that CUDA\ngraphs and a fused attention kernel are enabled is cheap. Avoid adding GPUs for capacity or quantizing the\nKV cache: the pool is not the constraint and cache traffic is small at batch 3. Also avoid raising batch\nlimits; there is no demand to fill them. If traffic later grows and the pool fills, the answer changes."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does weight quantization speed up single-request decode even though the GPU was not compute-limited?","options":["Smaller numbers are faster to multiply on tensor cores","Decode at batch 1 is bound by bytes read per step, and quantization halves or quarters those bytes","Quantized models have fewer layers to run","It reduces the number of tokens that must be generated"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At batch 1 the step time is the time to stream the weights through memory. Fewer bytes per weight means less traffic and a lower floor. Multiply speed is not the limit, and quantization changes neither depth nor output length."},{"q":"When does speculative decoding stop paying off?","options":["When the draft model is smaller than the target model","When prompts are longer than 1,000 tokens","When the batch is large enough that the GPU is already compute-busy, so verification has no slack to use","When the KV cache is quantized"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculation exploits idle compute during bandwidth-bound decode. Once batching has made the step compute-bound, verifying drafted tokens competes with real work, and throughput falls. The draft being smaller is the normal setup, not a failure condition."},{"q":"Which measurement tells you whether to prioritize KV-cache reductions over weight reductions?","options":["Average prompt length in characters","Whether the cache pool is near full and cache bytes read per step rival weight bytes","The GPU's peak FLOPS rating","Tokenizer vocabulary size"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cache levers (quantized cache, GQA models, prefix caching) matter when concurrency and context make the cache the dominant memory traffic or the thing that caps concurrency. If the pool is mostly empty and batches are small, weight bytes dominate and weight quantization is the lever."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/prefill-vs-decode","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Agrawal et al., 2023 — SARATHI: Efficient LLM Inference by Piggybacking Decodes with Chunked Prefills","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving"],"why":"The split is not a design choice; it falls out of autoregression. The prompt is known in full before\ngeneration starts, so all of its tokens can be pushed through the model together as one big matrix\nmultiplication per layer. Generated tokens are not known in advance, so each depends on the previous one\nand must be produced sequentially. Same weights, same layers, radically different shapes of work.\n\nWhy does that matter enough to deserve its own lesson? Because a GPU's efficiency depends almost entirely\non the shape of the work. A matmul over 2,000 prompt tokens reads each weight once and uses it 2,000\ntimes; the arithmetic units are saturated. A decode step over one token reads the same weights and uses\neach once; the GPU spends the step waiting on memory. The result is that prefill of a 4,000-token prompt\ntakes about as long as a dozen or so decode steps, not 4,000 of them (with the illustrative numbers\nbelow: ~70 ms of compute-bound prefill against ~5.5 ms per bandwidth-bound step), and that a faster GPU\nhelps prefill a lot and decode barely at all.\n\nThe naive mental model, \"cost is proportional to tokens\", gets both pricing and scheduling wrong. It\npredicts that a long prompt with a short answer and a short prompt with a long answer cost the same; in\npractice the second one occupies the GPU far longer. It also predicts that batching helps uniformly, when\nin fact batching is what rescues decode and does almost nothing for prefill. Every serving metric that\nmatters, TTFT, TPOT, throughput, is really a statement about one phase or the other, and you cannot tune\nwhat you have not separated.","how":"### Prefill\nInput: the prompt's *P* token embeddings as a `[P, d_model]` matrix. Each layer runs its projections and\nMLP as dense matmuls over all *P* rows at once, computes causal attention among the *P* positions, and\nwrites K and V for every position into the KV cache. The last position's logits produce the first output\ntoken. Cost scales with *P* for the matmuls and with *P²* for attention (mitigated by fused attention\nkernels that avoid materializing the full matrix). Arithmetic intensity is roughly *P* FLOPs per weight\nbyte, so for any prompt longer than a few hundred tokens the phase is compute-bound: TTFT grows roughly\nlinearly with prompt length and shrinks with more FLOPs.\n\n### Decode\nInput: one new token, shape `[1, d_model]` per request. Each layer runs the same matmuls over a single row,\ncomputes attention of that one query against every cached K and V, and appends one K and one V. Per step,\nthe GPU must read all weights plus the entire KV cache of the request, and the useful work is a handful of\nFLOPs per byte. Step time is therefore approximately `(weight_bytes + cache_bytes) / bandwidth`, and\nTPOT is almost independent of prompt length except through the cache-read term. Batching *B* requests\nreads the weights once for *B* tokens, which is why continuous batching is the throughput lever for decode\nand irrelevant for prefill. The amortization is only partial, though: every request's KV cache is its own\nand must still be read separately, so the cache term grows linearly with *B* and context length, and at\nlarge batches of long contexts it, not the weights, sets the step time. That is why decode at scale is\nlimited by cache bytes (GQA, cache quantization) as much as by weight bytes.\n\n### Why they interfere\nBoth phases run on the same GPU inside the same iteration. If the scheduler admits a 10k-token prompt\ninto a batch of 50 decoding requests, that iteration takes as long as the prefill, and all 50 users see a\npause in their token stream: a TPOT spike caused by someone else's TTFT. Conversely, prioritizing decode\nmakes new arrivals wait and TTFT climbs. The two phases trade against each other inside every scheduling\ndecision.\n\n### Techniques that manage the collision\n- **Chunked prefill.** Split long prompts into fixed-size chunks (a token budget per iteration) and run\n  each chunk alongside the decode batch. The decode tokens ride along at near-zero marginal cost because\n  the iteration was going to be compute-bound anyway, and no single iteration becomes a stall. The cost\n  is slightly longer TTFT for the long prompt.\n- **Prefill priority versus decode priority.** Engines expose this as scheduler policy; prefill-first\n  minimizes TTFT, decode-first smooths TPOT. The right setting depends on the product.\n- **Disaggregated serving.** Run prefill on one pool of GPUs and decode on another, transferring the KV\n  cache between them over the interconnect. Each pool is then tuned for its phase (prefill wants FLOPs,\n  decode wants bandwidth and memory capacity), and one phase's load can no longer spike the other's\n  latency. The price is the transfer and a more complex system; it pays at scale.\n- **Prefix caching.** Reuse an already-computed KV prefix so part of the prefill is skipped entirely; the\n  biggest TTFT lever when prompts share a long system prompt.\n- **Speculative decoding.** Turns several decode steps into one verification pass, exploiting the slack\n  compute that bandwidth-bound decode leaves idle.\n\n### What the metrics mean\n- TTFT ≈ queue wait + prefill time (+ a cache transfer in disaggregated setups).\n- TPOT ≈ decode step time, which rises with batch size and with the total cache the batch reads.\n- Throughput in tokens/sec is dominated by decode batching; prefill throughput is a separate number and\n  is what a long-document, short-answer workload actually consumes.","example":"A worked scenario on one 80 GB GPU serving an 8B GQA model in fp16 (weights ~16 GB, cache ~128 KB per\ntoken), with rounded, illustrative hardware figures of ~3 TB/s bandwidth and ~900 TFLOPs.\n\nRequest A: 4,000-token prompt, 50-token answer. Request B: 200-token prompt, 1,000-token answer.\n\nPrefill A: 2 × 8e9 × 4,000 = 64 TFLOPs of matmul work; at ~900 TFLOPs that is roughly 70 ms, plus\nattention and overhead, so TTFT on the order of 100 ms when the GPU is otherwise idle. Prefill B is 20× less\nwork: a few milliseconds, so TTFT is dominated by queueing.\n\nDecode A: each step reads ~16 GB of weights plus ~0.5 GB of cache (4,050 tokens × 128 KB); at ~3 TB/s\nthat is ~5.5 ms per step, so the 50 tokens take ~280 ms. Decode B: ~16 GB + ~0.15 GB per step, ~5.4 ms,\ntimes 1,000 tokens ≈ 5.4 s.\n\nTotal GPU time: A ≈ 0.1 + 0.28 ≈ 0.4 s; B ≈ 0.005 + 5.4 ≈ 5.4 s. B has one-twentieth the prompt tokens\nbut occupies the GPU 13× longer, and nearly all of that time the GPU is streaming the same 16 GB of\nweights over and over. Batch 32 B-like requests together and the per-step cost rises only to ~7 ms\n(weights read once, cache reads grow), so aggregate throughput goes up ~25× while each user's TPOT\nrises from 5.4 to 7 ms. Now admit one A-like prompt into that batch without chunked prefill: that\niteration takes ~100 ms instead of 7 ms, and all 32 users see a visible hitch. With chunked prefill at a\n512-token budget, A's prefill is spread over 8 iterations of ~15 ms each: A's TTFT becomes ~120 ms\ninstead of ~100 ms, and nobody else stalls.","tradeoffs":["Chunked prefill smooths TPOT for everyone and raises utilization, but lengthens TTFT for long prompts; flips when the workload is mostly long prompts with short answers and TTFT is the metric sold.","Disaggregating prefill and decode isolates the phases and lets each pool be sized for its bottleneck, but adds KV transfer and operational complexity; it pays only at a scale where interference is measurable.","Prefill-first scheduling minimizes TTFT at the cost of decode stalls; decode-first does the opposite; the right choice depends on whether users wait for the first token or read the stream.","Faster-FLOPs hardware shortens prefill substantially but barely moves decode; buying it makes sense for document-heavy, short-output workloads and not for chat with long answers."],"pitfalls":["Benchmarking \"latency\" as a single number; symptom is a product with fine average latency whose users complain either about the pause before text appears or about stuttering mid-stream.","Assuming cost is proportional to total tokens; symptom is a pricing or capacity model that is wildly wrong for long-output workloads, because output tokens cost far more GPU time than input tokens.","Leaving chunked prefill off on a mixed workload; symptom is periodic TPOT spikes across all users that coincide with the arrival of long prompts, not with request rate.","Expecting a GPU upgrade to speed up chat decode; symptom is TTFT improving while tokens/sec per user is unchanged, because bandwidth did not change much.","Reporting prefill throughput and decode throughput as one \"tokens/sec\"; symptom is capacity estimates that are off by an order of magnitude when the input/output mix shifts."],"when":"This split is the first thing to apply when a latency or cost number looks wrong. Ask which phase the\nmetric belongs to: TTFT problems lead to prefill (prompt length, queueing, prefix caching, chunking);\nTPOT problems lead to decode (batch size, cache size, bandwidth, quantization). Use it when sizing\nhardware: a summarization service of long documents wants FLOPs and prefill throughput, a chat service\nwith long answers wants bandwidth and memory. Use it when pricing or budgeting: count input and output\ntokens separately because they cost different resources. In interviews, explaining why decode is\nbandwidth-bound and prefill is compute-bound, with the arithmetic-intensity reasoning, is the baseline\nexpectation for any serving question.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Enables chunked prefill and sets the per-iteration token budget, reads TTFT and TPOT as separate histograms, and attributes spikes to the phase that caused them.","architect":"Chooses GPU types and whether to disaggregate the phases based on the workload's input/output mix, and sets latency targets per phase rather than one blended number.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their long-document pipeline and their chat assistant need different tuning, and reproduces their stutter by injecting a long prompt into a loaded replica."},"check":{"q":"A document-summarization service has 15k-token prompts and 300-token outputs. A chat assistant has\n300-token prompts and 1,500-token outputs. Both run on identical GPUs. Which hardware upgrade would help\neach, which scheduling settings differ, and how should the two be billed relative to each other?","a":"Summarization is prefill-dominated: 15k tokens of compute-bound matmul per request (for an 8B\nmodel, 2 × 8e9 × 15,000 ≈ 240 TFLOPs, a few hundred milliseconds on one GPU), so TTFT is the pain and more\nFLOPs per GPU would help directly; prefix caching helps only if documents share prefixes, which they\nusually do not. Chat is decode-dominated: 1,500 bandwidth-bound steps per request, so more\nbandwidth and more memory for larger batches help, and FLOPs do not. Scheduling: summarization benefits\nfrom chunked prefill so that its long prompts do not stall other users, and from a higher per-iteration\ntoken budget; chat wants a large `max_num_seqs`, decode-friendly scheduling and possibly speculative\ndecoding. Billing: compare cost per token, not per request. With the illustrative figures from\nthe example, a prefill token costs ~70 ms / 4,000 ≈ 0.02 ms of GPU time, while a decode token costs a share\nof a ~7 ms step, ≈ 0.2 ms even when 32 users share each step: roughly an order of magnitude more. So\ninput and output tokens must be metered and priced separately, with output well above input, which is\nthe shape of public API price lists. A blended per-token price would undercharge the chat product and\novercharge the summarizer."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the decode phase memory-bandwidth-bound while prefill is compute-bound?","options":["Decode uses a different, slower set of kernels than prefill","Decode reads all weights and the cache to produce one token per request, while prefill reuses each weight across every prompt token","Prefill does not need to write to the KV cache","Decode runs on the CPU in most engines"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Arithmetic intensity is FLOPs per byte moved. Prefill performs thousands of operations per weight byte; decode performs one or a few, so its time is set by how fast memory can be streamed. Same kernels, same device, different shape of work."},{"q":"What is the primary purpose of chunked prefill?","options":["To reduce the total compute needed for long prompts","To allow prompts longer than the context window","To interleave pieces of a long prompt with decode steps so one prefill does not stall every running request","To compress the KV cache written during prefill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chunking does not reduce work; it spreads it across iterations so decode batches keep flowing. The prefill's own TTFT gets slightly longer, in exchange for smooth TPOT for everyone else."},{"q":"A team upgrades to a GPU with twice the FLOPs and similar memory bandwidth. Which metric improves most?","options":["Time per output token for a single chat request","Time to first token on long prompts","Maximum number of concurrent requests","KV-cache memory per token"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill is compute-bound, so doubling FLOPs roughly halves its time and TTFT drops. Decode is bandwidth-bound and gains little; concurrency is set by memory capacity; cache size per token is a model property."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-memory-estimation","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Weights are the visible cost of a model; the cache is the invisible one. A team that budgets 16 GB for an\n8B fp16 model and assumes the remaining 64 GB is \"headroom\" has not budgeted at all, because every\nrunning request pins a cache that grows with its context, and the engine refuses or preempts requests\nonce that pool is exhausted. The failure is silent until load arrives: nothing crashes at low traffic, and\nthen under real concurrency the server either OOMs, stalls admissions, or evicts half-finished generations\nand recomputes them, which shows up as latency collapse.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"try it and see\", fails because the right answer depends on things you do not\nobserve until production: the distribution of prompt lengths, output lengths and concurrency, and how they\nmove together. Load tests with synthetic 500-token prompts say nothing about the day a customer pastes a\n40-page contract. The other naive alternative, \"pick the biggest GPU\", fails on cost and often still does\nnot fit: a 70B model's cache at 128k context on a modest batch exceeds any single card. The estimate exists\nso you can reason forward from a config file and a traffic model to a GPU count before buying anything,\nand backward from an OOM to the parameter that caused it.","how":"### Bytes per token\nEach transformer layer stores one key vector and one value vector per KV head per token. So:\n\n```\nbytes_per_token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element\n```\n\nThe factor 2 is K and V. `n_kv_heads` is the number of key/value heads, not attention heads: with\nmulti-head attention they are equal, with grouped-query attention (GQA) KV heads are fewer (8 is common),\nand with multi-query attention it is 1. `head_dim` is usually `hidden_size / n_attention_heads`.\n`bytes_per_element` is 2 for fp16/bf16, 1 for fp8 or int8 cache. All of these come from the model's\nconfig file (`num_hidden_layers`, `num_key_value_heads`, `hidden_size`, `num_attention_heads`).\n\nMulti-head latent attention (DeepSeek-V2/V3 and derivatives) replaces per-head K and V with one compressed\nlatent vector per layer per token plus a small decoupled RoPE key, so the formula becomes\n1 × layers × (latent_dim + rope_dim) × bytes (for DeepSeek-V3: 61 × (512 + 64) × 2 ≈ 70 KB in bf16, versus\nseveral MB for an equivalent MHA layout); read the architecture before applying the standard formula.\n\nSliding-window attention is the other common exception. A layer that only attends to the last *W*\npositions needs at most *W* tokens of cache no matter how long the sequence gets, and several recent\nmodel families interleave sliding-window layers with full-attention layers. Then the estimate is a sum:\nfull-attention layers scale with sequence length, windowed layers are capped at *W*, and the average\nbytes per token falls as sequences grow past the window. Whether an engine actually frees the\nout-of-window blocks depends on the engine and version, so confirm with its startup log.\n\n### From per-token to per-GPU\n```\ncache_per_request = bytes_per_token × (prompt_tokens + max_output_tokens)\npool_bytes        = gpu_memory × utilization_fraction − weight_bytes − activation_workspace\nmax_concurrency   ≈ pool_bytes / cache_per_request\n```\n\nFour refinements matter in practice:\n\n1. **Weights.** Parameters × bytes per parameter: 8B × 2 = 16 GB in fp16, 8 GB at int8, ~4.5 GB at 4-bit\n   (plus a little for scales). Quantizing weights frees cache space; that is often its biggest payoff.\n2. **Activation and workspace.** Engines profile a worst-case forward pass at startup (the largest\n   prefill batch they will allow) and reserve that memory before sizing the pool. CUDA graphs, sampling\n   buffers and the framework's own allocator overhead also take a few GB. vLLM exposes the knob as\n   `gpu_memory_utilization` (default 0.9) and prints the number of cache blocks it managed to allocate;\n   that printed number, times block size, is the real pool in tokens.\n3. **Sequence length is a distribution.** Use the p95 or p99 of (prompt + output), not the mean, to decide\n   what fits without preemption, and use the mean to estimate steady-state concurrency. Paged allocation\n   means a request only consumes what it has actually generated so far, so admission can be optimistic\n   about the mean while the max context length bounds the worst case.\n4. **Tensor parallelism splits the cache, up to a point.** With tensor parallelism of degree *T*, each GPU\n   holds the K/V of `n_kv_heads / T` heads, so per-GPU cache per token is divided by *T* along with the\n   weights, and the pool is the sum over GPUs. Once *T* exceeds `n_kv_heads` (say *T* = 16 on a model\n   with 8 KV heads) heads must be replicated, and the per-GPU cache stops shrinking. *T* must also divide\n   the head counts, which is why real deployments use TP degrees of 2, 4 or 8, not 3 or 6.\n\n### Running the numbers for common shapes\nIllustrative, computed from public config values:\n- 32 layers, 32 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16 (a Llama-2-7B-class MHA model): 2 × 32 × 32 × 128 × 2 =\n  524,288 B ≈ 512 KB per token. 8k context ≈ 4 GB per request.\n- 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16 (a Llama-3-8B-class GQA model): 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 =\n  131,072 B = 128 KB per token. 8k context ≈ 1 GB per request. Same size class, 4× less cache: this is why\n  GQA exists.\n- 80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16 (a 70B-class GQA model): 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 B =\n  320 KB per token. 8k context ≈ 2.5 GB per request; 128k context ≈ 40 GB for one request.\n\n### Reading it backwards\nIf the engine reports N cache blocks of 16 tokens, the pool holds 16N tokens. Divide by your p50 sequence\nlength for expected concurrency, by your `max_model_len` for guaranteed concurrency. If the gap between\nthose two is large, the scheduler will run many requests at once and preempt when the tail arrives; that\nis a policy choice, not an error, but you should make it consciously.","example":"A helper you can run against any Hugging Face config to answer \"how many fit\" before touching a GPU.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\ndef kv_bytes_per_token(cfg, cache_bytes=2):\n    n_heads = cfg[\"num_attention_heads\"]\n    n_kv = cfg.get(\"num_key_value_heads\", n_heads)        # GQA/MQA; defaults to MHA\n    head_dim = cfg.get(\"head_dim\") or cfg[\"hidden_size\"] // n_heads\n    return 2 * cfg[\"num_hidden_layers\"] * n_kv * head_dim * cache_bytes\n\ndef capacity(cfg, params_b, gpu_gb=80, util=0.90, weight_bytes=2, workspace_gb=4,\n             p50_seq=3000, max_seq=8192, cache_bytes=2):\n    per_tok = kv_bytes_per_token(cfg, cache_bytes)\n    weights = params_b * 1e9 * weight_bytes\n    pool = gpu_gb * 1e9 * util - weights - workspace_gb * 1e9\n    return {\n        \"kv_kb_per_token\": per_tok / 1024,\n        \"pool_gb\": pool / 1e9,\n        \"pool_tokens\": int(pool // per_tok),\n        \"concurrency_at_p50\": int(pool // (per_tok * p50_seq)),\n        \"concurrency_at_max\": int(pool // (per_tok * max_seq)),\n    }\n\nllama3_8b_like = {\"num_hidden_layers\": 32, \"num_attention_heads\": 32,\n                  \"num_key_value_heads\": 8, \"hidden_size\": 4096}\nprint(json.dumps(capacity(llama3_8b_like, params_b=8), indent=1))\n# -> ~128 KB/token, pool ≈ 52 GB ≈ 400k tokens, ~132 requests at a 3k p50, ~48 at an 8k max.\nprint(capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, cache_bytes=1)[\"concurrency_at_p50\"])   # fp8 cache: ~2x\nprint(capacity(llama3_8b_like, 8, weight_bytes=0.5)[\"pool_gb\"])           # 4-bit weights: +12 GB pool\n```\n\nThe last two lines are the two cheapest levers when the answer is \"not enough\": quantize the cache to\ndouble token capacity, or quantize the weights to enlarge the pool. Compare the printed `pool_tokens`\nwith the block count the engine logs at startup; if they disagree by more than ~10%, the workspace\nestimate is wrong for that engine and model and you should trust the log.","tradeoffs":["Sizing to the maximum context guarantees no preemption but leaves most of the pool idle on typical traffic; sizing to the median packs more users but accepts preemption on the tail.","Quantizing the cache to 8-bit doubles token capacity at a small, task-dependent quality cost; the trade flips against it for workloads where exact long-range recall is the product.","Choosing a GQA model shrinks the cache several-fold and raises concurrency, but constrains model choice; a stronger MHA model may still win when quality per request matters more than requests per GPU.","Raising `gpu_memory_utilization` toward 1.0 enlarges the pool but removes headroom for activation spikes and other processes; it flips to dangerous when the GPU is shared or prompts are bursty."],"pitfalls":["Using the attention-head count instead of the KV-head count; symptom is a capacity estimate four or eight times too pessimistic for GQA models, and a purchase of GPUs you did not need.","Forgetting that output tokens also need cache; symptom is OOM or preemption on long generations even though every prompt fit comfortably in the estimate.","Trusting the formula over the engine's startup log; symptom is a pool 10–20% smaller than planned because activation workspace and allocator overhead were not budgeted.","Estimating with mean sequence length and promising that concurrency; symptom is latency collapse the first time a batch of long-context requests arrives together.","Applying the standard formula to a model with latent or sliding-window attention; symptom is an estimate off by a large factor in either direction, because the cache layout is different."],"when":"Do this arithmetic at four moments: when choosing a model and GPU before any code exists; when setting\n`max_model_len`, `max_num_seqs` and utilization flags for a deployment; when an OOM or preemption storm\nappears in production; and when someone asks for a longer context window or a bigger model on the same\nhardware. Keep the per-token number for every model you serve in your head or in a table, because it\nanswers most capacity questions in seconds. Measure actual cache utilization and preemption counts in\nproduction to validate the estimate against real traffic. In interviews, deriving bytes per token from a\nconfig, then turning it into a concurrency number with the weights subtracted, is the canonical serving\nquestion, and the follow-up is always \"what would you change if it does not fit\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes the pool before deployment, sets context and concurrency flags from it, and checks the engine's logged block count against the estimate on every model change.","architect":"Owns the capacity model that links context-window policy, model choice (GQA or not), GPU tier and replica count, and reviews any request to raise the context limit against it.","fde":"Turns a customer's \"we need 200 concurrent users with 50-page documents\" into a per-token number, a GPU count and a cost, and shows the trade when it does not fit their budget."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants a 70B-class GQA model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128) in fp16 to serve 40\nconcurrent sessions that average 12k tokens of context, on 80 GB GPUs. How many GPUs, and what would you\npropose if they only have two?","a":"Cache per token is 2 × 80 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 327,680 B ≈ 320 KB. Per session at 12k tokens: ~3.9 GB.\nForty sessions: ~157 GB of cache. Weights in fp16 are ~140 GB, which already needs at least two 80 GB GPUs\nwith tensor parallelism, and at 90% utilization those two offer ~144 GB, so after weights and workspace\nthere is almost nothing left for cache: the model loads but serves one or two sessions. Four GPUs (TP = 4)\ngive ~288 GB usable, roughly 130–140 GB after weights and per-GPU workspace, about 34 sessions at the\naverage and no tail headroom, so slightly short. Six GPUs is not a valid TP degree for 8 KV heads, so the\nnext real step is eight: either one TP = 8 replica with ~400 GB of cache, or two TP = 4 replicas of ~34\nsessions each, which also buys redundancy. With only two GPUs: quantize the weights to 8-bit (~70 GB) and\nthe cache to 8-bit (halving per-session cost to ~2 GB); with ~65 GB left after weights and workspace that\nfits roughly 30–33 sessions at the average. Then cap context per session at 16k to bound the tail, enable\npaging and prefix caching, and run a quality eval on the quantized configuration before promising\nanything. A strong answer notes that decode at 40 sessions\nwill have higher TPOT because every step reads all 40 caches."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which model-config fields do you need to compute KV-cache bytes per token?","options":["Vocabulary size, hidden size and number of layers","Number of layers, number of KV heads, head dimension and the cache element size","Number of parameters and the context length","Number of attention heads, MLP width and dropout rate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache stores one K and one V vector per layer per KV head per token, so those dimensions plus the bytes per element are exactly what is needed. Vocabulary, parameter count and MLP width do not appear in the cache at all."},{"q":"Two 8B models differ only in that one has 32 KV heads and the other 8. What is the effect on serving capacity?","options":["No difference; cache size depends on parameter count","The 8-KV-head model needs 4× more cache because each head is larger","The 8-KV-head model needs about one quarter of the cache per token, so roughly 4× more sequences fit","The 32-KV-head model is faster to decode because attention is more parallel"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cache bytes scale linearly with the number of KV heads. Grouped-query attention shares each K/V head across several query heads precisely to cut this number, which is why GQA models serve far more concurrent requests per GPU."},{"q":"Your estimate says 60 requests fit, but the engine's startup log allocates blocks for only 45. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The engine is using a larger block size than you assumed","Activation workspace, CUDA graphs and allocator overhead reduce the pool below \"VRAM minus weights\"","The engine stores keys and values in separate pools","Your formula double-counted the factor of 2 for K and V"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Engines profile a worst-case forward pass and reserve memory for activations, graphs and sampling before sizing the cache pool. Block size does not change total pool bytes, and the K/V factor is correct. Trust the log and tighten the workspace term in the estimate."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/kv-cache-paging-and-eviction","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zheng et al., 2023 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Xiao et al., 2023 — Efficient Streaming Language Models with Attention Sinks"],"why":"A request's cache grows by one token per decode step and the final length is unknown until the model\nemits a stop token. The straightforward allocator reserves a contiguous region sized to the maximum\ncontext length for every admitted request. That fails in two ways. First, internal waste: a request\nallowed 8k tokens that stops at 600 has pinned 8k tokens' worth of memory for its whole life, so the pool\nis mostly reserved-but-empty and concurrency is a fraction of what the hardware could hold. Second,\nexternal fragmentation: requests of different lengths finish at different times, leaving holes that are\neach too small for the next arrival even though the free total is large.\n\nThe next idea, allocate contiguously but only what is used so far and grow in place, fails because the\nneighbour's region is right behind you; growing means copying the whole cache to a bigger hole, which is\nexpensive and still fragments. Attention kernels were written to read K and V as one contiguous tensor,\nwhich is why everyone accepted the waste for years.\n\nPaging breaks the contiguity assumption. If the kernel can follow a table from logical token positions to\nphysical blocks, memory can be handed out in small fixed units on demand, waste is bounded by one block\nper sequence, and any free block fits any request. The same indirection then gives you something the\ncontiguous design never could: two sequences can point at the same physical blocks, which is the basis of\nprefix sharing. Eviction exists because paging makes the pool usable but not infinite; a policy is still\nneeded when demand exceeds supply, and the choice of policy is what decides whether overload shows up as\ngraceful queueing or as latency collapse.","how":"### Blocks and block tables\nThe engine carves the cache pool into blocks of a fixed number of tokens (vLLM's default is 16). Each block\nholds K and V for every layer and KV head for those token positions. Each running sequence owns a block\ntable: logical block *i* of the sequence maps to some physical block number. When the sequence's current\nlast block is full, the allocator pops a free block from a free list and appends it to the table. The\nattention kernel receives the table and, for each query, gathers keys and values block by block. This is\nPagedAttention; the dedicated lesson covers the kernel in detail.\n\nConsequences that follow directly:\n- **Waste is bounded.** Only the last block of each sequence can be partially filled, so internal waste is\n  under one block per sequence rather than up to a full context length.\n- **Any free block serves any request.** External fragmentation disappears because allocation is in\n  uniform units.\n- **Admission is optimistic.** The scheduler admits a request based on the blocks its prompt needs plus a\n  small reserve, not its maximum output. Far more requests run at once, with the risk that they\n  collectively outgrow the pool.\n\n### Sharing and copy-on-write\nBecause mapping is indirect, several sequences can map logical blocks to the same physical block with a\nreference count. Parallel sampling (n candidates from one prompt) and beam search share the prompt's blocks;\nwhen a sequence needs to write into a shared block, the engine copies it first. Prefix caching generalizes\nthis across requests by keeping blocks alive after their owner finishes, keyed by a hash of their content.\n\n### Running out: sequence-level preemption\nWhen the free list is empty and a running sequence needs a new block, something must give. Engines preempt\nat the granularity of whole sequences because a sequence needs all of its blocks to take even one step;\nfreeing half of one helps nobody. Two mechanisms:\n- **Recomputation.** Free the victim's blocks entirely, put it back in the waiting queue, and when it is\n  readmitted re-run prefill over its prompt plus the tokens it had generated so far. It needs no host\n  memory or transfer machinery, and the re-prefill is a compute-bound pass that batches well, which is\n  why it is vLLM's default.\n- **Swapping.** Copy the victim's blocks to pinned CPU memory and copy them back on resumption. Saves\n  compute but is limited by PCIe bandwidth and host memory, and copying many small blocks is less\n  efficient than one large transfer.\n\nWhich is cheaper is arithmetic, not doctrine. Recompute costs roughly `2 × params` FLOPs per token plus\nan attention term that grows with the square of the sequence length; swap costs `bytes_per_token` over\nPCIe, twice. Swap therefore gains as sequences get long (the quadratic attention term) and as the cache\nper token gets small relative to the model (GQA, fp8 cache); recompute gains when the cache is fat (MHA)\nor the host link is slow or shared. Measure resume latency for both on your hardware before choosing.\n\nVictim selection is typically the most recently admitted sequences first (so that older requests, which\nhave already consumed the most resources, finish), and preemption is all-or-nothing per sequence. The\nuser-visible symptom is a pause in the token stream and a jump in TTFT-like latency for the preempted\nrequest.\n\n### Running out: evicting cached prefixes\nBlocks kept alive for prefix reuse have a reference count of zero and are reclaimable. The engine evicts\nthem before preempting anything, usually in LRU order, so a prefix cache degrades gracefully under memory\npressure: hit rate falls, nobody is preempted. Some deployments add a tier below VRAM, demoting evicted\nprefix blocks to host memory or local disk instead of discarding them, which turns a miss into a\ntransfer rather than a full re-prefill; it pays when prefixes are long and reused across minutes. SGLang organizes cached prefixes as a radix tree and evicts\nleaves least-recently-used, which keeps shared ancestors (the system prompt) resident longest.\n\n### Eviction inside a sequence\nA different question is whether a single sequence must keep every token's K and V. Sliding-window\nattention models only attend to the last *W* positions, so older blocks can be released as soon as the\nwindow moves past them; this is exact for that architecture. For full-attention models, research methods\ndrop tokens judged unimportant (low accumulated attention, or everything except the first few \"sink\"\ntokens plus a recent window) to bound memory for very long streams. These are approximations: they\nchange the model's output, sometimes imperceptibly and sometimes badly on recall-heavy tasks, and must be\nevaluated like any lossy optimization. Production engines treat them as opt-in or do not ship them at all.\n\n### What to watch\nEngines expose the number of free and used blocks, the preemption count and the prefix-cache hit rate.\nPreemptions above zero under normal load mean the admission policy is too optimistic for the sequence\nlength distribution; falling prefix hit rate with rising cache usage means the pool is too small for\nboth duties.","example":"A toy block allocator with a block table per sequence and LRU reclaim of unreferenced cached blocks. It is\nnot an attention kernel; it shows the bookkeeping that makes paging and eviction work.\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nBLOCK = 16                      # tokens per block\n\nclass Pool:\n    def __init__(self, n_blocks):\n        self.free = list(range(n_blocks))\n        self.refs = {}                 # physical block -> reference count\n        self.cached = OrderedDict()    # content_hash -> block, LRU order (ref 0)\n\n    def alloc(self):\n        if not self.free and self.cached:            # evict an unreferenced cached block\n            _, blk = self.cached.popitem(last=False)\n            self.free.append(blk)\n        if not self.free:\n            raise MemoryError(\"pool exhausted: preempt a sequence\")\n        blk = self.free.pop(); self.refs[blk] = 1\n        return blk\n\n    def release(self, blk, content_hash=None):\n        self.refs[blk] -= 1\n        if self.refs[blk] == 0:\n            if content_hash: self.cached[content_hash] = blk   # keep for prefix reuse\n            else: self.free.append(blk)\n\nclass Seq:\n    def __init__(self, pool): self.pool, self.table, self.n = pool, [], 0\n    def append_token(self):\n        if self.n % BLOCK == 0: self.table.append(self.pool.alloc())\n        self.n += 1\n\npool = Pool(n_blocks=4)\na, b = Seq(pool), Seq(pool)\nfor _ in range(40): a.append_token()           # a uses 3 blocks (last one 8/16 full)\nfor _ in range(16): b.append_token()           # b uses the 4th block; pool is now full\ntry:\n    b.append_token()                           # needs a 5th block -> no free, no cached\nexcept MemoryError as e:\n    print(e)                                   # scheduler would now preempt b (recompute or swap)\n```\n\nWith 4 blocks of 16 tokens the pool holds 64 tokens, and internal waste is the 8 empty slots in `a`'s\nlast block. If `a` had finished and its blocks had been released with a content hash, `b`'s next\nallocation would have evicted the least-recently-used of them instead of raising.\n\nScale the same bookkeeping to a real deployment: `max_model_len` 8,192, an 8B GQA model at 128 KB per\ntoken, and a request that stops after 600 tokens. A contiguous allocator reserves 8,192 slots (1 GB) for\nit; the request uses 600 (75 MB), so over 90% of the reservation is dead for the request's whole life.\nPaged, it holds 38 blocks = 608 slots, wasting 8. On a ~50 GB pool that is the difference between about\n50 resident requests and, if typical requests look like this one, several hundred, which is why paging\nis what made large continuous batches possible at all.","tradeoffs":["Smaller blocks cut internal waste and make prefix matching finer-grained, but enlarge block tables and reduce kernel efficiency; the balance flips toward larger blocks on long contexts with few sequences.","Optimistic admission packs many more sequences per GPU, but makes preemption possible; it flips to harmful when sequence lengths are long-tailed and preemption storms recompute more than they save.","Recomputation-based preemption is simple and batches well, but wastes prefill compute; swapping saves compute at the cost of PCIe traffic and host memory, and wins as sequences grow long, because re-prefill cost grows faster than linearly with length while transfer cost grows linearly.","Keeping finished prefixes resident raises hit rate but competes with live sequences for blocks; under sustained load the LRU eviction turns prefix caching into a weak benefit rather than a guarantee.","Token-level eviction inside a sequence bounds memory for unbounded streams but changes outputs; it is acceptable for chatty, low-recall workloads and not for retrieval or long-document QA."],"pitfalls":["Reading \"memory utilization 95%\" as a healthy sign while the preemption counter climbs; symptom is periodic multi-second stalls on individual streams with no error logged.","Setting `max_model_len` far above real prompt lengths and assuming it is free; symptom is a preemption storm the first time a handful of requests actually grow into that headroom, because optimistic admission let them in on their prompt size and the pool was sized for the typical case.","Enabling swap-based preemption on hosts with little pinned memory or slow PCIe; symptom is preempted requests taking longer to resume than recomputation would have.","Changing block size on a tuned deployment without re-measuring; symptom is a surprise drop in prefix hit rate or kernel throughput that nobody attributes to the flag.","Turning on an approximate token-eviction method for long conversations; symptom is the model forgetting instructions from early in the chat while memory charts look great."],"when":"Paging is not optional in a modern engine; what you decide is the policy around it. Use the preemption\ncounter as your primary overload signal and tune admission (`max_num_seqs`, per-iteration token budget,\n`max_model_len`) until it is near zero at target load. Choose recomputation unless your sequences are so\nlong that re-prefill is the dominant cost. Keep prefix caching on when prompts share prefixes and watch its\nhit rate under load; if it collapses, the pool is too small for both duties and you need memory, not\nflags. Treat in-sequence token eviction as a research option to evaluate, not a default. In interviews,\nexplaining why preemption is per-sequence, how blocks bound waste, and what LRU prefix eviction buys is the\nexpected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes block size, admission limits and the preemption mode, and alerts on preemption count and free-block count rather than on raw memory utilization.","architect":"Decides whether the pool is shared between live sequences and cached prefixes or split by replica class, and sets the context-length policy that bounds worst-case reservations.","fde":"Reads a customer's engine metrics to explain mid-stream pauses as preemption, then proposes the admission or hardware change that removes them without breaking their latency promise."},"check":{"q":"A deployment runs at 92% KV-cache utilization with 3–5 preemptions per minute and a prefix-cache hit\nrate that has fallen from 60% to 15% since traffic grew. Explain what is happening inside the pool and\nwhat you would change, in order.","a":"The pool is oversubscribed. Live sequences have grown until there are almost no free blocks, so the\nengine first evicted unreferenced cached prefixes (hit rate collapsed) and, once those were gone, began\npreempting the most recently admitted sequences, which then recompute their prefill and consume the\ncompute that was supposed to go to decode. First, make admission less optimistic: lower `max_num_seqs` or\nthe per-iteration token budget so that live sequences fit without eviction; queueing at admission is\ncheaper than preemption mid-stream. Second, reduce per-sequence cost: cap `max_model_len` to the real\np99, and quantize the cache to fp8 if an eval allows. Third, if hit rate matters to TTFT, add memory\n(another replica or a larger GPU) so that cached prefixes survive; prefix caching is only a benefit when\nthere is slack. Measure preemptions per minute after each change and stop when it is near zero at peak."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does paging bound internal fragmentation to less than one block per sequence?","options":["Because blocks are allocated in sorted order of sequence length","Because a sequence only ever has one partially filled block, its most recent one; all earlier blocks are full","Because the engine compacts the cache after every decode step","Because block size is chosen to divide the maximum context length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tokens are appended sequentially, so every block except the last is completely used. Contiguous reservation, by contrast, wastes the whole unused remainder of the maximum context for each request."},{"q":"When the pool is exhausted, why do engines preempt entire sequences rather than freeing some blocks from several?","options":["Because block tables cannot be modified while a sequence is running","Because partial frees would corrupt the attention mask","Because a sequence needs all of its blocks to take even one decode step, so partially freeing it makes it useless","Because recomputation is only possible from the beginning of a sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Attention for the next token reads every cached position. Removing some blocks from a live sequence means it cannot proceed at all, so the only useful units of eviction are whole sequences or blocks nobody references."},{"q":"Which eviction is lossless for a full-attention model?","options":["Dropping tokens with the lowest accumulated attention scores","Keeping only the first few tokens and a recent window","Reclaiming cached prefix blocks that no running sequence references","Discarding every other block once context exceeds a threshold"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unreferenced prefix blocks are pure optimization state; losing them costs a recompute on the next matching request but changes no output. Token-dropping methods alter what the model attends to and are approximations that must be evaluated."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/prefix-caching","sources":["Zheng et al., 2023 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Real prompts are mostly repetition. A product's system prompt is the same thousands of tokens on every\ncall; a few-shot block is the same examples every time; turn *n* of a conversation begins with all of turns\n1 to *n−1*; an agent loop re-sends the whole trajectory on every tool step. Prefill is compute-bound and\nlinear in prompt length, so a 3,000-token boilerplate prefix costs 3,000 tokens of matmul on every single\nrequest, and that work produces exactly the same K and V vectors every time. Paying for it repeatedly is\npure waste.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"shorten the prompt\", helps but has a floor: the instructions, schema and examples\nare there because quality needs them. The other naive alternative, \"cache the response\", only works for\nidentical full prompts, which almost never recur beyond FAQs. What recurs is the *beginning*. Causal\nattention makes that exploitable: because position *i* attends only to positions ≤ *i*, the cache for a\nprefix is a pure function of the prefix tokens and the weights. Nothing after it can change it. So the\ncache computed for one request's first 3,000 tokens is bit-for-bit what the next request with the same\nfirst 3,000 tokens would compute. The only engineering needed is to keep those blocks around, find them\nagain quickly, and reclaim them when memory is scarce. Paged KV memory made this cheap: blocks are\nalready the unit of allocation, so sharing them between requests is a reference count, not a copy.","how":"### Content-addressed blocks\nWith paging, a prefix is a sequence of full blocks. The engine hashes each block's content, typically the\nhash of the previous block combined with the token ids in this block, so a block's key encodes everything\nbefore it, not just its own 16 tokens. That matters: the same 16 tokens at a different position, or after\na different prefix, have different K and V (positions and preceding context both feed into them), so the\nhash must be of the whole prefix up to and including the block.\n\n### Lookup on arrival\nWhen a request is admitted, the scheduler walks its prompt block by block, computing hashes and looking\neach up in the cache table. The longest chain of hits from the start is the cached prefix; those physical\nblocks are attached to the new request's block table with their reference counts incremented, and prefill\nruns only over the remaining tokens (and, in some engines, a partial last block is always recomputed\nbecause only full blocks are cached). TTFT drops in proportion to the fraction of the prompt that hit.\n\n### Two payoffs: compute and memory\nThe obvious saving is prefill compute. For an 8B model, a 3,000-token prefix is about\n2 × 8e9 × 3,000 ≈ 48 TFLOPs, on the order of 50 ms on one GPU at illustrative peak rates, against a\nmillisecond or two for a 50-token question; with a hit, TTFT is dominated by the question and the queue.\nThe less obvious saving is capacity. Requests that share a prefix point their block tables at the same\nphysical blocks, so 100 concurrent requests with that 3,000-token prefix (at 128 KB per token, ~390 MB\neach) hold one copy instead of 100: roughly 39 GB of pool returned to live sequences. On workloads with\nlong shared prefixes, that is often worth more than the TTFT gain, because it raises the batch size.\n\n### Lifetime and eviction\nWhen a request finishes, its blocks' reference counts drop. Blocks with zero references are not returned\nto the free list immediately; they stay in the cache table, reclaimable in LRU order when live sequences\nneed memory. Under light load, a system prompt stays resident indefinitely; under heavy load, it is\nevicted and recomputed, and the hit-rate metric tells you which regime you are in. SGLang represents\ncached prefixes as a radix tree so that requests sharing a long ancestor and diverging late reuse the\nancestor and evict the leaves first; vLLM's hash table reaches the same behaviour with per-block LRU.\n\n### What counts as \"the same prefix\"\nToken identity, not semantic similarity. A single different token invalidates every block from that point\non. The classic mistakes are placing a timestamp, request id, user name or randomly ordered retrieval\nresults near the top of the prompt. The rule for prompt layout is: stable content first (system prompt,\ntool definitions, few-shot examples), then slowly changing content (conversation history, document), then\nthe volatile part (the current question). Chat templates must also be stable: a template that emits the\ndate, or that formats the system prompt differently depending on the presence of tools, silently breaks\nsharing. The key must also cover everything else that changes K and V for the same tokens: a different\nLoRA adapter, or different image inputs in a multimodal prompt, produce different caches, so engines mix\nthose identities into the block hash, and two tenants on different adapters never share.\n\n### Multi-replica serving\nA cache lives on one GPU. With several replicas behind a load balancer, the second request for a tenant\nlands on a replica that has never seen its prefix unless routing is cache-aware. Options are sticky\nrouting by tenant or conversation, consistent hashing on a prefix hash computed at the router, or a\nrouter that queries replicas' cache state. Without one of these, measured hit rate on a fleet is far\nbelow what a single replica shows, and the problem is routing, not the engine.\n\n### Managed APIs\nProviders expose the same mechanism with different control surfaces: some cache automatically for prompts\nabove a minimum length, others require explicit cache breakpoints in the request, and most price cached\ninput tokens at a fraction of normal input price while charging a premium to write a cache entry. Cache\nlifetime is short (minutes) unless refreshed, and the rules differ per provider and change over time, so\ntreat the exact numbers as something to look up, not memorize. The engineering discipline is identical:\nstable prefix first, measure hit rate, do not let volatile content leak upward.\n\n### Sampling independence and correctness\nThe cache stores K and V, not outputs, so it is independent of temperature, seed and sampling settings;\nreusing it never changes the distribution the model samples from. The one subtlety is numerical: with\nbatched kernels, a cached prefix computed in one batch shape may differ in the last bits from a fresh\nprefill in another, which is why cached and uncached runs are not always bit-identical even at\ntemperature 0.","example":"Enable prefix caching in vLLM's offline engine and measure the effect of prompt layout. The two prompt\nstyles contain identical content; only the position of the volatile part changes.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n\nllm = LLM(\"meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct\", enable_prefix_caching=True,\n          max_model_len=8192, gpu_memory_utilization=0.9)\nsp = SamplingParams(temperature=0, max_tokens=32)\n\nsystem = \"You are a contracts assistant.\\n\" + (\"Policy clause.\\n\" * 400)   # ~1.2k+ tokens, stable\nquestions = [f\"Question {i}: does clause {i} allow early termination?\" for i in range(20)]\n\ndef run(prompts):\n    llm.generate(prompts[:1], sp)                      # warm: first call pays full prefill\n    t = time.perf_counter(); llm.generate(prompts[1:], sp)\n    return (time.perf_counter() - t) / (len(prompts) - 1)\n\ngood = [f\"{system}\\n{q}\" for q in questions]                               # stable prefix first\nbad  = [f\"Request-id: {i}\\n{system}\\n{q}\" for i, q in enumerate(questions)]  # volatile token first\n\nprint(f\"stable-first : {run(good)*1000:.0f} ms/request\")\nprint(f\"volatile-first: {run(bad)*1000:.0f} ms/request\")\n# Expect the stable-first layout to be markedly faster per request: the >1k-token\n# policy block hits the cache on every call. The volatile-first layout misses on\n# every block because the first few tokens differ, so every request re-prefills the whole block.\n# llm.llm_engine metrics (or the server's /metrics endpoint) expose the prefix hit rate.\n```","tradeoffs":["Skips prefill for shared prefixes, cutting TTFT and GPU time, but holds cached blocks in VRAM that live sequences could use; the gain reverses when the pool is tight and cached blocks keep getting evicted.","Forces a prompt layout discipline (stable first, volatile last) that improves hit rate, but constrains prompt design; it flips to irrelevant when prompts share nothing beyond a short system line.","Cache-aware routing raises fleet-wide hit rate, but concentrates a tenant's traffic on one replica, reducing load balancing; worth it when prefixes are long and tenants are many.","On managed APIs, cache writes cost more than plain input and reads cost less; it pays once a prefix is reused a few times within the cache lifetime, and loses money on one-off prompts."],"pitfalls":["A timestamp, request id or user name at the top of the prompt; symptom is a prefix hit rate near zero despite a long identical system prompt.","Retrieval results inserted before the question in a non-deterministic order; symptom is hits only on the system prompt and never on the document block, with TTFT still scaling with retrieved length.","Round-robin load balancing across replicas; symptom is hit rate on a single replica far higher than the fleet's, and TTFT that depends on which replica the request landed on.","Measuring hit rate only on a benchmark that replays the same prompt; symptom is a dashboard showing 95% hits and production showing 20%, because real conversations diverge after the system prompt.","Assuming the cache stores responses; symptom is confusion when the same prompt yields different text at non-zero temperature, which is expected because only K and V are reused.","Sharing one prefix cache across mutually untrusted tenants; the symptom is invisible in metrics, but a hit is measurably faster than a miss, so a tenant can probe whether someone else recently sent a given prefix (a timing side channel). Where that matters, isolate tenants by replica or mix a per-tenant salt into the block hash, which some engines support, at the cost of cross-tenant hits."],"when":"Turn it on whenever prompts share a long common beginning: product system prompts, few-shot blocks, tool\nschemas, multi-turn chat, and agent loops that re-send the trajectory each step; those are the majority\nof production workloads. Measure prefix hit rate and TTFT before and after, and check the preemption\ncounter to confirm cached blocks are not starving live sequences. Skip it, or expect little, for\nsingle-shot workloads with unique prompts such as bulk document summarization. On managed APIs, restructure\nprompts so the stable part is first and add explicit cache breakpoints where the provider requires them.\nIn interviews, the depth expected is to explain why causal attention makes prefix reuse exact, why a\nsingle changed token invalidates everything after it, and how routing affects hit rate on a fleet.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Restructures prompts so stable content comes first, enables the flag or cache breakpoints, and tracks hit rate and TTFT as a pair in the serving dashboard.","architect":"Decides on cache-aware routing versus simple load balancing and how much of the pool cached prefixes may hold, trading hit rate against live concurrency.","fde":"Audits a customer's prompt templates for volatile tokens near the top and shows the TTFT and cost difference with a before-and-after measurement on their traffic."},"check":{"q":"A customer's agent loop sends a 4k-token system prompt plus the growing trajectory on every tool\nstep, and complains that each step's latency grows and that prefix caching \"does not work\" on their\nfour-replica deployment. What would you check, and what would you change?","a":"First confirm the prefix really is identical across steps: look for a timestamp, step counter or\nfreshly serialized tool list near the top, and check that the chat template emits the system prompt\nidentically whether or not tools are present. Second, check routing: four replicas behind round-robin\nmeans each step lands on a replica that likely has not seen the prefix, so the fleet hit rate is poor\neven if the engine's is fine; route by conversation id or prefix hash so a trajectory sticks to one\nreplica. Third, verify the trajectory is append-only: if the agent rewrites or summarizes earlier steps,\nevery block after the edit is invalidated, which is correct behaviour and must be weighed against context\nsavings. Finally check the preemption and eviction metrics; if the pool is saturated the cached blocks are\nbeing evicted between steps, and the fix is memory or admission limits, not the cache flag. With those in\nplace, each step should prefill only the newest tool result, and step latency should stop growing with\ntrajectory length except for the decode-side cache-read term."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the KV cache of a prompt prefix be reused exactly by a different request?","options":["Because the engine stores the model's output for that prefix","Because with causal attention, K and V at each position depend only on the tokens before it, so an identical prefix yields identical cache","Because the attention weights are averaged across requests","Because prefix tokens are not attended to by later tokens"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Causality makes the cache for positions 1..n a pure function of tokens 1..n. Nothing that follows can change it, so the vectors computed once are correct for any request beginning with those tokens. Outputs are not stored; later tokens do attend to the prefix."},{"q":"A system prompt is 3,000 tokens and byte-identical across requests, but the measured hit rate is near zero. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The system prompt is too long to cache","Temperature is set above zero, which disables caching","A volatile token such as a timestamp or request id appears before the system prompt in the rendered prompt","The model uses grouped-query attention, which is incompatible with prefix caching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Block hashes include everything before the block, so any difference at the start changes every hash. Length is not a problem, sampling settings do not affect K and V, and GQA simply makes the cache smaller."},{"q":"What is the main reason prefix caching under-delivers on a multi-replica deployment?","options":["Each replica has a smaller KV pool, so eviction is more frequent","Without cache-aware routing, repeat requests land on replicas that have not computed the prefix","Hashes are computed differently on each replica","Replicas cannot share GPU memory over the network"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The cache is local to a GPU. If a load balancer spreads a tenant's requests across replicas, most of them are cold on arrival. Sticky or hash-based routing fixes it; the engine was never the problem."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/continuous-batching","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Agrawal et al., 2023 — SARATHI: Efficient LLM Inference by Piggybacking Decodes with Chunked Prefills"],"why":"Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound: one request produces one token per full read of the weights. The only\nway to use the hardware is to produce many tokens per read, which means batching requests so that one\nstep advances all of them. Classic request-level batching does this the way every ML server did: collect\n*B* requests, run them together until all are done, then collect the next *B*. For fixed-cost models that is\nfine. For generation it fails because requests do not finish together. One stops after 20 tokens, another\nruns to 2,000. In a static batch the early finisher's slot sits idle, padded with nothing useful, until the\nlongest request ends, and no new request can start until the whole batch drains. Measured utilization\ncollapses toward that of the single longest sequence, and arriving requests see queueing delay equal to\nthe remaining life of the current batch.\n\nThe patch, \"make batches smaller or time-box them\", trades one waste for another: small batches lose the\nbandwidth amortization that was the whole point, and time-boxing kills long generations. The actual fix\nis to stop thinking of the batch as a unit of work. The model does not care which sequences are in a step;\nevery step is just \"take one token from each of these sequences\". So the batch membership can change\nbetween steps. A finished sequence frees its slot and its KV memory immediately; a waiting request is\nprefilled and joins the next step. The scheduler becomes a loop that runs once per iteration, which is why\nthe original paper called it iteration-level scheduling. Orca, which introduced it, still reserved each\nsequence's maximum length contiguously, so only a small batch fit in memory; paged KV memory (vLLM) was\nthe enabler that let the batch grow to the sizes the hardware could use, because joining and leaving\nsequences no longer require contiguous allocation or copying and no slot is reserved for tokens that\nmay never be generated.","how":"### The scheduler loop\nEach iteration the engine does roughly this:\n\n1. **Retire.** Remove sequences that emitted a stop token, reached `max_tokens`, or were cancelled; free\n   their KV blocks.\n2. **Admit.** Pull waiting requests from the queue, oldest first (or by priority), as long as two budgets\n   hold: the number of running sequences stays under `max_num_seqs`, and the tokens to be processed this\n   iteration stay under `max_num_batched_tokens`. A new request's prompt counts its full length against the\n   token budget because its prefill will run in this step; a running request counts one token.\n3. **Check memory.** Ensure every running sequence can get at least one more block; if not, preempt the\n   most recently admitted ones (recompute or swap) until the rest fit.\n4. **Run one forward pass** over the union: prefill tokens for newcomers and one decode token for each\n   running sequence, packed into one batch with per-sequence block tables.\n5. **Sample and stream** one token per sequence to its client, then loop.\n\n### Why one forward pass can mix prefill and decode\nA transformer layer applies the same weights to every token in the batch regardless of which sequence it\nbelongs to; only attention needs to know the boundaries, and with block tables each query reads its own\nsequence's cache. So a step with 3 newcomers of 500 tokens each plus 60 decoding sequences is a single\nbatch of 1,560 tokens, which is close to compute-bound and therefore efficient. The decode tokens ride\nalong almost free, because the weights were going to be read anyway.\n\n### The interference problem and chunked prefill\nThat same mixing has a cost: a 10k-token newcomer makes the iteration take as long as its prefill, and\nall 60 decoding users see a pause. Early implementations chose a policy: prefill-first (admit eagerly,\naccept TPOT spikes, minimize TTFT) or decode-first (defer admissions until decode is quiet, accept TTFT\ngrowth). Chunked prefill removes the dilemma by capping the prefill tokens per iteration and splitting a\nlong prompt across several steps, each of which also carries every decode token. TPOT stays smooth, the\nlong prompt's TTFT grows a little, and utilization is high every step because each step is filled up to\nthe token budget.\n\n### Throughput versus latency inside the loop\nPer-step time grows slowly with batch size (weights are read once; cache reads and compute grow with the\nnumber of tokens), while tokens produced per step grow linearly. So throughput rises steeply with\nconcurrency until the step becomes compute- or cache-bound, and every user's TPOT rises along the way.\n`max_num_seqs` and the token budget are the two dials: raise them to sell throughput, lower them to sell\nlatency. Queueing theory applies in front of the loop. Little's law sizes the batch you will actually\nneed: running sequences ≈ arrival rate × time each spends generating. At 5 requests/s and 300 output\ntokens at ~20 ms TPOT (6 s each), about 30 sequences are in flight on average, so a `max_num_seqs` of 16\nguarantees a growing queue however fast each step is. And if arrivals exceed what the loop retires, the\nqueue grows without bound and TTFT explodes, which is the signal for backpressure or more replicas.\n\n### What it does not fix\nContinuous batching maximizes GPU use; it does not shorten any single request's decode below the\nbandwidth floor, it cannot make a batch of one efficient, and it makes memory the binding constraint\nbecause the batch is now limited by how many caches fit rather than by a fixed batch size. That is why\nthis lesson and the KV-cache lessons are inseparable.","example":"A simulation of static versus continuous batching for the same workload, using an illustrative step-time\nmodel: a step costs a fixed weight-read time plus a small per-token cost. The output lengths are\ndeliberately skewed, as real traffic is.\n\n```python\nimport random\nrandom.seed(0)\nN, MAX_SEQS = 200, 32\nlengths = [random.choice([20, 50, 100, 400, 1500]) for _ in range(N)]   # output tokens per request\n\ndef step_ms(n_tokens):               # illustrative: 5 ms weight read + 0.02 ms per token\n    return 5.0 + 0.02 * n_tokens\n\ndef static_batching():\n    t = 0.0\n    for i in range(0, N, MAX_SEQS):\n        batch = lengths[i:i + MAX_SEQS]\n        for step in range(max(batch)):                       # batch lives until its longest finishes\n            active = sum(1 for L in batch if L > step)       # idle slots are wasted\n            t += step_ms(active)\n    return t\n\ndef continuous_batching():\n    t, waiting, running = 0.0, list(lengths), []\n    while waiting or running:\n        running = [L for L in running if L > 0]              # retire finished sequences\n        while waiting and len(running) < MAX_SEQS:           # admit newcomers into freed slots\n            running.append(waiting.pop(0))\n        t += step_ms(len(running))\n        running = [L - 1 for L in running]\n    return t\n\ntotal_tokens = sum(lengths)\nfor name, fn in ((\"static\", static_batching), (\"continuous\", continuous_batching)):\n    ms = fn()\n    print(f\"{name:11s}: {ms/1000:6.1f} s total, {total_tokens/ms*1000:8.0f} tok/s\")\n# With this seed: static ~54 s (~1.6k tok/s), continuous ~20 s (~4.5k tok/s), close to 3x,\n# because static batches spend most steps at low occupancy waiting for the 1500-token\n# outliers. Make the length skew harsher and the gap widens; make lengths uniform and it vanishes.\n```\n\nThe simulation ignores prefill and memory, which is exactly why it is clean: it isolates the one effect\nthat continuous batching is responsible for. Add a random long prefill to `step_ms` for newcomers and you\nreproduce the TPOT spike that chunked prefill exists to smooth.","tradeoffs":["Keeps the GPU saturated and multiplies throughput, but every admitted request lengthens each step and therefore every user's TPOT; the balance flips toward smaller `max_num_seqs` for interactive products.","Admitting newcomers eagerly minimizes TTFT but injects prefill into decode steps; deferring them does the opposite; chunked prefill blunts the trade at the cost of slightly longer TTFT on long prompts.","Iteration-level scheduling makes memory, not batch size, the limit, which packs far more requests but introduces preemption as a failure mode when the sequence-length tail is long.","A large per-iteration token budget raises utilization on long-prompt workloads but makes each step heavier; on chat workloads with short prompts a smaller budget gives smoother streaming."],"pitfalls":["Benchmarking with uniform output lengths; symptom is a load test that shows little gain over static batching, followed by surprise when production's skewed lengths behave completely differently.","Raising `max_num_seqs` until throughput peaks and shipping that; symptom is excellent tokens/sec with user complaints that text streams slowly, because TPOT was never on the dashboard.","Leaving chunked prefill disabled on a mixed workload; symptom is periodic stalls across all streams that correlate with the arrival of long prompts rather than with request rate.","Letting the waiting queue grow unbounded; symptom is TTFT climbing linearly over minutes under steady overload, because the loop retires fewer sequences per second than arrive.","Assuming continuous batching makes single-request latency better; symptom is disappointment when a lightly loaded server's TPOT equals the bandwidth floor regardless of settings."],"when":"Every production LLM engine does this; what you choose is the dials. Set `max_num_seqs` and the token\nbudget from a load test that records TTFT, TPOT and tokens/sec together, and pick the point where TPOT is\nstill inside the product's budget. Enable chunked prefill unless the workload is purely short prompts.\nWatch queue depth and preemptions as the overload signals, and add replicas or backpressure when the\nqueue grows faster than it drains. If you are building a thin server over a raw model library rather than\nan engine, this is the first feature you are missing, and the reason to switch to an engine. In interviews,\nexplaining why static batching wastes slots, how iteration-level scheduling fixes it, why it needs paged\nmemory, and what chunked prefill adds is the expected narrative.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes `max_num_seqs`, the per-iteration token budget and chunked prefill from load tests, and plots TPOT against concurrency to find the knee for the product's latency budget.","architect":"Sets the throughput-versus-latency policy per endpoint class, decides replica counts from the loop's retirement rate versus arrival rate, and defines the backpressure contract.","fde":"Demonstrates to a customer why their homegrown batching server caps out early, and reproduces their stutter by injecting long prompts into a loaded replica before proposing chunked prefill."},"check":{"q":"A team's engine shows 85% KV-cache utilization, average batch size 12, and users report that\nresponses stream fast at night and slowly during the day. Request rate doubles during the day. Explain\nwhat the scheduler is doing and how you would tune it without buying hardware.","a":"(Note the metric: KV-cache utilization, not GPU memory, which an engine pre-allocates and which\ntherefore always reads ~90%.) At night the running batch is small, so each decode step reads the\nweights plus a few caches and TPOT sits near the bandwidth floor. During the day the loop admits more sequences up to `max_num_seqs` or\nthe memory limit; average batch 12 is a daily average hiding daytime batches several times larger. Each\nstep now reads many caches and carries newcomers' prefill, so TPOT grows and streaming slows; that is the\nexpected throughput-for-latency trade, not a bug. First, put TPOT and batch size per step on the\ndashboard to confirm. Then enable chunked prefill so daytime admissions stop injecting stalls. Next,\ndecide the TPOT budget and cap `max_num_seqs` at the concurrency where the load test crosses it, accepting\nthat excess requests queue and TTFT rises; if the queue then grows without bound, the fleet is undersized\nand backpressure or a replica is the honest answer. Prefix caching for the shared system prompt reduces\nprefill tokens per iteration and helps both metrics. Only after those should quantization or hardware\nenter the conversation."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the core change continuous batching makes relative to static request-level batching?","options":["It pads shorter sequences so that all sequences in a batch finish together","It lets sequences join and leave the batch at every decode iteration rather than waiting for the whole batch to finish","It runs prefill and decode on separate GPUs","It increases the batch size until the GPU is compute-bound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The unit of scheduling becomes one iteration. Finished sequences free their slots immediately and waiting requests fill them, so occupancy stays high regardless of length skew. Padding is what static batching does; disaggregation and batch sizing are separate techniques."},{"q":"Why does continuous batching gain so much from paged KV-cache memory?","options":["Paging compresses the cache so more sequences fit in a static batch","Without paging each sequence reserves its maximum length contiguously, so few fit and the batch stays small; blocks let sequences join, grow and leave without reservations or copies","Paging allows prefill to be skipped for new sequences","Paging makes decode compute-bound"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Iteration-level scheduling works without paging (Orca did it), but contiguous worst-case reservations cap the number of resident sequences far below what memory could hold, and growing in place forces copies. Block-based allocation removes both limits. Skipping prefill is prefix caching, a different feature."},{"q":"What problem does chunked prefill solve inside a continuous-batching scheduler?","options":["It reduces the total compute required to prefill a long prompt","It lets prompts exceed the model's context window","It prevents a long newcomer's prefill from making one iteration so slow that every decoding user sees a stall","It allows prefill to run on the CPU while decode runs on the GPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Mixing a long prefill into one step stretches that step for everyone. Chunking spreads the prefill across several steps, each still carrying all decode tokens, so TPOT stays smooth. Total work and context limits are unchanged."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/pagedattention","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Every token in flight costs KV-cache memory: about 0.5 MB per token for a Llama-2-7B-style model in\nfp16 (see the KV-cache lesson for the derivation). The awkward fact is that when a request arrives, the\nserver has no idea how many tokens it will produce. Attention kernels before PagedAttention expected the\nK and V tensors of a sequence to be contiguous in memory, so the only safe thing to do was to reserve a\ncontiguous slab for the maximum possible length at admission time. For a 2,048-token limit that is about\n1 GB per request on the model above, whether the request ends up generating 20 tokens or 2,000.\n\nThat reservation strategy wastes memory in three ways. *Reserved* slots are held for tokens that have\nnot been generated yet, so nothing else can use them. *Internal fragmentation* is the part of the\nreservation the request never reaches because it stops early. *External fragmentation* appears between\nslabs of different sizes once requests with different limits come and go, leaving holes too small for the\nnext arrival. The vLLM paper measured that in prior systems only a minority of KV-cache memory, roughly\n20–40%, actually held token state; the rest was waste.\n\nThe naive fixes do not work. Allocating the exact length is impossible because the length is unknown.\nGrowing a contiguous buffer by reallocate-and-copy means copying hundreds of megabytes every few tokens\nand still fragments. Lowering the length limit refuses legitimate long requests. What was needed was the\nsame move operating systems made decades ago for RAM: stop requiring physical contiguity and translate\nlogical addresses through a table.","how":"### Blocks and block tables\nThe KV cache is carved into *physical blocks* of a fixed number of tokens, call it B. For one layer, a\nblock holds K and V for B tokens across all KV heads, so its size is `2 × n_kv_heads × head_dim × B ×\nbytes_per_element`. For Llama-2-7B in fp16 with B = 16 that is 2 × 32 × 128 × 16 × 2 = 262,144 bytes, 256\nKB per layer, or 8 MB per block summed over 32 layers, which is exactly 16 tokens × 0.5 MB.\n\nEach request owns a sequence of *logical blocks* (block 0 holds tokens 0–15, block 1 holds 16–31, and\nso on) and a *block table* mapping each logical block to the physical block that currently holds it.\nLogical blocks are contiguous; physical blocks can be anywhere in the pool. This is a page table, and the\nblock table lookup is the address translation.\n\n### Allocation on demand\nA request is admitted with only the blocks its prompt needs. When the last block fills during decode,\nthe scheduler hands out one more. The only waste is the unused tail of a request's final block, which is\nstrictly less than B tokens per sequence, and because every block has the same size there is no external\nfragmentation at all: any free block fits any request. This is *because* fixed-size units are the one\nallocation scheme where freed space is always reusable, which is why the kernel's memory manager and the\nOS page allocator work the same way.\n\n### The attention kernel\nThe engine hands the kernel a block-table tensor of shape [num_seqs, max_blocks_per_seq] plus each\nsequence's current length. For each sequence the kernel takes the new token's query, walks that row of\nthe table, loads K and V from each physical block, and accumulates attention block by block. Softmax\nneeds the maximum and the sum over *all* scores, which are unknown until the last block, so the kernel\nkeeps a running maximum *m*, a running denominator *l* and a running weighted sum of V, and rescales what\nit has accumulated by `exp(m_old − m_new)` whenever a later block raises the maximum (the same\nonline-softmax trick FlashAttention uses). The output is exact; only the memory layout changed.\n\nThe indirection costs something: memory accesses are less coalesced than for a single contiguous\ntensor, so the kernel itself is somewhat slower than a contiguous one. The design accepts that *because*\ndecode throughput is limited by how many sequences fit, not by per-sequence kernel speed; a slightly\nslower kernel over far more sequences wins. The idea has outlived the original kernel: current engines\noften run FlashAttention- or FlashInfer-family kernels that accept a block table directly, so\n\"PagedAttention\" now names the memory-management scheme more than one specific kernel.\n\n### Sizing the pool\nAt startup the engine measures what the weights take, runs a dummy forward pass at the maximum batch to\nfind peak activation memory, and gives the remainder of its configured budget (vLLM's\n`gpu_memory_utilization`, historically defaulting to 0.9) to the block pool. Capacity in tokens is simply\n`num_blocks × B`, and the scheduler admits a request only when enough free blocks exist for its prompt.\n\n### Sharing and copy-on-write\nBecause requests reference blocks indirectly, two requests can map the same physical block, tracked with\na reference count. Parallel sampling (`n > 1`) and beam search share the prompt's blocks instead of\ncopying them. Prefix caching extends this across requests: full blocks are hashed by their content (and\nthe prefix before them), so a new request whose system prompt matches an earlier one reuses the resident\nblocks and skips that part of prefill. When a sequence needs to write into a shared block, the engine\ncopies it first, the same copy-on-write rule as `fork()`.\n\n### Running out of blocks\nOn-demand allocation means the pool can run dry mid-generation. The scheduler then preempts a sequence,\neither by swapping its blocks to CPU memory or by freeing them and recomputing the prefix later (which of\nthe two an engine offers varies by version: vLLM's V1 engine recomputes only). Swap cost grows with the\nsequence's cache bytes over PCIe, recompute cost with the prefill FLOPs to rebuild it; the rough\nintuition is that recompute suits short sequences and swap long ones, but the crossover depends on the\nhardware and on how busy the GPU already is, so neither wins universally. Either way, preemption counters rising in the metrics is the sign the pool\nis too small for the traffic.\n\n### Choosing the block size\nLarger blocks mean fewer table entries and better memory coalescing in the kernel, but more waste in each\nsequence's last block and coarser prefix-cache sharing (only complete blocks are shared). Smaller blocks\nreverse the trade. Sixteen tokens is the common compromise.","example":"Capacity for a Llama-3-8B-class model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, fp16) on an 80 GB GPU.\nKV per token is 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 = 131,072 bytes = 128 KB, so a 16-token block is 2 MB across all\nlayers. With weights at ~16 GB, a 0.9 memory budget (72 GB) and an illustrative 2 GB activation peak,\nabout 54 GB goes to the pool: 27,648 blocks, roughly 442,000 tokens of KV capacity.\n\nCompare two allocators serving chats whose actual length averages 1,500 tokens under an 8,192-token\nlimit. Contiguous reservation holds 8,192 × 128 KB = 1 GB per request no matter what, so 54 requests fit.\nPaged allocation holds about 1,500 tokens plus a partial block per request, roughly 188 MB, so around 290\nrequests fit in the same pool, about 5× more, and the kernel never has to copy anything when a chat runs\nlong. The script below reproduces the arithmetic so you can plug in your own model.\n\n```python\ndef kv_capacity(gpu_gb, weights_gb, util=0.9, act_gb=2.0,\n                layers=32, kv_heads=8, head_dim=128, dtype_bytes=2, block=16):\n    per_token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes          # bytes\n    pool = (gpu_gb * util - weights_gb - act_gb) * 2**30\n    blocks = int(pool // (per_token * block))\n    return per_token, blocks, blocks * block\n\nper_tok, blocks, tokens = kv_capacity(80, 16)\nprint(f\"{per_tok/1024:.0f} KB/token, {blocks} blocks, {tokens} tokens of KV\")\nfor avg_len, max_len in [(1500, 8192), (6000, 8192)]:\n    contiguous = tokens // max_len                       # reserves max_len per request\n    paged = tokens // (avg_len + 15)                     # actual tokens + at most one partial block\n    print(f\"avg {avg_len}: contiguous fits {contiguous}, paged fits {paged}\")\n```","tradeoffs":["You recover most of the memory lost to reservation and fragmentation, but the attention kernel pays an indirection and coalescing cost per block, a net loss only when the batch is too small for the extra sequences to matter, such as one long request alone on a GPU.","On-demand allocation admits many more requests, but it can run out of blocks mid-generation, so the system needs a preemption policy (swap or recompute) that contiguous reservation never needed.","Block sharing makes beam search, parallel sampling and prefix caching nearly free in memory, at the cost of reference counting and copy-on-write logic that makes the memory manager harder to reason about.","Larger blocks give faster kernels and fewer table entries; smaller blocks waste less in partial blocks and share prefixes at finer granularity. The best size flips as average request length shrinks."],"pitfalls":["Reading the paper's throughput gain as \"a faster attention kernel\". The kernel is slightly slower; the 2–4× comes from batching more sequences. If your batch is already small, you will not see it.","Ignoring preemption metrics. A pool that is slightly too small shows up as a throughput cliff and jittery latency under load, not as an OOM, because sequences are silently swapped or recomputed.","Expecting prefix caching to share partially identical prompts. Only whole blocks with identical content and prefix are shared, so a timestamp in the first line of the system prompt defeats it entirely; the symptom is a prefix-cache hit rate near zero for prompts that look identical.","Setting `gpu_memory_utilization` to 0.98 to get more blocks and then OOMing when another process, a CUDA graph or a larger-than-profiled activation peak needs the headroom."],"when":"You do not switch PagedAttention on; every mainstream open engine (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM and\nothers) uses block-based KV management, so the decision is how to configure and observe it. Reach for the\nblock arithmetic when sizing a deployment: tokens of capacity, expected tokens per request, and therefore\nconcurrency. Watch block utilization and preemption counts in production; they tell you whether to lower\n`max_model_len`, raise the memory fraction, add prefix caching, or add GPUs. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is to explain the three kinds of waste, why fixed-size blocks remove two of them, and why the win is\nmeasured in concurrent sequences rather than kernel speed.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Tunes block pool size, `max_model_len` and prefix caching in the engine config, and reads preemption and cache-hit metrics to diagnose latency jitter under load.","architect":"Translates block capacity into a concurrency and context-length policy per GPU tier, and decides whether prompt design should favour shared prefixes to exploit block sharing.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their self-hosted engine serves 5× more chats than their old contiguous deployment, and why a dynamic header in their system prompt kills prefix reuse."},"check":{"q":"A team sees aggregate throughput collapse by half whenever traffic shifts from short Q&A to long\ndocument chats, with no OOM errors. Using PagedAttention concepts, what is happening and what would you\nchange?","a":"Long chats consume many more blocks per sequence, so the pool runs out mid-generation and the\nscheduler starts preempting sequences, swapping their blocks to CPU or dropping them and recomputing the\nprefix. Both cost time and the recomputation or swap traffic halves useful throughput while never raising\nan OOM, because allocation is lazy. I would confirm with the preemption and block-usage metrics, then\neither shrink demand per sequence (lower `max_model_len`, shorter retrieved context, fp8 KV cache),\nincrease supply (higher memory fraction after re-profiling activation peaks, a GQA model, more GPUs with\ntensor parallelism), or cap concurrency so admitted requests finish instead of thrashing. If the chats\nshare a long system prompt, enabling prefix caching recovers blocks too."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where does most of PagedAttention's throughput improvement come from?","options":["A faster attention kernel that reads memory in larger contiguous chunks","Fitting far more concurrent sequences in the same KV-cache memory because little is wasted","Skipping attention over padding tokens inside each block","Storing keys and values in 8-bit precision inside each block"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The paged kernel is actually slightly slower than a contiguous one because of the indirection. The gain comes from eliminating reservation and fragmentation waste, so many more sequences are decoded per step on a memory-bound workload."},{"q":"Why does fixed block size eliminate external fragmentation?","options":["Because blocks are allocated at startup and never freed","Because the block table compacts memory after each request finishes","Because any free block can satisfy any allocation, so freed space is always reusable","Because blocks are always allocated in contiguous runs per sequence"],"answer":2,"explanation":"External fragmentation arises when freed holes are the wrong size for the next request. With one block size there is no wrong size; the only waste left is the unused tail of a sequence's last block."},{"q":"Two requests share a 400-token system prompt but one adds a date on its first line. What happens to block sharing under prefix caching?","options":["Both still share all prompt blocks because the hash ignores digits","They share blocks after the differing line once the content realigns","They share nothing, because block hashes include the full prefix and diverge from the first block","They share blocks only if both requests arrive in the same batch"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blocks are identified by their content and everything before them, so a difference in the first block changes every subsequent hash. Shared content must be a byte-identical prefix to be reused."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/batching-and-concurrency-limits","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Take a Llama-3-8B-class model in fp16: about 16 GB of weights. On a GPU with roughly 3.35 TB/s of memory\nbandwidth (an H100 SXM's published figure), simply reading the weights once takes about 4.8 ms, which\ncaps a single sequence at roughly 200 tokens per second regardless of how fast the tensor cores are. The\nFLOPs for that one token, about 2 × 8 billion = 16 GFLOP, take a small fraction of that time. The GPU is\nmostly waiting on memory.\n\nNow put 64 sequences in the step. The weights are read once and reused for all 64 tokens, so the step\ntakes about the same 4.8 ms plus the extra KV-cache reads, and aggregate throughput rises toward 64×.\nThat is the whole reason serving systems batch: arithmetic intensity grows linearly with batch size until\nit reaches the hardware's ridge point (peak FLOPs ÷ bandwidth, roughly 300 FLOP/byte for that GPU in\nbf16), after which the step becomes compute-bound and latency per token starts growing with the batch.\nThe arithmetic is simple: each 2-byte bf16 weight does 2 FLOPs (a multiply and an add) per sequence, so\na batch of B gives about B FLOPs per weight byte, and the weight matmuls alone would stay bandwidth-bound\nup to a batch in the low hundreds. In practice the knee usually arrives earlier, for the reason in the\nlatency section below: each sequence also brings its own KV cache to read, and that traffic does not\namortise across the batch.\n\nSo why not admit everything? Three reasons. KV memory is finite and each admitted sequence consumes it\nfor its whole lifetime, so over-admission leads to preemption, swap traffic and eventually OOM. Latency\nobjectives: beyond the knee, time per output token (TPOT) climbs with every extra sequence and time to\nfirst token (TTFT) climbs with queue depth, so throughput keeps rising while the fraction of requests\nmeeting their SLO, the goodput, falls. And blast radius: one tenant sending 10,000-token prompts can\nstall everyone's decode if nothing bounds how many prefill tokens enter a step. Limits turn an engine\nthat degrades for everyone into one that queues or rejects predictably.","how":"### Static versus continuous batching\nStatic batching groups N requests, runs them until the longest finishes, and only then admits more; the\nGPU idles on finished slots. *Dynamic* batching, the classic model-server approach (Triton's\nqueue-delay setting is the familiar example), waits up to a few milliseconds to collect requests into\none batch, then runs it as a unit; that suits single-pass models such as embedders and classifiers,\nwhere every request takes the same one forward pass, but for generation it still holds the batch until\nthe longest output finishes. Continuous (iteration-level) batching, introduced by Orca, re-forms the\nbatch every decode step: finished sequences leave, waiting ones join. The continuous-batching lesson\ncovers the scheduler; here the point is that it makes \"batch size\" a dynamic quantity bounded by limits\nrather than a fixed number you pick.\n\n### The three limits inside an engine\nEvery engine exposes some version of these (vLLM's names in parentheses):\n1. **Maximum sequences per step** (`max_num_seqs`): bounds decode batch size and therefore worst-case\n   TPOT once compute-bound, and bounds the per-step sampling buffers.\n2. **Maximum tokens per step** (`max_num_batched_tokens`): bounds how many tokens, prompt and decode\n   together, are processed in one step. With chunked prefill (Sarathi-Serve's idea), a long prompt is split into chunks that\n   ride along with decode tokens, so a 20k-token prompt cannot monopolise a step and stall every\n   in-flight conversation. Lower values give smoother TPOT; higher values give faster prefill. The\n   budget can be modest *because* prefill is already batched across positions: a chunk of T prompt\n   tokens reuses each weight T times, so a chunk of a few hundred to a few thousand tokens already\n   saturates the tensor cores, and making it larger mainly lengthens the step that decode tokens wait on.\n3. **KV-cache capacity** (the block pool): the hard ceiling on tokens in flight. The scheduler admits a\n   request only if its prompt fits, and preempts when generation outgrows the pool.\nEach step the scheduler fills the step with decode tokens first, then admits waiting prompts up to the\ntoken budget, *because* stalling in-flight sequences is visible to users while delaying a new one by a\nfew steps usually is not.\n\n### Where the latency actually goes\nStep time is roughly `max(bytes_read / bandwidth, FLOPs / peak_FLOPs) + fixed overhead`. Bytes read\ninclude the weights *and the KV cache of every active sequence*, and the second term is the one people\nforget. With 64 sequences each at 6k tokens on the model above (128 KB of KV per token), the step reads\n64 × 6,144 × 128 KB = 48 GiB (about 51.5 GB) of cache on top of 16 GB of weights: about 20 ms, so\nroughly 50 tokens per second per user instead of 200. The cache reads now outweigh the weights three to\none, and they grow with every sequence added. Notice also that this configuration nearly fills an 80 GB\ncard: when the KV pool is full, each decode step reads close to all of HBM, so the step-time floor is\nroughly HBM capacity ÷ bandwidth (about 24 ms here) however the bytes divide between weights and cache.\nThe safe concurrency therefore depends on context length, not just request count, which is why limits\nshould be expressed in tokens.\n\n### Little's law and the queue\nConcurrency in the system equals arrival rate × residence time. If you need 10 requests/s and each\nlives 8 s, you need 80 in flight. If the knee for your traffic shape is 48, the queue grows without\nbound; no scheduler setting fixes that, only more replicas, a smaller model, shorter outputs or load\nshedding. This is the calculation that converts an engine benchmark into a replica count.\n\n### Enforcing the limit at three layers\n- **Engine:** the parameters above plus a bounded waiting queue; when it is full, fail fast with 429/503\n  rather than accepting a request that will time out anyway.\n- **Gateway:** per-tenant concurrency and token-rate caps so one client cannot consume the whole knee,\n  and priority or fair-share queuing where SLOs differ.\n- **Client:** a semaphore sized to the share it was given, retries with jitter only on explicit\n  retry-after signals, and timeouts longer than expected queueing time so retries do not amplify load.\n\n### Measuring the knee\nSweep concurrency with prompts and output lengths that match production, record aggregate tokens/s, p50\nand p95 TTFT and TPOT, and plot throughput against p95 latency. Throughput rises steeply, flattens, and\np95 latency bends upward; the bend is the knee. Re-run whenever the model, quantization, context\ndistribution or GPU changes, because every one of them moves it.","example":"A load sweep against an OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The client-side semaphore is the concurrency\nlimit; the script reports throughput and tail latency per level so you can see where the curve flattens.\nStreaming is used so TTFT and TPOT can be separated; each streamed chunk is counted as one token.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time, httpx\n\nURL, MODEL = \"http://localhost:8000/v1/completions\", \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"\nPROMPT, MAX_TOK = \"Explain why decode is memory-bandwidth bound.\", 128\n\nasync def one(client, sem):\n    async with sem:                                        # the client-side concurrency limit\n        t0, first, n = time.perf_counter(), None, 0\n        body = {\"model\": MODEL, \"prompt\": PROMPT, \"max_tokens\": MAX_TOK, \"stream\": True}\n        async with client.stream(\"POST\", URL, json=body, timeout=300) as r:\n            async for line in r.aiter_lines():\n                if line.startswith(\"data:\") and \"[DONE]\" not in line:\n                    n += 1\n                    first = first or time.perf_counter() - t0\n        total = time.perf_counter() - t0\n        first = total if first is None else first           # no tokens streamed: count as all wait\n        return first, (total - first) / max(n - 1, 1), n   # TTFT, TPOT, tokens\n\nasync def sweep(level, requests=64):\n    sem, t0 = asyncio.Semaphore(level), time.perf_counter()\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:\n        res = await asyncio.gather(*(one(client, sem) for _ in range(requests)))\n    wall = time.perf_counter() - t0\n    ttft = sorted(r[0] for r in res); tpot = sorted(r[1] for r in res)\n    print(f\"conc={level:3d} tok/s={sum(r[2] for r in res)/wall:7.0f} \"\n          f\"p50 TTFT={ttft[len(ttft)//2]*1e3:6.0f}ms p95 TPOT={tpot[int(len(tpot)*.95)-1]*1e3:5.1f}ms\")\n\nfor level in (1, 4, 16, 64):\n    asyncio.run(sweep(level))\n# Expect tok/s to climb steeply then flatten while p95 TPOT bends upward: that bend is the knee.\n# Re-run with your real prompt and output length distribution before choosing limits.\n```","tradeoffs":["A higher sequence limit raises throughput and lowers cost per token, but once the step is compute-bound or KV-read-bound every extra sequence adds to every user's TPOT.","A lower tokens-per-step budget keeps TPOT smooth during prompt bursts, but stretches TTFT for long prompts because their prefill is spread over more steps.","Rejecting at the queue (429/503) protects latency for admitted requests, but pushes retry logic and fairness onto clients; accepting everything is simpler and fails slowly for all.","Per-tenant caps guarantee isolation, yet leave capacity idle when one tenant is quiet unless the gateway can lend unused share, which adds scheduling complexity."],"pitfalls":["Limiting by request count only; a handful of 100k-token requests exhausts the KV pool while the count looks healthy. The symptom is preemption spikes and latency jitter at low request rates.","Measuring the knee with short synthetic prompts, then deploying to traffic with long retrieved context. Throughput looks fine in the benchmark and TPOT doubles in production.","Client timeouts shorter than queueing time under load, so clients retry while the original request is still running: load doubles exactly when the system is saturated.","Disabling chunked prefill for peak prefill speed and then seeing in-flight conversations stall for hundreds of milliseconds each time a long prompt arrives.","Setting the tokens-per-step budget very high. Engines that profile the activation peak at startup (vLLM) respond by shrinking the KV pool, so capacity silently drops; engines that do not profile OOM on a large prefill, a failure that only shows up when a big prompt and a full decode batch coincide."],"when":"Every self-hosted deployment needs explicit limits; the question is their values. Start from the VRAM\nbudget to get a token ceiling, run the sweep above with production-shaped traffic to find the latency\nknee, and set the sequence and token limits a little below it. Use Little's law to convert the target\nrequest rate into replicas. Apply per-tenant caps whenever more than one team or customer shares an\nengine, and always bound the queue. When using a hosted API the same thinking applies to provider rate\nlimits: a client-side semaphore and token budget avoid 429 storms. In interviews, be ready to derive why\nbatching is nearly free for decode, where it stops being free, and what you would measure to find out.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Sets engine limits, writes the load-sweep harness, sizes client semaphores, and adds queue-depth and preemption metrics so the knee is visible on a dashboard.","architect":"Owns the SLO-to-capacity model: goodput targets, per-tenant isolation policy, replica count from Little's law, and when to split prefill and decode onto separate pools.","fde":"Reproduces the customer's traffic shape in a sweep, explains why their long-document workload needs lower concurrency than the vendor benchmark suggested, and tunes limits on site."},"check":{"q":"A single-GPU deployment reports 3,000 tokens/s at concurrency 32 and 3,400 tokens/s at\nconcurrency 96, so the team wants to raise the limit to 96. What would you check before agreeing, and\nwhat do those numbers suggest?","a":"Throughput rose only 13% for a 3× increase in concurrency, so the engine is already past the\nknee: it is compute-bound or, more likely with long contexts, bound by KV-cache reads. Per-user rate fell from\nabout 94 tokens/s (3,000 ÷ 32) to about 35 (3,400 ÷ 96), so each user's TPOT is roughly 2.5–3× worse at\n96 for almost no extra throughput, so goodput against the\nlatency SLO probably fell. I would check p95 TPOT and TTFT at both levels, preemption and queue-depth\nmetrics, and KV block utilization, and I would re-run the sweep with production prompt and output length\ndistributions. If more throughput is needed, the answer is a second replica, a quantized or smaller\nmodel, or shorter contexts, not a higher limit on the same GPU."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does adding sequences to a decode batch raise throughput almost linearly at first?","options":["Because the GPU runs each sequence on a separate streaming multiprocessor in parallel","Because the weights are read from memory once per step and reused for every sequence in the batch","Because attention cost is shared across sequences that have similar prompts","Because the sampler can pick tokens for all sequences in a single kernel launch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Decode is bound by streaming the weights through memory bandwidth. One read serves all sequences in the step, so until the step becomes compute-bound or KV reads dominate, more sequences cost little extra time."},{"q":"What does a tokens-per-step budget with chunked prefill primarily protect?","options":["Peak memory used by the model weights","Time to first token for the longest prompts in the queue","Time per output token for conversations already in flight when long prompts arrive","The accuracy of generated tokens under high load"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without the budget, a long prompt's prefill occupies entire steps and stalls every active decode. Chunking spreads it over steps so TPOT stays smooth, at the cost of a slower TTFT for that long prompt."},{"q":"You need 20 requests/s and each request lives about 6 s in the system. The measured knee for one replica is 40 concurrent requests. How many replicas do you need, roughly?","options":["One, because 40 concurrent requests exceeds 20 requests per second","Two, because 20 requests per second is half the knee","Three, because Little's law gives 120 concurrent requests and each replica handles 40","Six, because 120 concurrent requests must be halved for headroom"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Concurrency = arrival rate × residence time = 20 × 6 = 120 in flight. At a knee of 40 per replica that is three replicas before headroom. Confusing requests per second with concurrent requests is the classic error."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tensor-parallelism-at-inference","sources":["Shoeybi et al., 2019 — Megatron-LM: Training Multi-Billion Parameter Language Models Using Model Parallelism","Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"A 70B-parameter model in bf16 is about 140 GB of weights. No single 80 GB GPU can hold it, let alone\nleave room for a KV cache. Something has to split the model, and the question is what to split and what\nthat costs per token.\n\nPipeline parallelism splits by layers: GPU 0 runs layers 1–40, GPU 1 runs 41–80. It fits the model, but\na single request still passes through the GPUs one after another, so per-token latency does not improve,\nand keeping both GPUs busy needs several requests in flight at different stages. Offloading keeps part of\nthe weights in CPU memory and streams them in, but PCIe moves data roughly 50–100× slower than HBM,\nso decode slows to seconds per token. Quantization halves or quarters the bytes but costs quality, and a\n4-bit 70B model still needs about 40 GB: it fits an 80 GB card, does not fit a 24 GB one, and leaves\nlittle cache room on a 48 GB one.\n\nTensor parallelism splits *inside* each layer. Every GPU works on the same token at the same time on its\nslice of every matrix, so the memory bandwidth of N GPUs is pooled: decode, which is bandwidth-bound,\ngets faster roughly in proportion to N as long as the cost of exchanging partial results stays small.\nThat is why TP is the default within a node for large models, and why its limits are set by the\ninterconnect rather than by the GPUs.","how":"### Megatron-style partitioning\nConsider a two-matrix MLP, `Y = GeLU(X·A)·B`, with A of shape [h, 4h] and B of shape [4h, h]. Split A by\n*columns* into N pieces: each GPU computes `GeLU(X·A_i)`, an [tokens, 4h/N] slice. No communication is\nneeded *because* GeLU is elementwise, so each slice of the intermediate activation is independent. Then\nsplit B by *rows* to match: GPU i computes the partial product `GeLU(X·A_i)·B_i`, which has the full\noutput shape [tokens, h] but contains only its share of the sum. One all-reduce adds the N partial\noutputs and every GPU ends up with the full Y. Llama-style SwiGLU MLPs have three matrices, but the\npattern is identical: gate and up projections column-parallel, down projection row-parallel, one\nall-reduce.\n\nAttention is split by heads. With 64 query heads and TP = 8, each GPU owns 8 query heads, their\nprojections, and the K/V heads they attend with, so the attention computation for those heads is\nentirely local. The output projection W_O is row-parallel, producing partial sums that one all-reduce\ncombines. Embeddings and the LM head are split along the vocabulary dimension. Net result: two\nall-reduces per layer per forward pass, and every GPU holds 1/N of the weights.\n\n### The KV cache is sharded too\nBecause heads are partitioned, each GPU stores K and V only for its heads: per-GPU KV cache per token is\nthe full figure divided by N, so the aggregate free memory of all N GPUs is available for cache. This is\noften the real reason to choose TP = 4 over TP = 2 even when the weights fit at TP = 2.\n\nGrouped-query attention adds a constraint. Llama-3-70B has 8 KV heads, so TP up to 8 gives each GPU at\nleast one; beyond that, KV heads must be replicated across GPUs that share them, which engines support\nbut which forfeits part of the memory saving. The number of attention heads, the MLP intermediate size\nand the vocabulary should all be divisible by the TP degree, which is why TP degrees are almost always powers of two.\n\n### Why the interconnect decides everything\nEach all-reduce exchanges a [tokens_in_step, hidden] activation. For a decode step with 32 sequences on\na hidden size of 8,192 in bf16, that is 32 × 8,192 × 2 = 512 KB, and an 80-layer model issues 160 of them\nper step. These messages are far too small to be bandwidth-bound; the cost is per-collective latency,\ndozens of microseconds each on NVLink with NCCL as an illustrative order of magnitude. A few milliseconds\nof overhead per step is acceptable when the step itself is 10 ms, but on PCIe-only hosts each collective\nis slower, and across nodes over Ethernet or even InfiniBand the latency multiplied by 160 per token is\nruinous. Hence the rule: tensor parallel within a node, pipeline parallel (or independent replicas)\nacross nodes.\n\nA step-time model makes the limit explicit:\n\n```\nt_step(N) ≈ (weight_bytes + kv_bytes_read) / (N × HBM_bw)  +  2 × n_layers × t_allreduce(N)  +  overhead\n```\n\nThe first term shrinks as 1/N; the second does not shrink at all, and grows slightly with N because a\nring all-reduce takes 2(N − 1) communication steps (each GPU sends about 2(N − 1)/N of the message in\ntotal, so bandwidth cost is nearly flat in N while latency is not). Speedup therefore saturates once the\ncollectives are comparable to the memory term. Illustratively, for a 70B bf16 model on H100-class GPUs\nthe memory term is about 141 GB ÷ (4 × 3.35 TB/s) ≈ 10.5 ms at TP = 4 and 5.3 ms at TP = 8; add 160\nall-reduces at an assumed 20 µs each (3.2 ms) and the step goes from about 13.7 ms to 8.5 ms, a 1.6×\ngain for 2× the GPUs. Engines fight the second term directly: vLLM, for example, ships a custom\nall-reduce kernel for small messages over NVLink, and CUDA graphs remove per-kernel launch overhead from\nthe decode step.\n\nPrefill is a different regime. Its all-reduce messages are [prompt_tokens, hidden]: 4,096 × 8,192 × 2\nbytes = 64 MiB each for the model above, so the collectives become bandwidth-bound rather than\nlatency-bound. NVLink's bandwidth hides them behind prefill's large matmuls; over PCIe they can become a\nlarge share of time to first token.\n\n### When TP speeds up a model that already fits\nTP = 2 on a model that fits one GPU roughly halves the weight bytes each GPU reads per step, so\nsingle-stream decode gets faster, less than 2× because of the all-reduces. But two independent replicas\n(data parallelism) give 2× throughput with zero communication at the same per-token latency. If you\ncare about tokens per second per user, TP; if you care about aggregate tokens per dollar at acceptable\nlatency, replicas, as long as each replica has enough KV room.\n\n### Related splits\nPipeline parallelism (layer ranges, cross-node friendly, adds latency), expert parallelism (whole experts\nof a mixture-of-experts model on different GPUs, exchanging tokens via all-to-all), and context or\nsequence parallelism (splitting a very long prompt across GPUs for prefill) are combined with TP in large\ndeployments. At inference on a single node, TP alone is the common case.","example":"Row- and column-parallel partitioning of one MLP, run with `torchrun --nproc_per_node=2`. Each rank\nkeeps only its slice of the weights, computes its partial output, and the single all-reduce recovers the\nfull-precision result; rank 0 checks it against the unsharded computation and prints the bytes the\ncollective moved, which is the per-layer communication cost discussed above.\n\n```python\n# torchrun --nproc_per_node=2 tp_mlp.py\nimport torch, torch.distributed as dist\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\n\ndist.init_process_group(\"nccl\")\nrank, N = dist.get_rank(), dist.get_world_size()\ntorch.cuda.set_device(rank); dev = f\"cuda:{rank}\"\nh, ff, tokens = 4096, 11008, 8                     # hidden, intermediate, tokens in this decode step\ntorch.manual_seed(0)                               # identical full weights on every rank\nA = torch.randn(h, ff, dtype=torch.float16) * 0.02  # up projection  [h, ff]\nB = torch.randn(ff, h, dtype=torch.float16) * 0.02  # down projection [ff, h]\nX = torch.randn(tokens, h, dtype=torch.float16)\n\nA_i = A[:, rank * ff // N:(rank + 1) * ff // N].to(dev)   # column-parallel slice of A\nB_i = B[rank * ff // N:(rank + 1) * ff // N, :].to(dev)   # matching row-parallel slice of B\nX = X.to(dev)\n\nZ_i = F.gelu(X @ A_i)                              # [tokens, ff/N]; elementwise GeLU needs no comm\nY_i = Z_i @ B_i                                    # partial sum with full output shape [tokens, h]\ndist.all_reduce(Y_i, op=dist.ReduceOp.SUM)         # the one collective for this MLP\n\nif rank == 0:\n    ref = F.gelu(X.cpu().float() @ A.float()) @ B.float()\n    print(\"max abs error vs unsharded:\", (Y_i.cpu().float() - ref).abs().max().item())\n    print(\"bytes moved by the all-reduce:\", Y_i.numel() * Y_i.element_size())\n    print(\"weight bytes held per rank:\", (A_i.numel() + B_i.numel()) * 2)\ndist.destroy_process_group()\n```\n\nA sizing check for the same idea at scale: a Llama-3-70B-class model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim\n128) has 320 KB of fp16 KV per token. At TP = 4 on 80 GB GPUs, each holds about 35 GB of weights and 80\nKB of KV per token; with a 0.9 budget and an illustrative 2 GB activation reserve, about 35 GB per GPU\nremains for cache, roughly 450k tokens. The weight-read floor per step on an H100-class GPU is 35 GB ÷\n3.35 TB/s ≈ 10 ms, so single-stream decode cannot exceed about 95 tokens/s before all-reduce overhead.\nAt TP = 2 each GPU holds about 70 GB of weights against a 72 GB budget, leaving essentially nothing for\ncache once the activation reserve is taken: not a usable deployment in bf16.","tradeoffs":["Per-token latency falls with TP because bandwidth is pooled, but each extra GPU adds all-reduce overhead; the gain flips negative once collective latency rivals the shrinking step time.","Weights and KV cache divide by N, letting a large model fit and serve long contexts, but N GPUs are tied to one request stream and a single GPU failure takes the whole replica down.","Compared with data-parallel replicas, TP gives faster single-stream decode; replicas give more total throughput per GPU-hour. Which wins depends on whether the SLO is per-user speed or cost.","Compared with pipeline parallelism, TP has no pipeline bubble and lower latency, but it demands a fast intra-node interconnect; pipeline parallelism tolerates slow links between nodes."],"pitfalls":["Running TP across PCIe-only GPUs or across nodes and seeing decode slower than on a single GPU with a quantized model; the symptom is tokens/s that barely improves or regresses as TP grows.","Choosing a TP degree that does not divide the KV head count, forcing KV replication; the symptom is less cache capacity than the per-GPU arithmetic predicted.","Assuming TP = 2 doubles throughput. Throughput per GPU usually drops relative to two replicas, because the communication is pure overhead when the batch is already large enough to saturate one GPU.","Forgetting that each TP rank keeps its own CUDA context, NCCL buffers and activation workspace, so the per-GPU memory left for cache is less than weights ÷ N would suggest.","Mixing GPU models or driver versions within a TP group; collectives run at the pace of the slowest device and some combinations fail to initialise at all."],"when":"Use TP when the model does not fit one GPU at an acceptable precision, or when it fits but single-stream\ndecode latency is the binding SLO and the GPUs share NVLink. Keep the degree as small as the memory\nbudget allows, usually the smallest power of two that leaves healthy KV room, and prefer replicas for\nscaling throughput beyond that. Across nodes, use pipeline parallelism or more replicas instead. Measure\ntokens/s per request and aggregate tokens/s at TP = 1, 2, 4 before committing; the curve tells you where\ncommunication starts to dominate on your hardware. Interviewers expect you to draw the column/row split,\ncount the all-reduces per layer, and explain why interconnect, not FLOPs, limits TP.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Picks `tensor_parallel_size`, verifies NVLink topology with `nvidia-smi topo -m`, and benchmarks per-request versus aggregate throughput across TP degrees before locking a config.","architect":"Decides the node shape and parallelism strategy per model tier: TP within NVLink nodes, replicas for scale, pipeline across nodes, and the failure domain each implies.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's slow 70B deployment on PCIe-attached GPUs and proposes a quantized single-GPU or NVLink-node alternative with measured tokens/s to back it."},"check":{"q":"A customer has eight A10G-class GPUs (24 GB each, PCIe only, no NVLink) and wants to serve a 70B\nmodel with sub-50 ms time per output token. Will TP = 8 get them there, and what would you propose?","a":"Fitting is possible: 140 GB of bf16 weights divided eight ways is 17.5 GB per GPU, leaving a few\nGB each for KV cache, which is thin but workable with a short context cap. The latency target is the\nproblem. An 80-layer model issues about 160 all-reduces per decode step; over PCIe each one costs far\nmore than on NVLink, and the per-GPU weight read of 17.5 GB at A10G-class bandwidth is already tens of\nmilliseconds. Sub-50 ms per token is unrealistic on that box. I would propose a 4-bit quantized 70B at\nTP = 2 or 4 to cut both bytes and collectives, evaluate its quality on their tasks, or move to an\nNVLink-connected node, and I would benchmark TP = 2, 4 and 8 on their hardware rather than argue from\nfirst principles alone."},"quiz":[{"q":"In Megatron-style tensor parallelism, why is the first MLP matrix split by columns and the second by rows?","options":["Because column splits are faster for cuBLAS and row splits save memory","So the elementwise activation between them needs no communication and one all-reduce finishes the layer","Because the second matrix is transposed in memory in most checkpoints","So that each GPU can hold a different attention head"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Splitting A by columns yields independent slices of the intermediate activation; the nonlinearity acts on each slice alone. Splitting B by rows lets each GPU produce a partial sum of the output that one all-reduce completes."},{"q":"What primarily limits how many GPUs you can usefully put in a tensor-parallel group at decode time?","options":["The total FLOPs of the GPUs","The size of the vocabulary","The latency of the two all-reduces per layer relative to the shrinking per-step compute","The number of transformer layers, since each GPU needs at least one"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decode messages are tiny, so collectives are latency-bound. As N grows each step gets shorter while the number of collectives stays fixed, so communication becomes the dominant cost, especially without NVLink."},{"q":"A model fits comfortably on one GPU. You need twice the aggregate throughput at the same per-token latency. Which option is usually best?","options":["Tensor parallelism across two GPUs","Two independent replicas behind a load balancer","Pipeline parallelism across two GPUs","CPU offloading half the layers to free memory for a larger batch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Replicas double throughput with no communication overhead and keep latency unchanged. TP would lower per-token latency but spends bandwidth and time on all-reduces, so throughput per GPU is lower."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/cpu-offloading","sources":["Sheng et al., 2023 — FlexGen: High-Throughput Generative Inference of Large Language Models with a Single GPU","Rajbhandari et al., 2021 — ZeRO-Infinity: Breaking the GPU Memory Wall for Extreme Scale Deep Learning","Official docs: llama.cpp","Official docs: Hugging Face Accelerate"],"why":"The model does not fit. A 70B model is about 140 GB in bf16 and about 40 GB at 4-bit, and the GPU on\nthe desk has 24 GB. Options are a smaller model, more aggressive quantization, more GPUs, or putting the\noverflow somewhere else and fetching it on demand. Host RAM is plentiful and cheap (hundreds of GB on a\nworkstation), and the PCIe bus can move data in both directions while the GPU computes, so offloading\nlooks like free capacity.\n\nThe catch is in the arithmetic of decode. Each generated token needs every weight once. Whatever is not\nresident on the GPU must cross PCIe on every step. A Gen4 x16 link moves about 25 GB/s in practice (the\ntheoretical figure is a little above 30 GB/s; Gen5 roughly doubles it), while an H100's HBM delivers\naround 3.35 TB/s and even a consumer GPU around 1 TB/s. Streaming 20 GB of weights per token therefore\ncosts about 0.8 s per token, before any computation happens. Offloading just 10% of a 40 GB model still\nputs roughly 160 ms of bus transfer on every token, several times the resident compute.\n\nSo \"just offload the layers that don't fit\" is a trap for interactive use. The idea survives because\nthree situations change the arithmetic: the offloaded share is small enough to be a rounding error, the\nworkload is offline and a huge batch amortises each weight transfer over thousands of tokens (FlexGen's\ndesign), or the thing offloaded is KV cache for sequences that are idle or preempted, which is read once\nwhen they resume rather than on every step.","how":"### What can live off the GPU\n1. **Weights**, usually at layer granularity. Hugging Face Accelerate's `device_map=\"auto\"` places\n   layers on GPU until it is full and the rest on CPU (or disk via `offload_folder`); vLLM has a\n   `--cpu-offload-gb` option; DeepSpeed's ZeRO-Inference and FlexGen stream weights with prefetching.\n2. **KV cache**, at block granularity: vLLM's `--swap-space` (its V0 scheduler; the V1 engine preempts\n   by recomputation only) reserves pinned host memory to swap the blocks of preempted sequences; tiered prefix caches keep warm system-prompt blocks in CPU RAM.\n3. **Activations and optimizer state**, which matter for training (ZeRO-Offload, ZeRO-Infinity) and\n   barely at all for inference.\n\n### Two execution styles\n*Streaming offload* keeps the compute on the GPU and copies each offloaded layer's weights in before it\nruns, ideally prefetching layer i+1 on a second CUDA stream while layer i computes. The host buffers must\nbe *pinned* (page-locked) memory *because* DMA engines cannot read pageable memory; an unpinned transfer\ngoes through a staging copy and roughly halves effective bandwidth. Decode cannot hide the transfer\nbehind compute because the compute per layer for one token is microseconds, so step time is essentially\noffloaded bytes ÷ PCIe bandwidth. Prefill of long prompts, or decode of very large batches, has enough\ncompute per layer to overlap the copies, which is exactly FlexGen's regime.\n\n*Split execution* leaves CPU-resident layers on the CPU and computes them there. llama.cpp's\n`-ngl` (number of GPU layers) works this way: the first N layers run on the GPU, the rest on CPU cores\nusing host memory bandwidth, typically tens of GB/s on a dual-channel desktop and a few hundred on a\nserver. Nothing crosses PCIe except the activations at the boundary, so the cost is CPU memory bandwidth\nand CPU matmul speed rather than bus speed. This is why split execution usually beats streaming at batch\nsize 1: host memory bandwidth (tens to hundreds of GB/s) is at least comparable to, and on a server well\nabove, the 25–60 GB/s of a PCIe link, and the weights only have to be read where they already live. The\ntwo halves run one after the other for each token, so the costs add: `step ≈ gpu_bytes / HBM_bw +\ncpu_bytes / host_bw`. Half of a 40 GB model on a desktop with ~60 GB/s of host bandwidth is about\n20 ms + 330 ms; on a many-channel server it is far less, but the CPU half still dominates the step.\n\n### A timing model\nWhen transfer and compute overlap, `step_time ≈ max(resident_bytes / HBM_bw, offloaded_bytes /\nPCIe_bw) + overhead`; when they do not, add them. Because PCIe bandwidth is so much lower, the second\nterm dominates as soon as more than a few percent of weights are offloaded. Solve the formula backwards\nto find how much you can offload while meeting a latency target; the answer is usually \"less than you\nhoped\", which is why quantizing first is nearly always the better lever.\n\n### KV cache swap versus recompute\nWhen the block pool runs dry, a preempted sequence's blocks can be swapped to pinned host memory and\nswapped back when it resumes, or dropped and recomputed by re-running prefill. Swapping an 8k-token\ncontext of a Llama-3-8B-class model moves about 1 GB, roughly 40 ms over Gen4, while recomputing its\nprefill costs on the order of a hundred TFLOP, several hundred milliseconds. For short prompts recompute\nis simpler and nearly free; for long prompts swap wins, although the crossover moves with GPU speed and\nlink speed. Which one runs is an engine policy rather than a per-request optimisation: vLLM's V0\nscheduler, for instance, recomputed single-sequence requests by default and swapped multi-sequence\ngroups (beam search, `n > 1`), for which it did not implement recomputation. The swap space size\nbounds how many sequences can be parked.\n\n### Mixture-of-experts changes the arithmetic\nIn an MoE model each token is routed to a few experts per layer, so a decode step reads only the routed\nexperts' weights, not the whole expert set. That makes a split that would be hopeless for a dense model\nworkable: keep attention, shared layers and the KV cache on the GPU, keep the large expert FFNs in host\nRAM, and compute the routed experts on the CPU. Bytes read on the CPU side per token scale with the\n*active* expert parameters while memory scales with the *total*, which is the right match for cheap,\nlarge host RAM. llama.cpp supports this kind of placement through its tensor-override options; batching\nerodes the advantage, because a batch of tokens routes to many different experts and the CPU ends up\nreading most of them each step.\n\n### NVMe and unified memory\nZeRO-Infinity and FlexGen extend the hierarchy to NVMe (GPU ← CPU ← SSD), with GPUDirect Storage able to\nDMA from disk to GPU, at bandwidths of a few GB/s per drive. At the other end, Apple silicon and Grace\nHopper systems expose unified memory shared by CPU and GPU, so a 40 GB model on a 64 GB Mac is not\n\"offloaded\" at all; it is simply bandwidth-limited by that machine's memory, which is why llama.cpp\nruns large models acceptably there.","example":"A 70B model quantized to about 4.5 bits per weight (the 4-bit `Q4_0` layout: 32 weights × 4 bits plus a\n16-bit scale is 144 bits per 32 weights) is roughly 40 GB across 80 layers, about 0.5 GB per layer. The\ntarget machine has a 24 GB GPU with about 1 TB/s of memory bandwidth and a Gen4 x16 slot. Reserving 4 GB\nfor KV cache, activations and the CUDA context leaves 20 GB for weights: 40 layers resident, 40 layers\nstreamed. The resident half costs 20 GB ÷ 1 TB/s = 20 ms per token; the streamed half costs 20 GB ÷ 25\nGB/s = 800 ms. Overlapped, the step is max(20, 800) = 800 ms, about 1.2 tokens/s, versus roughly 25\ntokens/s if the whole model were resident. Offloading only 10% (4 GB) still gives max(36, 160) = 160 ms,\na 4× slowdown. The calculator below reproduces this and lets you solve for the resident share a latency\nbudget allows.\n\n```python\ndef step_ms(total_gb, gpu_frac, hbm_tbs=1.0, pcie_gbs=25.0, overlap=True):\n    resident, offloaded = total_gb * gpu_frac, total_gb * (1 - gpu_frac)\n    t_gpu = resident / hbm_tbs                 # GB / (TB/s) = ms, since 1 TB/s is 1 GB per ms\n    t_bus = offloaded / pcie_gbs * 1000        # GB / (GB/s) = s, converted to ms\n    return max(t_gpu, t_bus) if overlap else t_gpu + t_bus\n\nfor frac in (1.0, 0.9, 0.5):\n    ms = step_ms(40, frac)\n    print(f\"{frac:.0%} resident -> {ms:6.0f} ms/token, {1000/ms:5.1f} tok/s\")\n\nbudget_ms = 100                                  # latency target per token\nmax_offload_gb = budget_ms / 1000 * 25.0         # bytes PCIe can move within the budget\nprint(f\"a {budget_ms} ms budget tolerates at most {max_offload_gb:.1f} GB offloaded\")\n```","tradeoffs":["Offloading turns an impossible deployment into a possible one, but every offloaded gigabyte of weights adds roughly 40 ms per token over Gen4 PCIe; worthwhile only when the share is tiny or latency is irrelevant.","Large-batch offline generation amortises weight transfers across many tokens per step, so throughput can be acceptable where single-stream latency is not; it flips back to useless when requests must be answered one at a time.","KV swap preserves long contexts cheaply compared with recomputation, but pinned host memory is reserved up front and PCIe traffic competes with any weight streaming on the same link.","Split execution on the CPU avoids bus traffic and suits a few layers on a fast machine, yet CPU matmul throughput caps batch size, so it does not scale to many concurrent users."],"pitfalls":["`device_map=\"auto\"` silently placing layers on CPU when the GPU is slightly too small; the symptom is a model that \"works\" at one or two tokens per second with the GPU at 5% utilization and host RAM full.","Streaming from pageable instead of pinned memory, which halves effective bandwidth and adds CPU copy overhead; visible as transfer time far above the PCIe spec.","Sizing swap space too small for the long-context tail. Depending on the engine, preemptions then fail outright or fall back to recomputation; the symptom is aborted requests, or a throughput cliff with prefill-heavy GPU traces, when concurrency rises.","Benchmarking on a workstation with a Gen5 x16 slot and deploying to a cloud instance whose GPU sits on a shared or narrower link; identical settings, half the speed.","Treating offloading as an alternative to quantization. Quantizing from fp16 to 4-bit removes 75% of the bytes with modest quality cost; offloading 75% of the bytes multiplies latency by tens."],"when":"Reach for weight offloading when a model is a few percent too large for the GPU and a short-term fix is\nacceptable, for offline batch jobs where tokens per hour matter and latency does not, for local\nexperimentation on a machine that is not going to serve anyone, or when the remaining layers can run on\na strong CPU via split execution. Prefer quantization, a smaller model, or tensor parallelism for anything\ninteractive. Use KV offloading deliberately: size the swap space for your long-context tail and watch\npreemption and swap metrics. Measure tokens/s with and without offload before deciding; the formula above\npredicts the result well. In interviews, the test is whether you can compute the PCIe penalty rather\nthan assert that offloading \"makes it fit\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures layer placement or swap space, pins host buffers, and reads GPU utilization and transfer timings to spot accidental offloading before it ships.","architect":"Decides where offloading is permitted (batch pipelines, dev boxes) and where it is banned (interactive SLOs), and chooses quantization or more GPUs as the standard remedy.","fde":"Gets a customer's oversized model running on the hardware they already own for a pilot, with explicit latency expectations, and a plan to move to properly sized GPUs for production."},"check":{"q":"A customer runs a 4-bit 70B model on a single 48 GB GPU by offloading the overflow to CPU and\nreports 3 tokens/s. They ask whether a faster CPU would help. What is your analysis and recommendation?","a":"A 4-bit 70B model is around 40 GB of weights; with KV cache and runtime overhead it slightly\nexceeds 48 GB, so a few gigabytes are offloaded. If the engine streams those layers over PCIe, step time\nis dominated by offloaded bytes divided by bus bandwidth, roughly 40 ms per gigabyte on Gen4, and a\nfaster CPU changes nothing; 3 tokens/s is about 330 ms per token, consistent with roughly 7–8 GB crossing\na Gen4 link every step. If it computes them on the CPU, the offloaded share runs at host memory\nbandwidth, so more memory channels help more than a faster core clock, and neither reaches GPU-class\nspeed. The real fix is to remove the\noverflow: cap context length so the KV cache shrinks, use a slightly smaller quantization or a model\nwith fewer KV heads, or add a second GPU with tensor parallelism. I would measure the offloaded size and\ntransfer time first, then predict the gain of each option before they buy anything."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does offloading even a small fraction of weights hurt decode latency so much?","options":["The CPU must re-quantize the weights on every transfer","GPU kernels cannot start until all layers are resident","Every decode step needs every weight once, and PCIe bandwidth is orders of magnitude below GPU memory bandwidth","Offloaded layers lose their KV cache and must recompute attention"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decode streams the full weight set per token. Bytes that live across PCIe move at tens of GB/s rather than terabytes per second, so a small offloaded share can take longer than the entire resident computation."},{"q":"When is swapping a preempted sequence's KV cache to CPU preferable to recomputing it?","options":["Always, because recomputation wastes GPU memory","When the context is long, so moving the cache over PCIe is cheaper than re-running prefill","When the context is short, because small transfers are nearly free","Only when the model uses grouped-query attention"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Swap cost scales with cache bytes, recompute cost with prefill FLOPs over the prompt. For long prompts the transfer is tens of milliseconds while prefill is hundreds; for short prompts recompute is simpler and about as fast."},{"q":"Why must host buffers used for GPU transfers be pinned?","options":["Pinned memory is compressed, so fewer bytes cross the bus","The GPU cannot address memory above 4 GB otherwise","DMA engines need page-locked memory; otherwise data goes through a staging copy and bandwidth drops","Pinned memory lets the GPU execute CPU layers directly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Page-locked memory cannot be swapped out, so the DMA engine can read it directly. Pageable memory forces the driver to copy into a pinned staging buffer first, roughly halving effective transfer bandwidth."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/quantization-quality-evaluation","sources":["Dettmers et al., 2022 — LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale","Frantar et al., 2022 — GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","Lin et al., 2023 — AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization for LLM Compression and Acceleration","Official docs: llama.cpp"],"why":"A 4-bit checkpoint lets a 70B model fit where a 16-bit one did not and roughly quarters the bytes decode\nmust stream, so the pressure to quantize is constant. The method lesson in Training & adaptation covers\n*how* GPTQ, AWQ, int8 and fp8 work; this one is about *whether the result is still the model you tested*.\nThe card that ships with a quantized checkpoint says something like \"perplexity 5.47 → 5.62 on\nWikiText-2\" (illustrative numbers),\nand teams read that as \"lossless enough\" and deploy.\n\nThat inference is unsafe for several reasons. Perplexity averages log-loss over every token of generic\ntext, and most tokens are easy; a task usually hinges on a few decisive tokens (a digit, a function\nname, a closing brace) whose probabilities can shift a lot while the average barely moves. The\ncalibration data used by GPTQ or AWQ, often a few hundred segments of web text, may not resemble your\ndomain, so the error is concentrated where you were not looking. Outlier activation channels, which\nLLM.int8() showed emerge in models above a few billion parameters, make some layers far more fragile\nthan others. And evaluation sets of a few hundred items have noise larger than the effect you are\nhunting, so a naive \"accuracy dropped 1%\" is often meaningless in either direction.\n\nThe naive alternative, trusting the vendor's benchmark or eyeballing a few chats, fails precisely on the\nrare, high-stakes behaviours that production exposes at scale. What is needed is a layered measurement\nthat goes from cheap distribution fidelity to expensive task evidence, with the full-precision model as\nthe control.","how":"### Step 1: distribution fidelity with teacher forcing\nRun the same fixed text through both models and compare their next-token distributions position by\nposition. Because the context is forced to be identical, this isolates the quantization error from\nsampling and from divergence after a first wrong token. Three numbers come out of one pass:\n- **Perplexity delta** on held-out text: WikiText-2 and C4 are the conventional sets, but add a sample of\n  *your* domain text, because that is where calibration mismatch shows.\n- **Mean KL divergence** `KL(P_fp16 || P_quant)` per token. KL catches cases where perplexity improves\n  by accident while the distribution moves; it is the most sensitive cheap signal. Look at the tail as\n  well as the mean (the 99th-percentile or maximum per-token KL), *because* damage concentrates on a few\n  positions and a small mean can hide a handful of badly wrong ones. llama.cpp's perplexity tool can save\n  full-precision logits and report KL statistics, including percentiles, against a quantized run.\n- **Top-1 agreement**: the fraction of positions where both models' argmax token matches. Under greedy\n  decoding this is a direct proxy for how often the quantized model would have gone a different way.\nThese are deterministic and take minutes, so they belong in CI for every new quantized artifact.\n\n### Step 2: public benchmarks, run yourself\nRe-run a standard harness (EleutherAI's lm-evaluation-harness is the usual one) on both models with\nidentical settings. Knowledge-style multiple choice (MMLU-type) tends to survive 4-bit quantization\nwell; multi-step arithmetic (GSM8K-type), code generation (HumanEval-type) and instruction-following\nformat checks (IFEval-type) are the categories that reveal damage first. Report the delta, not the\nabsolute score, and report it with a confidence interval.\n\n### Step 3: your golden set, your graders\nTake the golden dataset you already use for releases and run both models with the *same* prompts,\n*same* system prompt, *same* decoding parameters. Use greedy decoding for the primary comparison to\nremove sampling noise, then repeat with the production configuration over several seeds. Grade with the\nsame task graders; for open-ended outputs, use pairwise LLM-as-judge with the fp16 output as the\nreference and randomised position. Because every prompt is scored under both models, use *paired*\nstatistics (paired bootstrap or a sign test on per-item wins) rather than comparing two independent\nmeans. The arithmetic shows why. On 300 items at 80% accuracy, two independent runs give a standard error\non the difference of √(2 × 0.8 × 0.2 / 300) ≈ 3.3 points, a 95% interval of about ±6.4. If the models\ndisagree on only 15 items (5%), the paired standard error is roughly √(0.05 / 300) ≈ 1.3 points, an\ninterval near ±2.5: the same data now resolves effects less than half the size.\n\nCount the *flips* as well as the net change: items that went correct → wrong and wrong → correct. A\nquantized model can hold accuracy flat while 10 answers break and 10 others happen to improve, and the\nusers who owned those 10 broken answers see a regression the average hides. The flip count is also the\ndiscordant count that drives the paired statistics, so it costs nothing extra to report.\n\n### Step 4: stress the known weak spots\nAdd targeted slices *because* average scores mask them: long-context retrieval (needle-style prompts at\nyour maximum context, especially if the KV cache is quantized too), multilingual prompts, numbers and\ndates, JSON or tool-call validity rates, and refusal or safety behaviour, which can shift in either\ndirection. Check speed as well: weight-only 4-bit helps bandwidth-bound decode but some kernels slow\nprefill through dequantisation overhead, so a \"faster\" model can raise time to first token.\n\n### Step 5: production confirmation\nShip behind a canary or shadow deployment and compare task metrics and user-facing signals against the\nfp16 fleet before full rollout. Treat the quantized artifact as a new model version in the registry with\nits own evaluation record, because that is what it is.\n\n### Knowing where damage comes from\nWeight-only schemes (W4A16: GPTQ, AWQ, NF4) keep activations in 16-bit and usually lose little on 7B-and-\nlarger models; smaller models lose more at the same bit width. Weight-and-activation schemes (W8A8 int8,\nfp8) exploit faster tensor-core math for prefill and large batches but must handle activation outliers,\nwhich is what SmoothQuant-style scaling and fp8's dynamic range address. Group size, per-channel versus\nper-tensor scales and the calibration set all move the result, so two \"4-bit\" files of the same model can\ndiffer more from each other than either differs from fp16. Quantizing the KV cache to fp8 is generally\nreported as low-impact; 4-bit KV is where long-context retrieval tends to degrade.","example":"A teacher-forced comparison of a full-precision model and its 4-bit counterpart on a sample of domain\ntext. It reports mean KL per token, top-1 agreement and perplexity under both models. Run this on your\nown text, not only on WikiText, and gate on the deltas before investing in the slower task evals.\n\n```python\nimport math, torch, torch.nn.functional as F\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B\"                        # swap in the checkpoint you plan to ship\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nfp16 = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16, device_map=\"cuda\").eval()\nq4 = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    name, device_map=\"cuda\",\n    quantization_config=BitsAndBytesConfig(load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n                                           bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.float16)).eval()\n\ntexts = open(\"domain_sample.txt\").read().split(\"\\n\\n\")[:200]   # YOUR domain, same text for both\nkl = agree = nll16 = nll4 = n = 0\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for t in texts:\n        ids = tok(t, return_tensors=\"pt\", truncation=True, max_length=1024).input_ids.cuda()\n        lp = F.log_softmax(fp16(ids).logits[0, :-1].float(), -1)   # predictions for positions 1..T\n        lq = F.log_softmax(q4(ids).logits[0, :-1].float(), -1)     # identical context: teacher forced\n        tgt = ids[0, 1:]\n        kl += (lp.exp() * (lp - lq)).sum(-1).sum().item()           # KL(P_fp16 || P_q4), summed\n        agree += (lp.argmax(-1) == lq.argmax(-1)).sum().item()\n        nll16 += -lp.gather(1, tgt[:, None]).sum().item()\n        nll4 += -lq.gather(1, tgt[:, None]).sum().item()\n        n += tgt.numel()\nprint(f\"tokens={n}  mean KL/token={kl/n:.4f}  top-1 agreement={agree/n:.2%}\")\nprint(f\"ppl fp16={math.exp(nll16/n):.3f}  ppl 4bit={math.exp(nll4/n):.3f}\")\n# Next: run the golden set under both models with identical decoding and compare with paired bootstrap.\n```","tradeoffs":["Perplexity and KL are cheap and sensitive, but they measure distribution drift on fixed text, not task outcomes; a model can pass them and still fail a format-critical workflow.","Task evals on your golden set are the real acceptance test, yet they cost GPU time and need enough items for paired statistics; for small sets, judge disagreement can exceed the quantization effect.","Greedy decoding gives a clean comparison but is not how production runs; sampled comparisons are realistic but need several seeds to average out noise.","Stricter acceptance thresholds protect quality but can block a quantization that would have cut serving cost in half; the right threshold depends on what the task's errors cost."],"pitfalls":["Comparing against a published fp16 benchmark score instead of re-running the fp16 model yourself under identical prompts and settings; the symptom is a \"regression\" that is really a harness difference.","Judging a quantized model by chat impressions. Fluency survives quantization long after arithmetic, code and long-context recall have degraded, so the demo looks fine and the eval does not.","Ignoring the calibration set. A GPTQ or AWQ file calibrated on English web text can degrade noticeably on code or another language; the tell is a domain KL far above the WikiText KL.","Quantizing weights and KV cache at once and attributing any loss to the weights; test them separately, since long-context failures usually trace to the cache.","Declaring a 0.8-point accuracy drop \"within noise\" or \"significant\" without a confidence interval from paired per-item results.","Reporting only net accuracy. Equal numbers of fixed and broken items cancel in the mean; the symptom is an eval that says \"no change\" followed by specific, reproducible complaints from production users."],"when":"Run the full ladder whenever a quantized artifact is a candidate for production: a new checkpoint, a new\nquantization method or group size, a new engine kernel, or KV-cache quantization. Make steps 1 and 3\nmandatory gates in the model release pipeline and steps 2 and 4 part of the evaluation record. Skip the\nexpensive steps only for throwaway experiments. Watch for the categories that break first and weight\nthem by business cost: a support bot may tolerate what a code assistant cannot. In interviews, the\nexpected answer to \"how do you know 4-bit is fine?\" is this ladder plus the reason perplexity alone is\nnot enough, not a quoted benchmark number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Owns the teacher-forced comparison script and the golden-set run, with paired statistics, wired into CI so every quantized artifact gets a delta report automatically.","architect":"Sets the acceptance thresholds per use case and the policy that quantized models are registered, evaluated and rolled out as distinct model versions.","fde":"Builds the domain text sample and golden set from the customer's own data, and shows them the measured quality-versus-cost curve before recommending a quantized deployment."},"check":{"q":"A team reports that their 4-bit model's WikiText perplexity rose by 2% and MMLU dropped by 0.3\npoints, and concludes it is safe for their contract-extraction product. What would you push back on?","a":"Both signals are generic. WikiText perplexity averages over easy tokens of encyclopaedic prose,\nand MMLU is multiple choice, which is the category that survives quantization best. Contract extraction\ndepends on long inputs, exact numbers and dates, and valid structured output, which are exactly the\ncategories that degrade first, and none were measured. I would ask for KL and top-1 agreement on a sample\nof their contracts, a paired comparison of extraction accuracy and JSON validity on their golden set under\nidentical prompts and decoding, a long-context slice at their maximum document length, separate tests if\nthe KV cache is quantized, and confidence intervals on every delta, plus the list of flipped items\nso someone reads what actually broke. A 0.3-point MMLU drop without an interval is not evidence either\nway."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is per-token KL divergence a better early signal of quantization damage than perplexity alone?","options":["KL is cheaper to compute because it does not need the reference text","KL measures how far the whole next-token distribution moved, so it catches shifts that leave average log-loss almost unchanged","Perplexity cannot be computed for quantized models","KL directly predicts task accuracy on downstream benchmarks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Perplexity only scores the probability of the actual next token. KL compares the full distributions, so it flags cases where alternative tokens gain or lose mass even when the target token's probability is similar."},{"q":"Two evaluations of the same quantized model on a 300-item golden set use the same prompts for both models. Which analysis is most appropriate?","options":["Compare the two mean accuracies and call any drop above 1% a regression","Run the quantized model five times and average, ignoring the fp16 run","A paired per-item comparison with a bootstrap confidence interval on the difference","A chi-squared test on the total number of correct answers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because each item is scored under both models, pairing removes item difficulty from the variance and makes the test far more sensitive at the same sample size. Independent-mean comparisons waste that structure."},{"q":"Which category is most likely to show quantization damage first?","options":["Short factual multiple-choice questions","Casual conversational fluency","Multi-step arithmetic, code and long-context retrieval with strict output formats","Translation between high-resource European languages"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Tasks that hinge on a few decisive tokens over long chains, or on recalling a detail far back in a quantized KV cache, amplify small distribution shifts. Fluency and recognition-style tasks are robust."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/structured-constrained-generation","sources":["Willard & Louf, 2023 — Efficient Guided Generation for Large Language Models","Dong et al., 2024 — XGrammar: Flexible and Efficient Structured Generation Engine for Large Language Models","Tam et al., 2024 — Let Me Speak Freely? A Study on the Impact of Format Restrictions on Performance of Large Language Models","Official docs: vLLM"],"why":"Code downstream of a model needs something it can parse: a JSON object with known fields, a function\ncall with typed arguments, one of five labels. Asked nicely, models mostly comply, and \"mostly\" is the\nproblem. They wrap JSON in Markdown fences, add a trailing comma, rename a field, emit a comment, or\nstart with \"Sure, here is the JSON\". At a 2% failure rate, a pipeline running a million calls a day\nbreaks twenty thousand times, and the failures cluster on the hardest inputs.\n\nThe naive fix is validate-and-retry: parse the output, and if it fails, send it back with the error\nmessage. That works at low volume and is still worth having for semantic checks, but each retry doubles\nlatency and cost on exactly the requests that were already slow, the failure rate is never zero, and\nsmall or heavily quantized models can loop without converging. Post-processing with regex repair is\nbrittle in the other direction: it silently accepts malformed intent.\n\nConstrained decoding attacks the root. The model proposes a distribution over next tokens; the engine\nknows, from the grammar and what has been emitted so far, which tokens are legal; it zeroes the rest\nbefore sampling. Invalid output becomes impossible rather than unlikely. The mechanism exists in open\nengines (vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp, TensorRT-LLM) and behind hosted \"structured outputs\" and strict\ntool-calling modes, so the pattern is now the default way to get machine-readable output.","how":"### Where the constraint sits\nEach decode step produces a logits vector over the vocabulary. A constraint is a *logits processor*: it\ncomputes the set of allowed token ids for the current state and adds −∞ to every other entry. Softmax\nthen assigns those tokens zero probability, and temperature, top-p and the rest apply only among the\nallowed ones. The model is never asked to do anything; it is simply not permitted to make certain moves.\n\n### The token-versus-character problem\nGrammars are defined over characters, but the model emits tokens, and a vocabulary of 100k+ entries\ncontains many tokens that straddle grammar boundaries: `\"na`, `me\":`, ` {\"`. The brute-force check,\n\"for each of 128k tokens, would appending its characters keep the output valid?\", costs a full grammar\nwalk per token per step, which is far too slow for a decode step budgeted in milliseconds.\n\nOutlines' insight was to do that work once. Compile the regex (or the regex derived from a JSON schema)\ninto a deterministic finite automaton, then, for every automaton state, walk every vocabulary token\nthrough the automaton and record which tokens are legal from that state and which state each leads to.\nAt generation time the engine tracks the current state and the allowed set is a table lookup; the\nexpensive vocabulary scan happened at compile time *because* it depends only on the grammar and the\ntokenizer, not on the request.\n\nA tiny case shows what the index holds. Take the pattern \"exactly three digits, then stop\" and a toy\nvocabulary containing `7`, `42`, `123`, `1234`, `a` and ` 5`. From the start state (no digits yet), `7`\nleads to the one-digit state, `42` to the two-digit state, `123` straight to the accepting state, and\n`1234`, `a` and ` 5` are illegal. From the two-digit state only single-digit tokens are legal; from the\naccepting state only end-of-sequence is. Note that multi-character tokens jump several automaton steps\nat once, which is exactly what a character-level grammar check cannot see without walking each token.\n\nNon-recursive JSON schemas compile to regexes; recursive or deeply nested grammars need a pushdown automaton (a context-free grammar), which is what llama.cpp's GBNF grammars and\nXGrammar implement, the latter caching the tokens whose legality does not depend on the stack so that\nmost steps are still a lookup.\n\n### What the automaton controls\nThree things fall out of the state machine. End-of-sequence is allowed only in an accepting state, so the\nmodel cannot stop halfway through an object. When exactly one token is legal (a closing brace, a fixed\nkey name), some engines skip sampling and append it directly, which saves decode steps on boilerplate.\nAnd enums, numeric ranges and string patterns become hard constraints: a `\"status\"` field declared as\n`enum: [\"open\", \"closed\"]` cannot come back as `\"Closed\"`.\n\n### The cost at serving time\nMasking has to fit inside a decode step that takes milliseconds for a whole batch, so engines treat it as\na pipeline problem. The grammar state advance and mask computation run on the CPU while the GPU computes\nthe forward pass for the same step, and the result is applied as a compact per-sequence bitmask over the\nvocabulary just before sampling; when mask computation is slower than the forward pass, it shows up\ndirectly as higher time per output token. Appending runs of forced tokens without sampling, mentioned\nabove, is what SGLang calls jump-forward decoding. Every sequence in a batch can carry a different\ngrammar, so the CPU-side work (compiling grammars and computing each sequence's mask) is the part that\nhas to scale with concurrency; applying the finished mask on the GPU is cheap.\n\n### What it does not control\nA valid schema says nothing about whether the values are true. A hallucinated invoice number passes a\nregex for invoice numbers. Semantic validation (Pydantic validators, cross-field rules, database\nlookups) still belongs after generation, and retry-with-feedback remains the right tool for *those*\nfailures. Constrained decoding also changes the distribution: at a point where the model wanted to write\nprose, it must choose among whitespace or a quote, and may take a low-probability path. Masking and\nrenormalising at each step is also not the same as sampling from the model's distribution conditioned\non producing valid output: the mask is greedy and local, so the model can be steered into a prefix whose\nonly legal continuations it considers unlikely, and it is never allowed to back up. Tokenization\nartefacts matter too; forcing output to begin with `{` rather than ` {` can land the model in a token\nsequence it rarely saw in training. One 2024 study (Tam et al.) reported that strict format restrictions\ncan degrade reasoning performance in some settings, which is why the mitigations below exist.\n\n### Design rules that follow\n- Put a free-text `reasoning` or `analysis` field *before* the answer fields, or run a two-stage\n  pipeline: think freely, then extract under constraint. The model reasons in prose; the schema captures\n  the result.\n- Keep schemas flat and small; prefer enums to open strings; make field order match the order evidence\n  appears.\n- Compile and cache grammars by schema hash. Compilation ranges from milliseconds to seconds for large\n  schemas, and a per-request dynamic schema throws that away every call.\n- Do not confuse \"JSON mode\" with schema enforcement: JSON mode guarantees syntactically valid JSON of\n  any shape, while structured-output modes enforce your schema's fields, types and enums.\n- Check backend coverage: hosted strict modes and open-engine backends support a *subset* of JSON Schema\n  (for example requiring every field and forbidding additional properties), so validate the schema\n  against the backend before relying on a feature.\n\n### Tool calling is the same mechanism\nFunction-calling APIs are structured generation with a schema per tool plus a choice over which tool to\ncall. Hosted \"strict\" tool modes and open engines' tool parsers run the same automaton idea over the\nargument schema, which is why argument validity is high while argument *correctness* still needs checks.","example":"A from-scratch constrained decoder for the simplest grammar: the output must be exactly one of a fixed\nset of choices, then stop. The allowed-token table is a trie built once from the tokenizer, so each step\nis a lookup rather than a vocabulary scan, which is the same structure Outlines and XGrammar generalise\nto regexes and grammars.\n\n```python\nimport torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, LogitsProcessor, LogitsProcessorList\n\nname = \"Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct\"\ntok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(name, torch_dtype=torch.float16).cuda().eval()\n\nclass ChoiceConstraint(LogitsProcessor):\n    \"\"\"Allow only token sequences that spell one of `choices`, then EOS.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, choices, prompt_len):\n        self.trie, self.prompt_len = {}, prompt_len\n        for c in choices:                                   # built ONCE: grammar x tokenizer\n            # one canonical tokenization per choice; real engines allow every token path that spells it\n            node = self.trie\n            for t in tok(c, add_special_tokens=False).input_ids:\n                node = node.setdefault(t, {})\n            node[tok.eos_token_id] = {}                     # accepting state: EOS becomes legal\n    def __call__(self, input_ids, scores):\n        node = self.trie\n        for t in input_ids[0, self.prompt_len:].tolist():  # replay emitted tokens to find the state\n            node = node.get(t, {})\n        allowed = list(node) or [tok.eos_token_id]\n        mask = torch.full_like(scores, float(\"-inf\"))\n        mask[0, allowed] = 0.0\n        return scores + mask                                # every other token gets probability 0\n\nmsgs = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Is the sky green? Answer with one word.\"}]\nids = tok.apply_chat_template(msgs, add_generation_prompt=True, return_tensors=\"pt\").cuda()\nproc = LogitsProcessorList([ChoiceConstraint([\"yes\", \"no\", \"unknown\"], ids.shape[1])])\nout = model.generate(ids, max_new_tokens=5, do_sample=False, logits_processor=proc)\nprint(repr(tok.decode(out[0, ids.shape[1]:], skip_special_tokens=True)))   # -> 'no'\n```","tradeoffs":["Syntactic validity becomes guaranteed and retries vanish, but the constraint can push the model down low-probability paths; the cost is small for flat schemas and grows with rigid, deeply nested ones.","Compile-once tables make per-step masking nearly free, but a new schema per request pays compilation each time; the win flips to a loss without a grammar cache.","Hosted strict modes are zero-effort, yet they support a schema subset and hide the mechanism; open engines expose regex, grammar and choice modes but you own backend selection and version drift.","Validate-and-retry handles semantic errors that no grammar can express, but it costs a full extra call per failure; combining both gives shape for free and spends retries only on meaning."],"pitfalls":["Trusting a schema-valid output as correct. The symptom is clean JSON with invented identifiers or wrong numbers that no parser will ever flag.","Placing the answer field first and a `reasoning` field last, so the model commits to an answer before it has \"thought\"; accuracy on reasoning tasks drops compared with unconstrained prompting.","Sending a fresh, slightly different schema on every request (a timestamp or request id baked into an enum), visible as high time to first token from grammar compilation on each call.","Using JSON Schema features the backend ignores (`format: date`, `minimum`, `pattern` in some modes) and discovering in production that values outside the range come through.","Forgetting that whitespace is part of the grammar. A schema-derived regex that allows unbounded whitespace between tokens lets a model loop on spaces or newlines until `max_tokens`; one that forbids it forces minified JSON the model rarely saw in training. Set the whitespace pattern deliberately."],"when":"Use constrained decoding whenever output is consumed by code: extraction, classification into a fixed\nlabel set, tool arguments, routing decisions, batch data transformation. Prefer the engine's or\nprovider's native structured-output mode over hand-rolled logits processors, and keep Pydantic (or\nequivalent) validation plus bounded retries for semantics. Skip it for free-form writing, and be careful\nwith reasoning-heavy tasks, where a two-stage design keeps accuracy. Measure validity rate, retry rate,\ntime to first token with and without the grammar, and task accuracy against an unconstrained baseline.\nIn interviews, the test is explaining why masking happens at the logits, how the token-boundary problem\nis solved by compile-time precomputation, and what the guarantee does not cover.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines Pydantic models, passes the schema to the engine's structured-output mode, caches compiled grammars, and adds semantic validators and bounded retries behind them.","architect":"Sets the contract between model outputs and downstream services, chooses hosted strict modes versus open-engine backends, and mandates where free-text reasoning precedes structured answers.","fde":"Turns a customer's extraction target into a schema the backend fully supports, then proves validity and accuracy on their documents against the unconstrained baseline."},"check":{"q":"A team switched an extraction pipeline from prompt-and-retry to constrained JSON decoding.\nValidity went to 100%, but accuracy on a set of hard cases dropped several points. What would you\ninvestigate and change?","a":"Constrained decoding changed the distribution the model samples from, not just the format. I\nwould first check field order: if the answer fields come before any reasoning field, the model is forced\nto commit before working through the evidence, and moving a free-text analysis field to the front or\nsplitting into a think-then-extract two-stage call usually recovers the loss. I would check whether the\ngrammar forces unusual tokenizations (no leading space, escaped newlines) that differ from how the model\nsaw JSON in training, and whether the schema is stricter than necessary, with enums that exclude valid\nanswers. Then I would compare accuracy on the same prompts under unconstrained decoding plus parsing to\nquantify the gap, and keep semantic validators and bounded retries for the residual errors."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do libraries like Outlines precompute a table from automaton states to allowed tokens?","options":["To reduce the model's vocabulary size at load time","Because logits cannot be modified after the forward pass","So each decode step is a lookup instead of checking every vocabulary token against the grammar","To make sampling deterministic regardless of temperature"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Checking 100k+ tokens against a grammar every step is far too slow. The grammar and tokenizer are fixed, so the legality of each token from each state can be computed once and reused for every request."},{"q":"What does constrained decoding guarantee about a generated JSON object?","options":["That every value is factually consistent with the input","That it parses and conforms to the schema's structure, types and enums","That it is the most probable output the model could produce","That it matches what the model would have produced without the constraint"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The automaton only forbids tokens that would break the grammar. Content is still the model's guess, so semantic validation and retries remain necessary for correctness."},{"q":"A reasoning-heavy classification task loses accuracy under a strict schema. What is the most likely effective fix?","options":["Raise the temperature so the model explores more tokens","Remove enums so the model can write any label","Add a free-text reasoning field before the answer field, or extract in a second constrained call","Switch from JSON to YAML output"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forcing an immediate answer removes the model's chance to work through the problem in prose. Letting it reason first, then constraining the final fields, restores the behaviour that unconstrained prompting had."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/gpu-memory-optimization","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Dao et al., 2022 — FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","Official docs: vLLM","Official docs: PyTorch (CUDA memory management)"],"why":"Two failures bracket the problem. The first is the out-of-memory error at peak traffic: the deployment\npassed every test because tests used short prompts and modest concurrency, and allocation is lazy, so\nthe first time a long prompt met a full decode batch the engine died. The second is quieter and costs\nmore: an 80 GB GPU running a model that leaves 50 GB unused because someone left the concurrency limit\nat a cautious default, so tokens per dollar is a third of what the hardware allows.\n\nBoth come from treating memory as something you observe rather than compute. `nvidia-smi` shows a\nnumber that mixes allocated, cached and reserved memory, and a model that \"uses 18 GB\" at idle can need\n60 GB under load. The naive alternative, \"start it and see if it fits\", answers the wrong question,\nbecause fitting at idle says nothing about capacity at peak, and because the quantities that matter,\ntokens in flight and prompt length distribution, are properties of traffic, not of the model.\n\nThe arithmetic is short and exact enough to plan on. Weights are a fixed cost known from the parameter\ncount and precision. KV cache is a per-token cost known from the architecture, multiplied by a traffic\nquantity you choose to cap. Activations are bounded by the largest step you allow. Everything else is a\nfew gigabytes of overhead you measure once. With those four terms you can decide, before launching,\nhow many concurrent conversations of what length a GPU can hold, and which lever to pull when it cannot.","how":"### Line 1: weights\n`bytes = params × bytes_per_param`. bf16/fp16 is 2, fp8/int8 is 1, 4-bit is 0.5 plus the scales and\nzero points: the simple `Q4_0` layout stores 32 weights × 4 bits plus a 16-bit scale, 4.5 bits per\nweight; group-128 schemes such as GPTQ or AWQ land near 4.1–4.2. Published parameter counts include the\nembedding table and LM head, but if you estimate from per-layer shapes, add them: for a 128k-token\nvocabulary and hidden size 8,192 in bf16 each is about 2.1 GB, and an untied model has both. They are\nalso often left at higher precision by weight-only quantizers, so a \"4-bit\" file is larger than\nparams × 0.5 suggests. Anything else resident belongs on this line too: a draft model for speculative\ndecoding (plus its own KV cache), a vision encoder, preallocated LoRA adapter slots. For mixture-of-experts models count *all* experts, since every expert must be resident even\nthough only a few run per token; a model with 47B total and 13B active parameters costs 94 GB in bf16,\nnot 26 GB.\n\n### Line 2: KV cache\n`bytes_per_token = 2 × n_layers × n_kv_heads × head_dim × bytes_per_element`, then multiply by the\ntokens in flight: the sum over active sequences of prompt plus generated tokens so far. With paged\nallocation that is the *actual* total, not the sum of per-request maximums. The lever list is the formula\nread term by term: fewer KV heads (GQA or MLA architectures), smaller elements (fp8 cache), fewer tokens\n(context cap, concurrency cap, prefix sharing), or divide the whole thing across GPUs with tensor\nparallelism.\n\n### Line 3: activations and workspace\nDuring prefill a chunk of T tokens flows through each layer as a [T, hidden] tensor, expands to\n[T, intermediate] inside the MLP, and attention needs its own buffers. FlashAttention-style kernels\nnever materialise the [T, T] score matrix, so attention memory is linear in T; without them a 32k\nprompt would need gigabytes of scores per head. Chunked prefill caps T per step, which caps this peak\nregardless of prompt length. For scale: with T = 8,192 and a 70B-class intermediate size of 28,672, the\ngate and up projections each produce 8,192 × 28,672 × 2 bytes ≈ 0.47 GB, about 0.94 GB together before a\ntensor-parallel split, and that is one layer's transient, reused layer after layer rather than summed.\nThe LM head is the other trap: run on every prompt position it would produce 8,192 × 128k × 4 bytes ≈\n4.2 GB of fp32 logits, which is why engines compute logits only at the positions they sample from. Sampling needs a logits buffer of [batch, vocab] in fp32: 256 sequences ×\n128k vocabulary × 4 bytes is about 131 MB, small but real. Engines estimate this line by running a dummy\nforward pass at the maximum batch and token budget during startup, which is why those limits change the\nreported KV capacity.\n\n### Line 4: runtime overhead and fragmentation\nThe CUDA context, cuBLAS workspaces, NCCL buffers for tensor parallelism and CUDA-graph captures take a\nfew hundred megabytes to a couple of gigabytes as an illustrative range, per process, which is why two\nprocesses on one GPU waste more than one. PyTorch's caching allocator holds freed blocks in reserve, so\n`memory_reserved()` exceeds `memory_allocated()`, and variable-size allocations fragment that reserve\nover time; the `expandable_segments` allocator option in recent PyTorch versions reduces it. Serving\nengines sidestep most fragmentation by pre-allocating the KV pool in fixed blocks and by profiling the\nactivation peak once, which is the design logic of PagedAttention applied to the whole budget.\n\n### Putting the ledger together\n```\nusable          = VRAM × memory_fraction          (engine budget, e.g. 0.9)\npool_for_kv     = usable − weights − activation_peak − overhead\ntokens_capacity = pool_for_kv / bytes_per_token\nconcurrency     = tokens_capacity / expected_tokens_per_sequence\n```\nRun it per GPU under tensor parallelism, with weights and KV divided by the TP degree but overhead not.\nThen check the result against the latency target, because a capacity of 60 concurrent sequences is\nuseless if the step time at 60 breaks the per-token SLO.\n\n### Reading the instruments\n`torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`, `memory_reserved()` and `max_memory_allocated()` separate live\ntensors, allocator reserve and the true peak; `torch.cuda.memory_summary()` shows fragmentation.\nEngines log the number of KV blocks they could allocate at startup, which is the single most useful\nnumber to compare against the ledger. `nvidia-smi` is for spotting unexpected processes and for the\ntotal, not for attribution.","example":"A Llama-3-70B-class model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, hidden 8,192, about 140 GB in bf16) on\ntwo 80 GB GPUs with tensor parallelism of 2 and a 0.9 memory fraction, so 72 GB usable per GPU.\n\nIn bf16, weights are about 70.6 GB per GPU against a 72 GB budget: after the activation reserve there\nis essentially nothing left for cache (a few GB at best, depending on how strictly you count GB versus\nGiB), so not a useful deployment. Quantize the weights to int8: about 35.3 GB per GPU. KV per token is 2 × 80 × 8 × 128\n× 2 = 320 KB in fp16, or 160 KB per GPU after sharding. Reserve an illustrative 2 GB for activation peak\nand overhead: 72 − 35.3 − 2 ≈ 34.7 GB for the pool, which is 34.7 × 2³⁰ ÷ 160 KB ≈ 227,000 tokens. At an\nexpected 8k tokens per conversation that is about 27 concurrent conversations. Switching the KV cache to\nfp8 halves bytes per token and doubles that to about 55; capping context at 4k doubles it again to about\n111. Each step of the plan is one term of the ledger, and the calculator below reproduces it so the\nnumbers can be re-run for any model. (It blurs GB and GiB, a 7% difference that is smaller than the\nuncertainty in the reserve; when the margin is that thin, use the engine's logged block count instead.)\n\n```python\ndef vram_plan(vram_gb, params_b, w_bytes, layers, kv_heads, head_dim, kv_bytes,\n              tp=1, fraction=0.9, reserve_gb=2.0, tokens_per_seq=8192, vocab=128_256, hidden=8192):\n    usable = vram_gb * fraction\n    weights = params_b * w_bytes / tp                       # GB per GPU (1e9 params ~ 1 GB at 1 byte)\n    kv_per_token = 2 * layers * kv_heads * head_dim * kv_bytes / tp\n    pool_gb = usable - weights - reserve_gb\n    tokens = int(pool_gb * 2**30 / kv_per_token) if pool_gb > 0 else 0\n    return dict(weights_gb=round(weights, 1), kv_kb_per_token=kv_per_token / 1024,\n                pool_gb=round(pool_gb, 1), tokens=tokens, concurrent=tokens // tokens_per_seq)\n\nbase = dict(vram_gb=80, params_b=70.6, layers=80, kv_heads=8, head_dim=128, tp=2)\nprint(\"bf16 weights, fp16 KV:\", vram_plan(w_bytes=2, kv_bytes=2, **base))\nprint(\"int8 weights, fp16 KV:\", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=2, **base))\nprint(\"int8 weights, fp8 KV: \", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=1, **base))\nprint(\"int8, fp8 KV, 4k ctx: \", vram_plan(w_bytes=1, kv_bytes=1, tokens_per_seq=4096, **base))\n```","tradeoffs":["Quantizing weights frees the largest line and speeds decode, but every quantized artifact needs its own quality evaluation; the saving is not worth a silent regression on the task.","An fp8 KV cache doubles token capacity at low reported quality cost, yet long-context retrieval is where cache precision bites, so the trade flips as context lengths grow.","A higher memory fraction gives more blocks, but shrinks the headroom for activation peaks and other processes; past about 0.9–0.95 the OOM risk rises faster than capacity.","Lowering the context cap raises concurrency proportionally but rejects or truncates legitimate long requests; the right cap is the measured p99 of real traffic, not the model's maximum.","Tensor parallelism divides weights and cache across GPUs, but adds per-GPU overhead and interconnect cost, so two GPUs give less than twice the capacity of one."],"pitfalls":["Sizing from weights alone. The symptom is a server that starts cleanly, reports healthy, and OOMs or preempts heavily the first time concurrency and context length rise together.","Reading `nvidia-smi` as \"memory the model needs\"; it includes the allocator's reserve and the pre-allocated KV pool, so it reports near-full even at idle for a serving engine.","Running a second process (an embedding model, a reranker) on the same GPU without re-profiling; the engine's pool was sized to the free memory at its own startup, and the newcomer OOMs or evicts it.","Testing with short prompts and small batches so the activation peak is never exercised; the first 32k-token prompt during a full decode batch triggers the OOM that profiling would have predicted.","Treating a mixture-of-experts model as its active-parameter size when budgeting weights, then finding the checkpoint does not load."],"when":"Do the ledger before every deployment, every model swap, every change to context or concurrency limits,\nand every time a new co-tenant lands on a GPU. Use it to pick between levers in a fixed order: a model\nwith fewer KV heads or a quantized weight format first, then context and concurrency caps matched to the\ntraffic p99, then fp8 cache, then tensor parallelism, with CPU offloading as a last resort for\nnon-interactive work. Measure the engine's reported block count and peak allocated memory under a\nproduction-shaped load test and compare them with the ledger; a gap means a term you forgot. In\ninterviews, deriving bytes-per-token and walking the four lines for a named model on a named GPU is the\nstandard test of serving depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Computes the ledger in a notebook, sets `gpu_memory_utilization`, `max_model_len` and sequence limits from it, and verifies against the engine's logged block count and peak memory.","architect":"Chooses GPU tier, precision policy and tensor-parallel degree per model from the ledger, and sets the context and concurrency policy that capacity planning depends on.","fde":"Sizes a customer's self-hosted deployment on the hardware they have, showing the four lines and which lever changes their concurrent-user number before any purchase decision."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants to serve a Llama-3-8B-class model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, ~16 GB\nin bf16) on a single 24 GB GPU with 16k-token documents and asks how many users it supports. Walk the\nledger.","a":"Usable memory at a 0.9 fraction is about 21.6 GB. Weights take 16 GB, and an illustrative 1.5 GB\ngoes to activation peak and runtime overhead, leaving roughly 4 GB for KV cache. KV per token is 2 × 32 ×\n8 × 128 × 2 = 128 KB in fp16, so the pool holds around 32k tokens: two 16k documents in flight, not a\nmulti-user service. The levers in order: quantize weights to int8 (8 GB freed, about 96k tokens, six\ndocuments) or 4-bit (another 4 GB, roughly 130k tokens), use an fp8 KV cache to double any of those,\nenable chunked prefill so a 16k prefill does not blow the activation peak, and confirm the step time at\nthe resulting concurrency still meets their latency target. If they need dozens of concurrent documents,\nthe answer is a larger GPU, not more tuning."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which term of the memory ledger grows with traffic rather than with the model?","options":["Weights, because each request loads its own copy of the parameters","The KV cache, because it scales with tokens in flight across all active sequences","Runtime overhead, because the CUDA context grows with each connection","The embedding table, because new tokens are added as prompts arrive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Weights and overhead are fixed once the model is loaded. The cache is proportional to the total number of prompt and generated tokens currently being served, which is a property of traffic you must cap."},{"q":"Why does a serving engine run a dummy forward pass at startup before allocating its KV pool?","options":["To warm up the CUDA kernels so the first request is fast","To verify the model weights loaded correctly","To measure the peak activation memory at the configured batch and token limits, so the pool can take the rest","To compute the perplexity of the model on a calibration set"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Activation and workspace memory depends on the maximum step the scheduler may build. Measuring it once lets the engine give every remaining byte to KV blocks without risking OOM on the largest step."},{"q":"A 47B-total, 13B-active mixture-of-experts model is planned for a GPU budget sized from 13B parameters. What happens?","options":["It fits, because only active experts are loaded per token","It fits if the KV cache is quantized to fp8","It fails to load, because all experts must be resident and weights cost 47B × bytes per parameter","It loads but runs at one-quarter speed due to expert swapping"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Routing picks experts per token, but every expert can be chosen, so the full set must sit in GPU memory. Active parameters predict compute per token, not the weight footprint."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/streaming","sources":["Official docs: WHATWG HTML Living Standard, server-sent events","Official docs: OpenAI API streaming","Official docs: Anthropic Messages API streaming"],"why":"Autoregressive decoding produces output one token at a time, and the total time is roughly\n`TTFT + (n_tokens − 1) × TPOT`. For a chat answer the second term dominates: a few hundred tokens at\ntens of milliseconds each is several seconds of pure waiting in which the server already has most of\nthe answer and the user sees nothing. People judge responsiveness by the first visible change, not by\ncompletion, so a non-streaming UI feels broken at exactly the output lengths that make LLMs useful.\n\nThe naive fixes all fail somewhere. Polling a job endpoint adds a round trip per poll, gives latency\ngranularity equal to the poll interval and multiplies request volume. Returning a raw chunked\n`text/plain` body gets tokens out early but has no framing: the client cannot tell a token from a\nmid-stream error, a tool-call argument fragment or the final usage record. WebSockets give full\nduplex and framing, but they need a protocol upgrade, long-lived connection state, their own\nreconnect logic and are the thing most likely to be blocked or mishandled by a corporate proxy, all\nfor a channel where only one side talks.\n\nSSE sits in the gap: ordinary HTTP response, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, a trivial line-based\nframing with named events, and native browser support. That is why OpenAI- and Anthropic-style APIs\nstream over it, and why every serving stack between the engine and the screen has to carry it\nwithout buffering.","how":"### The wire format\nAn SSE body is UTF-8 text made of events separated by a blank line. Each line is `field: value`.\nThe fields that matter are `data:` (the payload; several `data:` lines in one event are joined with\nnewlines), `event:` (an optional name the client can dispatch on), `id:` (lets a reconnecting client\nsend `Last-Event-ID`) and `retry:` (reconnect delay hint). A line starting with `:` is a comment; the\nclient ignores it, which makes `: keep-alive` a free heartbeat. The response stays open; under\nHTTP/1.1 it is sent with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, under HTTP/2 as an open stream. The framing is\ndefined by the blank line, not by network packets: one TCP read can hold half an event or three of\nthem, and can even split a multi-byte UTF-8 character, so a client must decode bytes incrementally,\nbuffer until it sees the blank line, and only then parse the event. A token stream is just:\n\n```\nevent: delta\ndata: {\"text\": \"The\"}\n\nevent: delta\ndata: {\"text\": \" KV\"}\n\nevent: done\ndata: {\"usage\": {\"output_tokens\": 2}}\n\n```\n\n### What flows through it\nProviders map generation onto events differently but the shape is the same. OpenAI-style chat\ncompletions send a sequence of `data:` JSON chunks, each carrying a `delta` (typically a first chunk\nwith only the role and empty content, then content fragments), and end with a literal\n`data: [DONE]` sentinel; token usage only arrives in a final chunk if you ask for it\n(`stream_options: {\"include_usage\": true}`). Anthropic's Messages API uses named events:\n`message_start` (with input-token usage), `content_block_start`, repeated `content_block_delta`\n(`text_delta` or, for tool use, `input_json_delta` carrying partial JSON), `content_block_stop`,\n`message_delta` (stop reason, output-token usage) and `message_stop`, plus periodic `ping` events.\nTwo consequences follow. Tool-call arguments arrive as fragments of JSON that are only parseable at\nthe end of the block. And the authoritative token count is at the tail of the stream, so a client\nthat disconnects early never sees it.\n\n### The path from GPU to screen\nThe engine (vLLM, SGLang, TGI, a provider's fleet) emits one token per decode step per sequence.\nThe serving layer serialises it as an event and *flushes* it; a framework that buffers generator\noutput defeats the whole point. Then it crosses every intermediary: your own reverse proxy, an API\ngateway, a load balancer, maybe a CDN, then the client's HTTP library. Each one is allowed by HTTP\nto buffer a response body, and several do by default (nginx `proxy_buffering`, response compression\nlayers that wait for a full block). The fixes are per hop: `X-Accel-Buffering: no` and\n`Cache-Control: no-cache` headers, disabling compression for `text/event-stream`, and heartbeats so\nidle-connection timers on load balancers (commonly around a minute) do not close a stream during a\nlong prefill or a reasoning pause.\n\n### Semantics that change once you stream\nThe status code is sent before generation starts, so a `200` means \"the stream opened\", not \"the\nanswer succeeded\". Mid-stream failures (model error, content filter stop, upstream timeout) must\ntravel in-band as an `error` event or a `finish_reason`, and clients must treat the output they have\nas partial. Streams are not resumable: the browser `EventSource` API will auto-reconnect and send\n`Last-Event-ID`, but an LLM server cannot replay generation from an id, so a dropped stream is a\ncancel-and-restart, not a resume. And `EventSource` only issues `GET` requests without custom\nheaders, so real clients consume the provider's `POST` stream with `fetch` and a `ReadableStream`\nreader, which is also where per-origin connection limits on HTTP/1.1 (six in most browsers) bite if\nevery tab holds a stream open.","example":"A minimal SSE endpoint that forwards an engine's tokens with correct framing, a connect comment that\nforces intermediaries to commit to the response, heartbeats on idle, in-band errors and a stop on\nclient disconnect. The token source is a stand-in for an engine or provider stream.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef sse(event: str, data: dict) -> str:\n    return f\"event: {event}\\ndata: {json.dumps(data)}\\n\\n\"      # blank line terminates the event\n\nasync def token_source(prompt: str):                             # stand-in for an engine stream\n    for i, tok in enumerate((\"Stream\", \"ing\", \" works\", \".\")):\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.3); yield i, tok\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/answer\")\nasync def answer(req: Request):\n    body = await req.json()\n    q: asyncio.Queue = asyncio.Queue()\n\n    async def pump():                                            # engine -> queue, errors in-band\n        n = 0\n        try:\n            async for i, tok in token_source(body[\"prompt\"]):\n                await q.put((\"delta\", {\"i\": i, \"text\": tok})); n += 1\n            await q.put((\"done\", {\"output_tokens\": n}))\n        except Exception as e:\n            await q.put((\"error\", {\"message\": str(e)}))          # status is already 200\n\n    async def gen():\n        task = asyncio.create_task(pump())\n        yield \": connected\\n\\n\"                                  # comment: flushes proxies, no client event\n        try:\n            while True:\n                try:\n                    ev, data = await asyncio.wait_for(q.get(), timeout=15)\n                except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n                    yield \": keep-alive\\n\\n\"; continue           # heartbeat beats idle timeouts\n                if await req.is_disconnected(): break            # client left: stop forwarding\n                yield sse(ev, data)\n                if ev in (\"done\", \"error\"): break\n        finally:\n            task.cancel()                                        # propagate the stop upstream\n\n    return StreamingResponse(gen(), media_type=\"text/event-stream\",\n        headers={\"Cache-Control\": \"no-cache\", \"X-Accel-Buffering\": \"no\"})\n```","tradeoffs":["Perceived latency drops to TTFT, but total completion time does not improve and may rise slightly from per-token serialisation and small TCP writes; the gain disappears when the consumer is a machine that needs the whole JSON anyway, although early cancellation and idle-timeout liveness still argue for streaming there.","SSE is simple and proxy-friendly because it is one-directional; the moment the client must talk mid-stream (interrupt, barge-in voice, collaborative edits) you need WebSockets or a second request channel.","Coalescing several tokens into one event every few tens of milliseconds cuts frame and render overhead on busy servers, at the cost of visibly choppier output at low concurrency.","Structured output cannot be validated until the final event, so a streaming JSON response either needs an incremental parser or loses the latency benefit for machine consumers.","Streaming exposes TTFT and inter-token gaps to measurement, which is valuable, but pushes usage and cost data to the end of the stream where disconnects can lose it."],"pitfalls":["A proxy or middleware buffers the body; the symptom is a stream that works on localhost and arrives all at once in production after the full generation time.","Using the browser `EventSource` against an authenticated `POST` endpoint; the symptom is 401 or 405 errors because `EventSource` can only `GET` without custom headers.","No heartbeats during long prefill or thinking pauses; the symptom is streams dying at a fixed interval (often exactly 60 s) with no error from either end.","Treating the `200` as success and ignoring in-band `error` events or stop reasons; the symptom is truncated answers logged as successes and never retried.","Rendering each delta directly into Markdown; the symptom is flickering, half-open code fences and broken links until the closing token arrives.","Counting SSE chunks as tokens; servers may coalesce or split, so the symptom is usage numbers that disagree with the provider's bill.","Hand-rolled clients that treat each network read as one event; the symptom is intermittent JSON parse errors or garbled non-ASCII characters that appear only on slow or distant networks, where reads split events differently than on localhost.","Forgetting that a disconnected client does not stop generation unless you propagate the stop; the symptom is GPU time and provider spend that stay flat when traffic drops."],"when":"Stream by default for anything a human reads that is longer than a sentence, and for long-running\nmachine calls where early cancellation and liveness matter (agent steps, tool-argument generation).\nDo not bother for short classification, extraction or embedding calls, or for batch pipelines that\nvalidate the complete output anyway. Measure TTFT, the distribution of inter-event gaps, stream\ncompletion rate and disconnect rate, and alert on \"all events arrived within the last 100 ms of the\nresponse\", which is the buffering signature. In interviews, be able to write the SSE framing from\nmemory, explain why not WebSockets, name the buffering layers, and describe how error semantics\nchange once the status code has already been sent.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the generator and client parser, sets the anti-buffering headers and heartbeats, and handles partial JSON for streamed tool calls.","architect":"Decides where streaming terminates (gateway vs service), which hops are allowed to buffer, and whether interactive and batch traffic share the same path.","fde":"Debugs \"it streams in dev but not behind the customer's proxy\", usually a buffering or idle-timeout setting on infrastructure the customer owns."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that your streaming endpoint \"works on localhost but does not stream in\nproduction, the whole answer just appears after ten seconds\". How do you diagnose it, in what order?","a":"First confirm the server really flushes per token by hitting the service directly with\n`curl -N` from inside the environment; if tokens trickle there, the server is fine. Then walk the\nhops outward: the reverse proxy (nginx buffers upstream responses by default; set\n`X-Accel-Buffering: no` or disable `proxy_buffering`), any compression middleware or gateway that\nwaits for a full block before emitting, a CDN or WAF that caches or inspects bodies, and the load\nbalancer's idle timeout. Finally check the client: awaiting the whole response body instead of\nreading the stream reproduces the exact symptom. Verify by sending a `: connected` comment first; if\neven that arrives late, the problem is an intermediary, not the model."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is SSE preferred over WebSockets for streaming LLM tokens to a browser?","options":["SSE delivers each message with lower latency than a WebSocket frame","Token output is one-directional, and SSE gives framing over ordinary HTTP that proxies, gateways and provider APIs already handle","WebSockets cannot carry JSON payloads reliably","SSE supports binary token ids while WebSockets are text only"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SSE is not faster per message; it wins because it is plain HTTP for a one-way channel, so it needs no protocol upgrade or connection state and survives intermediaries. WebSockets are the right tool only when the client must send data mid-stream."},{"q":"A streamed response returned HTTP 200, emitted 300 tokens, then the model errored. What must the client do?","options":["Nothing special; the 200 status already indicates the request succeeded","Automatically retry and append the new output to the 300 tokens it has","Handle the in-band error event or finish reason and treat the 300 tokens as a partial, failed result","Wait for the `[DONE]` sentinel, which is always sent after errors"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once streaming starts the status code is fixed, so failures can only be signalled inside the stream. Appending a retry's output would duplicate content; the partial answer must be marked incomplete and the retry started from scratch."},{"q":"Every token of a stream arrives at once, exactly when generation finishes. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Time-to-first-token is unusually high because of a long prompt","An intermediary or middleware is buffering the response body until it completes","The model does not support streaming so the server falls back to a single response","The client negotiated HTTP/2, which does not allow chunked transfer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Buffering is the classic signature: generation ran at normal speed but a proxy, compression layer or the client's own body reader held the bytes. High TTFT would delay the first token, not bunch all of them, and HTTP/2 streams SSE fine without chunked encoding."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/ttft","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized Large Language Model Serving"],"why":"\"Request latency\" is a useless number for an LLM call because it mixes two phases with different\nphysics. Prefill processes every prompt token in one batched forward pass; it is dominated by large\nmatrix multiplications and scales with prompt length. Decode then produces tokens one at a time,\neach step bound by memory bandwidth and scaling with output length. If you only track end-to-end\nlatency, a regression could be either phase and you cannot tell which lever to pull.\n\nTTFT isolates the first phase. It matters on its own because once a stream has started the user has\nsomething to read and tolerates the rest; before it starts they see nothing and assume the product is\nbroken. The same request can have a perfectly acceptable total time and an unacceptable TTFT if the\nfirst second is spent queueing or chewing through a 20k-token retrieved context.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"just watch mean latency\", fails twice. Means hide the queueing tail, which is\nwhere TTFT breaks first under load. And a team that measured TTFT with 200-token test prompts will\nship a RAG feature that stuffs 8k tokens into the context and never understand why the product\nsuddenly feels slow: the answer is in the prefill arithmetic, which this metric forces you to look at.","how":"### What is inside the number\nFrom the client's clock, TTFT is the sum of: network to the server (plus TCP and TLS handshakes,\nseveral round trips, unless the client reuses a pooled connection); time spent waiting for\nadmission (a free slot in the batch and free KV-cache memory); tokenisation; the prefill forward\npass over all prompt tokens; sampling the first token from the logits of the last prompt position\n(the first token falls out of the prefill pass itself, there is no separate decode step for it);\nserialisation and the first flush of the response; and network back. Only two of those terms are\nlarge and variable: the queue and prefill.\n\n### Prefill cost\nFor a dense transformer with *N* parameters, the forward pass costs roughly `2 × N` FLOPs per token\nfor the weight matrices, plus an attention term that grows with the square of the prompt length but\nis small until contexts get very long. So prefill FLOPs ≈ `2 × N × n_prompt`. Illustrative\narithmetic: an 8-billion-parameter model on a 4,000-token prompt needs about 2 × 8e9 × 4e3 = 6.4e13\nFLOPs. An H100-class GPU has a dense bf16 peak around 1e15 FLOP/s; at a realistic 40–50 % utilisation\nthat is roughly 130–160 ms of pure prefill. Double the prompt and you double it. A 70B model is\nnearly nine times more. This is why TTFT tracks prompt length almost linearly in practice and why\n\"we added RAG and TTFT went from 300 ms to 2 s\" is expected, not a bug.\n\n### Queueing under continuous batching\nA continuous-batching engine admits a new request only when it has a scheduling slot and enough KV\nmemory for its prompt. When either is exhausted, the request waits for others to finish or be\npreempted, and that wait lands entirely in TTFT. So TTFT p99 is a capacity signal: if it climbs\nwhile prompt lengths have not changed, you are queueing. Prefill also interacts with running\nrequests: a long prompt entering the batch makes that step expensive, which stalls every other\nstream's next token. Chunked prefill splits a long prompt across several steps to protect those\nstreams, at the cost of slightly longer TTFT for the long request itself. Disaggregated serving goes\nfurther and runs prefill on separate GPUs so decode latency and TTFT stop competing at all, paying\nwith a KV-cache transfer between machines.\n\n### Levers, in order of leverage\n1. **Send fewer tokens.** Tighter retrieval (fewer, shorter chunks), summarised history, and no\n   boilerplate. Linear payoff.\n2. **Prefix caching.** If requests share a byte-identical prefix (system prompt, tool schemas, a\n   document in a multi-turn chat), the engine reuses its KV cache and only prefills the suffix.\n   Dynamic content at the top of the prompt (timestamps, user names) breaks the match.\n3. **Admission control and capacity.** Bound concurrency per replica so requests fail fast or queue\n   visibly instead of silently inflating TTFT; scale on queue depth, not GPU utilisation.\n4. **Scheduling.** Chunked prefill, priority classes for interactive traffic, separate pools for\n   long-context jobs.\n5. **Hardware and parallelism.** Prefill is compute-bound, so a faster GPU or tensor parallelism\n   across GPUs does shorten it, unlike decode. Speculative decoding, KV-cache quantisation and\n   weight-only quantisation mostly do not; they target the decode phase.\n\n### Measuring it honestly\nMeasure at the client from \"request sent\" to the first *non-empty content* delta; the first SSE\nframe is often a role-only chunk or a `message_start` event and arrives before any prefill has\nfinished. Report percentiles bucketed by prompt length, and keep a separate \"time to first visible\ntoken\" when a model emits hidden reasoning before its answer, because that gap can be many seconds\nwhile TTFT looks fine. Server-side TTFT, measured from arrival, strips out network and is the number\nto compare across engine configurations.","example":"A short probe against any OpenAI-compatible streaming server (a provider, or vLLM/SGLang locally)\nthat measures TTFT to the first content delta and TPOT over the rest, then shows how TTFT grows with\nprompt length while TPOT stays flat.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")  # any OpenAI-compatible server\nMODEL = \"my-model\"\n\ndef measure(prompt: str, max_tokens: int = 200):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); t_first = t_last = None; chunks = 0; usage_tokens = None\n    stream = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        max_tokens=max_tokens, stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    for chunk in stream:\n        if chunk.usage:                                      # final usage-only chunk, if the server sends one\n            usage_tokens = chunk.usage.completion_tokens\n        if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:  # skip the empty role-only first chunk\n            now = time.perf_counter()\n            t_first = t_first or now\n            t_last = now; chunks += 1\n    n = usage_tokens or chunks                               # chunks ~ tokens only as a fallback\n    ttft = t_first - t0\n    tpot = (t_last - t_first) / max(n - 1, 1)\n    return ttft, tpot, n\n\nfor words in (50, 500, 5000):\n    ttft, tpot, n = measure(\"Summarise this text: \" + \"word \" * words)\n    print(f\"prompt~{words:5d} words  TTFT={ttft*1000:6.0f} ms  TPOT={tpot*1000:5.1f} ms  ({n} out tokens)\")\n# Expect TTFT to rise roughly linearly with prompt length (prefill) and TPOT to stay nearly constant.\n# Run it again with 20 concurrent copies: TTFT climbs (queueing) long before TPOT does.\n```","tradeoffs":["Prefix caching slashes TTFT for repeated prefixes but only when they are byte-identical and still resident; it buys nothing for one-off prompts or when dynamic fields sit before the shared part.","Chunked prefill protects the inter-token latency of running streams but lengthens TTFT for the long prompt being chunked; the balance flips for prefill-heavy workloads with few concurrent decodes, such as summarisation batches, where you would rather prefill at full speed.","Prioritising short prompts improves median TTFT at the risk of starving long ones; it only works with ageing or a separate pool for long-context requests.","Trimming retrieved context lowers TTFT linearly but can cut answer quality, so the cut must be checked against an eval set rather than assumed harmless.","Disaggregating prefill and decode isolates TTFT from decode load, but adds machines and a KV-cache transfer whose cost is only worth it at high, steady traffic."],"pitfalls":["Measuring to the first SSE frame rather than the first content token; the symptom is a TTFT that looks great and does not move when prompt length triples.","Reporting mean TTFT; the symptom is a green dashboard while users complain, because queueing shows up only in p95 and p99.","Blaming the model for high TTFT when it is queueing; the tell is that TTFT correlates with concurrency, not with prompt length, and drops at night.","Load-testing with short prompts and shipping long retrieved contexts; the symptom is a product that \"got slow when we added RAG\" with no change in the serving stack.","Ignoring hidden reasoning tokens; TTFT is fine but the user sees a blank screen for ten seconds, so measure time to first visible token separately.","Opening a new HTTPS connection per request from a distant region; the symptom is a TTFT floor of a few hundred milliseconds even for tiny prompts, which disappears with a reused client.","Counting cold starts (model load on a fresh replica, serverless GPU wake-up) inside TTFT; the symptom is a p99 dominated by a handful of 30-second outliers after each scale-out."],"when":"Give every user-facing surface a TTFT target before launch: a chat UI usually aims for well under a\nsecond at p95, inline completion needs a few hundred milliseconds, and a nightly batch job has no\nTTFT target at all. Budget it as network + queue + prefill and check which term you can actually\ncontrol. Watch it bucketed by prompt length and by concurrency so you can tell a prompt-size\nregression from a capacity problem. In day-to-day work it is the first number to look at when\n\"the assistant feels slow\", and in interviews the expected answer to \"latency doubled after we added\nretrieval\" is a decomposition of TTFT into prefill arithmetic plus the levers above.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments TTFT to the first content delta, enables prefix caching for the shared system prompt, and trims retrieved context when the prefill term dominates.","architect":"Sets per-surface TTFT SLOs, decides concurrency limits and admission policy, and chooses whether long-context traffic gets its own pool or disaggregated prefill.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their 200-page-document prompt costs seconds before the first word, and offers retrieval or chunking as the fix rather than a bigger GPU alone."},"check":{"q":"Your service's p50 TTFT is 400 ms but p99 is 9 s. The model, prompts and hardware have not\nchanged in a month. What is happening, how do you confirm it, and what do you change?","a":"A gap that large with stable prompts is queueing: requests waiting for a batch slot or KV\nmemory, which lands entirely in TTFT. Confirm by plotting TTFT against concurrent requests and\nengine queue depth; the p99 should rise with load and collapse off-peak, and the engine's\npreemption or waiting-queue metrics should spike at the same times. Prompt-length buckets rule out\na few long prompts as the cause. Fixes, cheapest first: cap per-replica concurrency and return a fast\n429 or visible queue position instead of a silent 9 s wait; enable prefix caching if prompts share a\nsystem prefix; turn on chunked prefill so long prompts stop stalling admission; autoscale on queue\ndepth rather than GPU utilisation; and move long-context or batch traffic to a separate pool."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change most directly reduces TTFT for requests with a 10k-token prompt?","options":["Enabling speculative decoding with a small draft model","Quantising the KV cache to 8-bit","Caching the KV state of the shared prompt prefix so only the suffix is prefilled","Raising `max_tokens` so the engine schedules the request sooner"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TTFT is dominated by prefill over the prompt; prefix caching removes most of that work when the prefix repeats. Speculative decoding and KV quantisation target the decode phase, and `max_tokens` affects output length, not the time to the first token."},{"q":"Holding hardware and model fixed, TTFT scales primarily with which two quantities?","options":["Output length and sampling temperature","Prompt length and time spent waiting for admission to the batch","Number of KV heads and vocabulary size","Batch size of the decode phase and the stop sequence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Prefill cost is roughly linear in prompt tokens, and any wait for a scheduling slot or KV memory is added on top. Output length only affects total time after the first token, and temperature changes which token is sampled, not how long it takes."},{"q":"Which measurement gives a correct client-side TTFT for a streamed chat completion?","options":["Time until the HTTP response headers arrive","Time until the first SSE frame of any kind is received","Time until the first delta containing non-empty content is received","Time until the final usage chunk divided by the number of tokens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Headers and the first frame (often a role-only chunk or `message_start`) are sent before prefill completes, so they under-report. The first content delta is the first evidence that generation has produced a token."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tpot","sources":["Pope et al., 2022 — Efficiently Scaling Transformer Inference","Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Leviathan et al., 2023 — Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding"],"why":"After the first token appears, total latency is `TTFT + (n_out − 1) × TPOT`, and for any answer longer\nthan a few sentences the second term wins. A 600-token answer at 50 ms per token is 30 s; at 15 ms it\nis 9 s. The user's experience of \"is this thing fast\" after the first word, and an agent's total\ntime across a dozen chained calls, are both governed by this one number.\n\nIt needs to be its own metric because decode has different physics from prefill. Prefill is one big\nmatrix multiplication per layer and is compute-bound. Decode processes a single new token per\nsequence, so the arithmetic per step is tiny while the model's entire weight set still has to be\nstreamed from HBM into the compute units every step. The step time is therefore set by bytes moved,\nnot by FLOPs, and the levers that help it (fewer bytes per step, more bandwidth, more tokens per step)\nare not the levers that help TTFT.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"report tokens per second\", is ambiguous between one stream and the whole\nserver, and hides stalls: a stream averaging 20 ms per token with a 400 ms freeze every few seconds\nlooks fine as a mean and feels broken. TPOT alongside its distribution (inter-token latency, ITL)\nmakes the difference visible and tells you which engine behaviour caused it.","how":"### Definition and its sibling\nTPOT is a per-request mean: total decode time divided by the number of gaps. Inter-token latency\n(ITL) is the distribution of the individual gaps; its p99 catches the stalls that the mean hides.\nBoth exclude TTFT on purpose, so a long prompt does not pollute the decode measurement. Tools are\nnot consistent about the names: some dashboards call the per-request mean \"ITL\", and some quote a\n\"latency per token\" of `end_to_end / n_out`, which silently folds TTFT back in. Check the formula\nbefore comparing two reports.\n\n### The bandwidth floor\nIn one decode step for a batch of *B* sequences, the GPU reads every weight matrix once (the same\nweights serve all *B* tokens) and, for attention, reads the KV cache of every sequence. Ignoring\ncompute, the step cannot be faster than:\n\n```\nstep_time ≥ (weight_bytes + Σ kv_bytes_per_sequence) / memory_bandwidth\n```\n\nIllustrative numbers: an 8B-parameter model in fp16 is about 16 GB of weights. An H100 SXM moves\nroughly 3.35 TB/s from HBM. At batch 1 with a short context, the floor is 16 / 3350 ≈ 4.8 ms per\ntoken, i.e. a ceiling of about 210 tokens per second for a single stream. Real systems land below\nthat ceiling because of kernel launch overhead, imperfect attention kernels, sampling and the host\nloop, but the ceiling is the right mental model: no amount of extra FLOPs lowers it. A GPU with\nsimilar bandwidth and double the FLOPs gives nearly the same TPOT at low batch.\n\n### Why batching is nearly free\nThe weight read is paid once per step regardless of *B*, so going from 1 to 16 concurrent sequences\nmultiplies the arithmetic by 16 but adds only the KV-cache reads to the bytes moved. Per-request\nTPOT rises gently while aggregate throughput rises almost linearly. This continues until one of two\nthings happens: the arithmetic finally catches up with the bandwidth, or the KV cache of all the\nsequences no longer fits in memory. The first crossover can be estimated. Counting only the weight\nmatmuls, a step does `2 × N × B` FLOPs while reading `N × bytes_per_param` bytes, so arithmetic\nintensity is `2B / bytes_per_param` FLOPs per byte. The GPU becomes compute-bound when that reaches\nits ratio of peak FLOP/s to bandwidth, roughly 1e15 / 3.35e12 ≈ 300 for an H100 in bf16. With fp16\nweights that is a batch near 300, independent of model size; with int8 weights it halves, because\neach byte read now feeds twice the arithmetic. KV reads add bytes without much arithmetic, which\npushes the real crossover later still. Long contexts pull that second limit in fast, because KV bytes scale with\n`B × context_length`, and at long contexts the KV reads, not the weights, dominate the step.\n\n### Levers that move TPOT\n- **Fewer weight bytes.** Weight-only int8 or int4 quantisation cuts the bytes read per step by 2–4×,\n  which is a direct TPOT win at low batch; at high batch, where compute starts to bind, the gain\n  shrinks and dequantisation cost shows.\n- **Fewer KV bytes.** Grouped-query attention, KV-cache quantisation and shorter contexts reduce the\n  per-sequence term, which matters most for long-context, high-concurrency serving.\n- **More bandwidth per model.** Tensor parallelism splits the weights across *k* GPUs so each reads\n  `1/k` of them per step; the all-reduce after each layer adds a fixed communication cost, so it\n  helps most on NVLink-connected GPUs and large models.\n- **More tokens per step.** Speculative decoding drafts several tokens cheaply and verifies them in one\n  forward pass of the big model, so a step that costs one weight read can yield two or three accepted\n  tokens. Effective TPOT falls at low batch; at high batch the wasted verification compute can lower\n  throughput instead. It also changes the shape of ITL: accepted tokens arrive in bursts of several\n  per step, so the gap distribution becomes lumpy (near-zero gaps inside a burst, a full step between\n  bursts) even when the mean improves.\n- **A smaller model.** Halving parameters roughly halves bytes per step; whether quality survives is\n  an eval question, not a serving one.\n\n### Where the stalls come from\nIn continuous batching, every running stream advances one token per engine step. When a new request\nis admitted, its prefill runs inside that step; a 10k-token prefill can take hundreds of\nmilliseconds, during which every other stream emits nothing. The result is a flat 25 ms ITL punctuated\nby spikes, which is a scheduling artefact, not a model property. Chunked prefill caps the prefill\nwork per step to bound the stall. Preemption is the other source: when KV memory runs out the engine\nevicts a sequence and later recomputes or swaps its cache back, and that sequence's ITL shows a\nmulti-second gap.\n\n### How fast is fast enough\nAdults read prose at roughly 200–300 words per minute, about 4–5 words per second, which at the\nusual 1.3 tokens per English word is around 5–7 tokens per second, so a TPOT near 150–200 ms\nalready keeps pace with a reader for plain chat. Products still aim much lower because users skim, scroll\nand want code to appear in blocks, and because agentic workloads consume tokens by machine: a\nplanner that makes eight sequential calls of 300 tokens each turns a 30 ms TPOT into over a minute\nof waiting before anyone reads a word.","example":"A worked scenario using the bandwidth floor, so you can predict TPOT before buying hardware. Take a\nLlama-3-8B-style model: 8B parameters in fp16 (16 GB of weights), 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head\ndimension 128. KV cache per token is `2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 131,072 bytes`, i.e. 128 KB. Put\nit on an H100 SXM with about 3.35 TB/s of HBM bandwidth and ignore compute, which is safe here\nbecause even at batch 100 the decode FLOPs per step (`2 × 8e9 × 100 = 1.6e12`) take only about\n3 ms at half of the roughly 1e15 FLOP/s peak, well under the bandwidth time at that batch.\n\nBatch 1, 4k-token context: bytes per step = 16 GB + 4,000 × 128 KB ≈ 16.5 GB → 16.5 / 3350 ≈ 4.9 ms →\nabout 200 tokens per second for that one user.\n\nBatch 32, each at 4k context: KV reads = 32 × 4,000 × 131,072 B ≈ 16.8 GB, so bytes per step ≈ 32.8 GB →\nabout 9.8 ms per step. Each user now sees roughly 100 tokens per second, but the server emits\n32 × 100 ≈ 3,300 tokens per second. Sixteen times the throughput for twice the TPOT.\n\nBatch 32 at 32k context: KV reads = 32 × 32,000 × 131,072 B ≈ 134 GB, which does not even fit next to the\nweights in 80 GB, and would take about 45 ms per step if it did. Long context, not model size, is\nwhat pushes TPOT up at scale. Measured numbers will be worse than every figure here; the point is\nthe ratios and which term dominates.","tradeoffs":["Raising concurrency multiplies throughput for a small TPOT cost while weights dominate the step, but once KV reads dominate (long contexts) or compute binds, each added sequence costs every user real latency.","Weight quantisation lowers TPOT at low batch and can cut quality on some tasks; at high batch the speed gain fades while the quality cost stays, so evaluate both before shipping.","Tensor parallelism cuts per-GPU bytes but adds a per-layer all-reduce; on PCIe-connected GPUs the communication can eat the gain, so it pays mainly with NVLink and large models.","Speculative decoding reduces effective TPOT when the acceptance rate is high and the batch is small; with low acceptance or a saturated GPU it burns compute and can reduce throughput.","Chunked prefill smooths ITL for running streams at the price of slower TTFT for long prompts; for a prefill-heavy, low-concurrency workload you may prefer the stalls."],"pitfalls":["Expecting a faster-FLOPs GPU to speed up single-stream decode proportionally; the symptom is a costly upgrade that moves TPOT only as much as the bandwidth ratio.","Reporting TPOT only as a mean; the symptom is users describing \"freezes\" while the dashboard shows a healthy 25 ms, because prefill stalls and preemptions live in p99 ITL.","Including TTFT in the TPOT calculation; the symptom is TPOT that mysteriously grows with prompt length even though decode speed has not changed.","Benchmarking TPOT at batch 1 and sizing concurrency from it; the symptom is per-user speed that halves the moment real traffic arrives.","Ignoring context length in capacity math; the symptom is TPOT and preemptions climbing as conversations get longer during the day.","Counting streamed chunks as tokens when computing TPOT; servers can coalesce tokens, so the symptom is a TPOT that looks better than the provider's own usage numbers imply."],"when":"Track TPOT (and p99 ITL) for every surface where output length is non-trivial: chat, code\ngeneration, long-form drafting, and above all agent loops where total time is a sum of many decode\nphases. Set the target from the consumer: a human reader is satisfied by a few hundred milliseconds\nper token, a code editor wants tens of milliseconds, and an orchestrator chaining calls wants the\nlowest you can afford. Use the bandwidth floor to sanity-check vendor claims and hardware choices\nbefore testing, then measure under realistic concurrency and context length. In interviews, deriving\nthe floor from weights, KV size and bandwidth, and then explaining why batching barely changes it, is\nthe expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Measures TPOT and p99 ITL from the stream, turns on chunked prefill when stalls appear, and tests weight quantisation against an eval set before enabling it.","architect":"Picks GPU class by memory bandwidth and capacity rather than FLOPs for decode-heavy workloads, and sets the concurrency ceiling where per-user TPOT still meets the SLO.","fde":"Explains to a customer with an on-prem A100 why their chat streams at half the speed of the demo on H100s, and which of quantisation, TP or a smaller model is viable for them."},"check":{"q":"Product wants chat tokens to stream twice as fast. The team proposes moving from A100 to H100\nGPUs. Is that enough, what else would you consider, and what are the catches of each option?","a":"Decode is bandwidth-bound, so the upgrade buys roughly the ratio of memory bandwidth, about\n1.6× from A100-80GB to H100 SXM, not 2×, and less if the batch is large enough to be partly\ncompute-bound. To close the gap: weight-only int8 or int4 quantisation halves or quarters the bytes\nper step, with a quality check required; tensor parallelism across two NVLink GPUs halves per-GPU\nreads but adds all-reduce latency and doubles hardware per replica; speculative decoding can give\n2–3× at low batch if the draft model's acceptance rate is high, but wastes compute at high load;\nlowering per-replica concurrency raises per-user speed at a throughput cost. Before any of it, check\np99 ITL: if the complaint is really periodic stalls from prefill or preemption, chunked prefill and\nmore KV memory headroom fix it with no hardware change."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does per-request TPOT rise only slightly when the batch grows from 1 to 16 sequences?","options":["The GPU has 16 streaming multiprocessors, one per sequence","The model weights are read from memory once per step and shared by all sequences, so only the KV-cache reads grow","Continuous batching pauses other sequences while each one decodes","Larger batches switch the engine from fp16 to int8 automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step is dominated by streaming the weights from HBM; that cost is paid once regardless of batch size. Each extra sequence adds only its KV reads and a little arithmetic, so throughput scales nearly linearly while per-token latency creeps up."},{"q":"Which of these does NOT reduce TPOT for a single stream at batch 1?","options":["Weight-only int4 quantisation","Speculative decoding with a well-matched draft model","Prefix caching of the system prompt","Tensor parallelism across two NVLink-connected GPUs"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prefix caching avoids prefill work and therefore helps TTFT, but every decode step still reads the full weights. Quantisation and tensor parallelism cut bytes per step, and speculative decoding yields several tokens per weight read."},{"q":"A stream shows a steady 25 ms between tokens with occasional 300 ms gaps. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Network jitter between the client and the server","The sampling temperature is too high for stable decoding","Prefill of newly admitted requests, or preemption, running inside the shared engine step","The tokenizer is slow on rare words"],"answer":2,"explanation":"In continuous batching, a long prompt entering the batch makes that step expensive and every other stream waits for it; preemption when KV memory is exhausted causes the same signature. Chunked prefill and more memory headroom are the fixes, not network or sampling changes."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/tokens-sec","sources":["Zhong et al., 2024 — DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized Large Language Model Serving","Official docs: vLLM benchmarking"],"why":"Serving an LLM is selling tokens produced per unit of GPU time, so the business, the capacity plan\nand the user experience all need a rate. The problem is that the obvious rate is overloaded. A\nvendor slide says \"1,200 tokens/s\"; a user complains the assistant \"only does 30 tokens/s\"; an\nengineer sizes a cluster from a benchmark at concurrency 1. All three are using the same words for\ndifferent quantities, and the mistakes compound: sizing from single-stream speed can undershoot\ncapacity by an order of magnitude, while judging user experience from aggregate throughput hides\nthat each stream is crawling.\n\nThe naive fix, \"just report one tokens/s per deployment\", fails because the number is not a property\nof the deployment. It depends on how many requests are in flight, how long their prompts and answers\nare, whether the measurement counts prompt tokens (which prefill chews through tens of times faster\nthan decode produces output), and even which tokenizer defines a token. Two models given the same\nEnglish text can differ in token count by double-digit percentages, and far more on code or\nnon-Latin scripts. Without pinning those variables down, tokens/s is marketing, not measurement.\nPinning them down is what this lesson is about.","how":"### The four numbers hiding in one phrase\n1. **Per-stream output tokens/s.** One request's decode rate after its first token: about `1 / TPOT`.\n   This is what a reader perceives.\n2. **Aggregate output tokens/s.** Output tokens produced by the whole replica per second across all\n   concurrent requests. In steady state it is roughly `concurrency × per-stream rate`, and it is the\n   number that sets cost per token.\n3. **Input (prefill) tokens/s.** Prompt tokens processed per second. Prefill is compute-bound and\n   batched, so this is typically one to two orders of magnitude higher than output rate on the same\n   hardware.\n4. **\"Total\" tokens/s.** Input plus output over wall time. It is dominated by the cheap input term, so\n   it makes any system look fast; treat a quote that does not say \"output\" as this number.\n\nAlongside these sit requests per second and *goodput*: the rate of requests (or tokens) served\n*within* the latency SLO. Goodput is the honest capacity figure because it refuses to count tokens\nthat arrived too late to matter.\n\n### How they relate\nPer-stream rate falls slowly as concurrency rises, because each decode step streams the weights once\nfor all sequences and only KV-cache reads grow with the batch. Aggregate rate therefore climbs almost\nlinearly at first, then bends as KV reads dominate or the GPU becomes compute-bound, and finally\nstops when KV memory is full and new requests queue. The two curves cross whatever SLO you have at\nsome concurrency; that crossing, not the plateau, is the capacity you can sell for interactive\ntraffic.\n\n\"Aggregate ≈ concurrency × per-stream rate\" is only the decode-phase view. Little's law makes it exact\nin steady state: requests per second = concurrency / mean request latency, so\n\n```\naggregate_output_tok/s ≈ concurrency × n_out / (TTFT + (n_out − 1) × TPOT)\n```\n\nWith 64 in flight, TPOT 25 ms and TTFT 500 ms, 1,000-token answers give about 64 × 1000 / 25.5 s ≈\n2,500 tokens/s, while 50-token answers give 64 × 50 / 1.7 s ≈ 1,900 tokens/s on the same engine,\nbecause each slot spends almost a third of its life in queue and prefill rather than decoding.\n\n### What moves the numbers\n- **Bytes per step:** model size and weight precision set the floor of per-stream speed.\n- **Memory bandwidth:** the hardware term; FLOPs matter only once compute binds at high batch.\n- **Concurrency:** trades per-stream for aggregate, as above.\n- **Context length:** longer sequences mean more KV bytes per step and fewer sequences that fit, so\n  both per-stream and aggregate fall as conversations grow.\n- **Output length distribution:** many short answers mean more scheduling churn and a larger prefill\n  share; aggregate output rate falls even though the GPU is busy.\n- **Tokenizer:** changes the denominator. Comparing models by tokens/s rewards the one with the\n  coarser vocabulary; compare characters or words per second for user-facing speed.\n\n### Measuring it without fooling yourself\nCount tokens from the server's usage report, not from the number of streamed chunks. Warm up and\nmeasure in steady state, excluding model load and cache fill. Decide between a closed-loop test\n(fixed concurrency, each client sends the next request when the previous finishes) and an open-loop\ntest (requests arrive at a set rate regardless of completion); closed-loop can never show queue\nbuild-up, so capacity numbers from it are optimistic. Use realistic prompt and output length\ndistributions, report failures and timeouts as part of the run, and publish percentiles of TTFT and\nTPOT next to the throughput figure. vLLM ships a serving benchmark script and NVIDIA's GenAI-Perf\nexists for the same job; a 30-line asyncio harness is enough to start.\n\n### From tokens/s to money\nCost per million output tokens on self-hosted hardware is\n`gpu_hourly_cost / (aggregate_output_tokens_per_second × 3600) × 1e6`. Illustrative: a replica\nsustaining 3,000 output tokens/s at an assumed $3 per GPU-hour produces 10.8 million tokens per hour,\nabout $0.28 per million output tokens, before idle time, redundancy and prefill. Run the same replica\nat concurrency 1 for 200 tokens/s and the same GPU-hour yields $4.17 per million. That spread is the\nentire economics of batching, and it is why the concurrency you choose is a pricing decision.","example":"A small closed-loop load generator against any OpenAI-compatible streaming server: *c* workers each\nsend a request, wait for it to finish, and send the next, so concurrency stays near *c* for the\nwhole run. It reports aggregate output tokens/s (total tokens over wall time, not an average of\nper-request rates), median per-stream rate and TTFT percentiles at several concurrency levels, which\nis the curve you need before setting a per-replica concurrency limit.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, statistics, time\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nclient = AsyncOpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:8000/v1\", api_key=\"unused\")\nMODEL, PROMPT = \"my-model\", \"Explain how a KV cache works in detail.\"\n\nasync def one(max_tokens=256):\n    t0 = time.perf_counter(); t_first = None; n = 0\n    stream = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=MODEL, messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": PROMPT}], max_tokens=max_tokens,\n        stream=True, stream_options={\"include_usage\": True})\n    async for ch in stream:\n        if ch.usage:                      # authoritative count, sent in the final chunk\n            n = ch.usage.completion_tokens\n        if ch.choices and ch.choices[0].delta.content and t_first is None:\n            t_first = time.perf_counter()\n    return t_first - t0, n, time.perf_counter() - t0\n\nasync def level(c: int, per_worker: int = 4):\n    res = []\n    async def worker():                    # closed loop: send the next request when one finishes\n        for _ in range(per_worker):\n            res.append(await one())\n    t0 = time.perf_counter()\n    await asyncio.gather(*(worker() for _ in range(c)))\n    wall = time.perf_counter() - t0\n    out = sum(n for _, n, _ in res)                                   # total output tokens / wall time\n    per_stream = [n / (total - ttft) for ttft, n, total in res if n > 1]\n    ttfts = sorted(ttft for ttft, _, _ in res)\n    print(f\"c={c:3d}  aggregate={out / wall:7.0f} tok/s  per-stream p50={statistics.median(per_stream):5.0f} tok/s\"\n          f\"  TTFT p50={ttfts[len(ttfts)//2]*1e3:5.0f} ms  p95={ttfts[int(len(ttfts)*.95)]*1e3:5.0f} ms\")\n\nasync def main():\n    await one()                                                       # warm-up\n    for c in (1, 4, 16, 64):\n        await level(c)\nasyncio.run(main())\n# Typical shape: aggregate climbs steeply then flattens; per-stream falls gently then faster;\n# TTFT p95 jumps when the engine starts queueing. Pick the largest c that still meets your SLOs.\n# A closed loop never offers more than c requests, so rerun open-loop before trusting the limit.\n```","tradeoffs":["Raising concurrency raises aggregate tokens/s and lowers cost per token, but lowers per-stream speed; the right point flips from \"maximise aggregate\" to \"cap concurrency\" the moment a per-stream or TTFT SLO exists.","Quoting total (input + output) tokens/s gives a bigger, more flattering number that is fair for prefill-heavy jobs like summarisation, and misleading for chat where output dominates the cost.","Comparing engines on peak throughput is cheap to run but rewards configurations that queue aggressively; goodput at your SLO is harder to measure and the only figure that predicts capacity.","Speculative decoding raises per-stream tokens/s at low concurrency but can lower aggregate at high concurrency, so a benchmark at one setting can reverse at the other.","Long-context workloads cut both numbers because KV reads grow and fewer sequences fit; a model that wins at 1k context can lose at 32k."],"pitfalls":["Comparing providers or models by tokens/s across different tokenizers; the symptom is the \"faster\" model taking longer to finish the same paragraph.","Benchmarking at concurrency 1 and dividing a fleet by that; the symptom is capacity that is wildly under- or over-provisioned depending on which number was used.","Counting streamed chunks as tokens; the symptom is tokens/s that disagrees with usage and with the bill, since servers coalesce or split tokens per event.","Closed-loop load tests only; the symptom is a capacity estimate that collapses in production when arrivals burst and queues form.","Ignoring prompt tokens in capacity math; the symptom is a RAG workload saturating prefill compute while the output tokens/s dashboard says the GPU has headroom.","Averaging per-request tokens/s instead of dividing total tokens by wall time; the symptom is an aggregate figure that short requests inflate and that no capacity model can reproduce."],"when":"Reach for tokens/s whenever the question is capacity or cost: how many replicas for a launch, what a\nGPU-hour yields, whether a quantised model pays for itself, whether a vendor's claim covers your\nworkload. Always state which of the four numbers you mean and the conditions behind it. Reach for\nTPOT and TTFT instead when the question is how one request feels. Day to day it appears in capacity\nreviews, vendor comparisons and pricing models; in interviews the strong move is to refuse to answer\n\"how many tokens/s does it do\" until concurrency, lengths, hardware and which tokens are specified,\nthen to connect the number to cost per million tokens.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the load harness, reads usage rather than chunk counts, and produces the concurrency-versus-throughput curve for each model and hardware pairing.","architect":"Converts aggregate tokens/s at the SLO into replica counts and cost per token, and rejects vendor numbers that omit concurrency and lengths.","fde":"Re-runs the benchmark on the customer's hardware and prompt mix, because their tokenizer, context lengths and traffic pattern invalidate the reference numbers."},"check":{"q":"A vendor claims their endpoint delivers 1,200 tokens per second. What questions do you ask\nbefore deciding whether it will serve your product, and why does each one matter?","a":"Which tokens: output only, or input plus output, since prefill inflates totals? At what\nconcurrency: one stream or the whole deployment, and is 1,200 what one user sees or what a GPU\nproduces? With what prompt and output lengths, because long contexts shrink both numbers and short\noutputs raise scheduling overhead? On which hardware and precision, so I can compare to a bandwidth\nfloor? Closed- or open-loop, and what were TTFT and TPOT percentiles at that rate, since throughput\nwithout an SLO is not capacity? Which tokenizer, since I will compare to my current model by words\nor characters, not tokens? Finally, is it sustained or peak, and does it include failures? The answers\nturn a slogan into a point on a curve I can place my workload on."},"quiz":[{"q":"A replica produces 4,000 output tokens/s in aggregate at a steady concurrency of 64. Roughly what does each user see?","options":["About 4,000 tokens/s, since every stream runs at full speed","About 60 tokens/s, because the aggregate is shared across the concurrent streams","About 256,000 tokens/s, the product of the two numbers","It cannot be estimated without the TTFT distribution"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In steady state aggregate ≈ concurrency × per-stream rate, so 4,000 / 64 ≈ 62 tokens/s per user. TTFT affects when a stream starts, not its decode rate once running."},{"q":"Which comparison between two models is NOT apples-to-apples?","options":["Aggregate output tokens/s on the same GPU at the same concurrency and lengths","Output tokens/s when the two models use different tokenizers","Characters of output per second on the same prompts","Cost per million output tokens at the SLO on the same hardware"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tokenizer with a coarser vocabulary emits fewer tokens for the same text, so a slower model can post a higher tokens/s figure. Characters or words per second, or cost at the SLO, remove that distortion."},{"q":"Which single change increases a replica's aggregate output tokens/s while lowering each user's per-stream rate?","options":["Weight-only int4 quantisation of the model","Moving to a GPU with higher memory bandwidth","Raising the maximum number of concurrent sequences","Enabling speculative decoding at batch size 1"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Higher concurrency shares each weight read among more sequences, raising total output while each step takes slightly longer. Quantisation and more bandwidth raise both numbers, and speculative decoding at batch 1 raises per-stream speed."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/throughput-vs-latency","sources":["Yu et al., 2022 — Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models","Agrawal et al., 2024 — Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW"],"why":"A GPU serving an LLM at concurrency 1 is mostly idle: it streams 16 GB of weights through its memory\nsystem to produce a single token, then does it again. Letting it produce 32 tokens per pass instead\ncosts almost nothing extra, which is why continuous batching exists and why self-hosting is\naffordable at all. But the same arithmetic means the server is now making 32 users share one\npipeline. Each step is a little slower, new arrivals wait for a slot, and under bursty traffic the\nwait, not the step, becomes the latency.\n\nTeams that never choose get the worst of both. Run at high utilisation to keep cost per token low and\nthe p99 latency explodes, because queueing delay grows without bound as utilisation approaches 100 %.\nRun at low concurrency to keep latency tight and you pay for GPUs that are 80 % idle. The naive\n\"autoscale on GPU utilisation\" does not resolve it either. The usual utilisation metric (what\n`nvidia-smi` reports) is the fraction of time any kernel is running, not the fraction of compute in\nuse, so a decode loop at batch 4 and one at batch 200 can both read near 100 %. It tells you the GPU\nis busy, not how much headroom is left, and it says nothing about the queue that users feel.\n\nProviders make the trade-off explicit in their pricing. Interactive endpoints are tuned for latency\nand cost more; batch endpoints run at the throughput end of the curve with multi-hour completion\nwindows at roughly half the interactive price at the time of writing. If you run your own engine,\nyou are the one who has to make that choice, per workload, with numbers.","how":"### The shape of the curve\nPlot concurrency on the x-axis. Throughput (aggregate output tokens/s) rises almost linearly, bends,\nand plateaus. Per-request latency (TPOT, and TTFT once queueing starts) stays nearly flat, then turns\nupward at a knee, then climbs steeply. The mechanism for the decode phase is a single line:\n\n```\nstep_time ≈ (weight_bytes + B × kv_bytes_per_seq) / bandwidth        (bandwidth-bound regime)\nthroughput ≈ B / step_time          per-request TPOT ≈ step_time\n```\n\nWhile `weight_bytes` dominates, *B* can grow with little effect on step time, so throughput scales\nwith *B*. As `B × kv_bytes` catches up (long contexts do this quickly) each new sequence costs every\nother one real latency. Eventually arithmetic overtakes bandwidth and the step becomes compute-bound,\nor KV memory fills and the engine can admit no one; from there additional load only lengthens the\nqueue.\n\n### Queueing sits on top\nEven below the knee, arrivals are random. Little's law says the average number of requests in the\nsystem equals arrival rate times average time in system (`L = λW`), and for a simple single-server\nqueue the time in system grows like `service_time / (1 − ρ)`, where ρ is utilisation. At ρ = 0.5 a\nrequest spends twice its service time in the system; at ρ = 0.9, ten times. LLM services have\nlong, highly variable service times (output length is unknown in advance), which makes the tail\nworse than the simple model predicts. That is why interactive services target moderate utilisation\nand why TTFT p99 is the first thing to break under load.\n\n### Choosing the operating point\n1. Write the SLO as percentiles: for example TTFT p95 ≤ 1 s and TPOT p95 ≤ 50 ms for chat.\n2. Run a load curve at realistic prompt and output lengths and find the largest concurrency at\n   which both hold. That is the replica's *goodput* capacity.\n3. Set the engine's per-replica concurrency limit (`max_num_seqs` in vLLM) and token budget there,\n   with headroom for bursts.\n4. Shed or queue beyond it visibly: a fast 429 with `Retry-After`, or a queue position, instead of a\n   silent 10-second TTFT.\n5. Autoscale on queue depth or TTFT, not on GPU utilisation.\n6. Separate pools: interactive traffic at the knee, batch and background jobs at the plateau where\n   each token is cheapest and nobody is waiting.\n\n### Techniques move the curve; they do not remove it\nContinuous batching lets sequences join and leave every step instead of waiting for the slowest.\nPagedAttention stops KV fragmentation so more sequences fit before memory binds. Chunked prefill\nbounds the prefill work per step so new arrivals do not stall running streams. Disaggregated\nprefill and decode put the two phases on different GPUs so one workload's TTFT stops competing with\nanother's TPOT. Quantisation shrinks bytes per step and KV footprint. Speculative decoding lowers\nlatency at low concurrency but spends compute that the plateau needs, so it can reduce peak\nthroughput. Each of these shifts the knee rightward or the floor downward; none lets you run at\n100 % utilisation with flat latency.\n\n### If you do not run the engine\nWith a hosted API you do not set the batch size, but the trade-off is still yours: you choose between\nthe interactive and batch tiers, how many requests you keep in flight per key, which timeouts you\naccept, and whether to pay for provisioned or dedicated capacity when tail latency matters. The\nprovider's rate limits are, in effect, the operating point they chose for you.","example":"A worked scenario on one replica, using the illustrative bandwidth model from the TPOT lesson: an\n8B fp16 model (16 GB of weights, 128 KB of KV cache per token) on an 80 GB GPU with about 3.35 TB/s\nof memory bandwidth, every request at 4k tokens of context, so each sequence holds about 0.5 GB of\nKV cache.\n\nMemory capacity caps concurrency first: after weights and a few GB of working space, roughly 60 GB\nremain, so at most about 120 sequences can be resident. Bytes per step at *B* sequences are\n`16 GB + B × 0.5 GB`, so TPOT ≈ `(16 + 0.5B) / 3350` seconds and throughput ≈ `B / TPOT`.\n\nNow apply an SLO. If chat needs TPOT ≤ 50 ms, the bandwidth model allows `16 + 0.5B ≤ 167 GB`,\ni.e. *B* up to about 300. The memory cap of ~120 binds first, so latency is not the limit: at\n*B* = 120 the step is `(16 + 60) / 3350 ≈ 23 ms`, comfortably inside the SLO, and throughput is\nabout 120 / 0.023 ≈ 5,300 tokens/s. Run the replica full.\n\nTighten the SLO to TPOT ≤ 15 ms, as a code-completion product might. Now `16 + 0.5B ≤ 50 GB`, so\n*B* ≤ 68 and latency binds before memory does; throughput falls to about 68 / 0.015 ≈ 4,500\ntokens/s. Tighten again to 10 ms and *B* ≤ 35, throughput ≈ 3,500 tokens/s. The same hardware\ndelivers about a third fewer tokens per hour, hence roughly 50 % higher cost per token, purely\nbecause of the latency target. That is the trade-off turned into a budget line, before any\nqueueing delay is added on top; real measurements will sit below every figure here.","tradeoffs":["Higher concurrency lowers cost per token and raises per-request latency; the balance flips from \"fill the GPU\" to \"cap the batch\" the moment a per-request SLO exists for that traffic.","Running at low utilisation buys flat tail latency and wastes hardware; it is the right call for interactive tiers and the wrong one for offline jobs that can wait hours.","Speculative decoding improves latency when the GPU has spare compute (low concurrency) and can cut peak throughput when it does not, so it belongs on the interactive pool, not the batch pool.","Sharing one pool between interactive and batch traffic maximises utilisation but lets batch arrivals push interactive TTFT past the knee; separate pools cost headroom and buy predictability.","Shedding load with fast 429s protects the latency of admitted requests at the cost of failed ones; queueing admits everyone and lets latency drift, which is better only when callers can wait."],"pitfalls":["Autoscaling on GPU utilisation; the symptom is a metric pinned near 100 % at both light and heavy load, so replicas scale out late or never while users wait through a queue.","Benchmarking throughput with a closed loop and no SLO; the symptom is a \"capacity\" figure that production never reaches without blowing p99.","Sizing from mean latency; the symptom is on-call pages for p99 while the dashboard average looks fine, since queueing lives entirely in the tail.","Letting one batch job share the interactive pool; the symptom is TTFT spikes at the exact minute a cron job starts.","Raising `max_num_seqs` to fix throughput without checking KV memory; the symptom is preemptions and recompute stalls that lower throughput and latency at once.","Treating speculative decoding as free throughput; the symptom is lower aggregate tokens/s at peak than without it."],"when":"This is the decision you make every time you configure a replica, choose a provider tier, or set a\nrate limit. Reach for the latency end whenever a person or a latency-sensitive agent is waiting:\nchat, completions, voice, interactive tools. Reach for the throughput end for evaluation runs, bulk\nextraction, embeddings backfills, nightly summaries and anything with a deadline measured in hours.\nMeasure the curve (throughput, TTFT p95, TPOT p95 against concurrency) for each model and hardware\npairing, pick the point, and enforce it with limits and alerts on queue depth. In interviews the\nstrong answer draws the curve, explains why it bends (weights shared per step, then KV and compute\nbinding), adds the queueing tail, and names the concurrency limit as the control.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Produces the load curve, sets per-replica concurrency and token budgets, and wires 429 shedding and queue-depth metrics into the serving layer.","architect":"Owns the operating point per traffic class, decides on separate pools or tiers, and translates the SLO into replica counts and cost per token.","fde":"Negotiates a customer's latency target against their GPU budget with the curve in hand, often by moving their batch workloads off the interactive pool."},"check":{"q":"One replica reports 85 % GPU utilisation and p99 TTFT of 6 s. Finance will not approve more\nGPUs. What do you do, and in what order?","a":"First establish which constraint binds: check KV-memory occupancy, preemption counts and\nengine queue depth; 85 % utilisation in a bandwidth-bound decode loop says little about headroom.\nThen protect latency with a per-replica concurrency cap set from a load curve, shedding the excess\nwith fast 429s so admitted requests stay inside the SLO instead of everyone waiting 6 s. Next\nrecover capacity without hardware: enable prefix caching for the shared system prompt, turn on\nchunked prefill so long prompts stop stalling admission, cap `max_model_len` to what the product\nneeds so more sequences fit, and evaluate weight or KV quantisation. Move any batch or background\ntraffic to off-peak hours or a separate cheap pool. Finally measure goodput at the SLO and present\nfinance with cost per token at the required latency, which is the real request."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does raising the batch size increase throughput almost linearly at first?","options":["Each sequence gets its own copy of the weights in GPU memory","The weights are read from memory once per decode step and shared by all sequences in the batch","The GPU clock increases under heavier load","Continuous batching compresses the KV cache as the batch grows"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A decode step is bound by streaming the weights from memory, a cost paid once per step regardless of batch size. More sequences per step mean more tokens for nearly the same time until KV reads or compute start to dominate."},{"q":"Which statement about queueing delay in a loaded LLM service is correct?","options":["Average wait grows linearly with utilisation, so 90 % load means 90 % more delay","Average time in the system grows roughly like 1 / (1 − utilisation), so tail latency explodes as load nears saturation","Queueing only matters once GPU memory is completely full","Mean latency captures queueing well because waits are evenly distributed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The simple single-server result `W ≈ service_time / (1 − ρ)` already gives ten times the service time at ρ = 0.9, and LLM service times are long and variable, which makes the tail worse. The damage shows up in p95 and p99 long before memory is exhausted."},{"q":"Which technique improves per-request latency at low load but can reduce peak throughput at high load?","options":["PagedAttention","Prefix caching","Speculative decoding","Chunked prefill"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Speculative decoding spends extra compute drafting and verifying several tokens per step; that compute is spare at low concurrency but is exactly what a saturated GPU needs, so peak throughput can fall. PagedAttention, prefix caching and chunked prefill shift the curve without that penalty."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/timeouts","sources":["Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Official docs: httpx timeouts","Official docs: OpenAI Python SDK"],"why":"Ordinary HTTP timeouts assume a response takes tens of milliseconds and that anything slower is\nbroken. An LLM response at 20 ms per token takes 20 s for 1,000 tokens when everything is healthy;\na reasoning model can pause for a long stretch before emitting anything; and a request queued behind\na burst can wait seconds before prefill even starts. Variance of 100× between fast and slow legitimate\ncalls is normal. Under those conditions a \"30 seconds and give up\" rule silently fails a large share\nof long outputs, while \"ten minutes to be safe\" lets a dead upstream pin a worker, a connection-pool\nslot and a user for ten minutes.\n\nThe damage compounds. Hung requests hold slots, so healthy requests queue behind them; the client\ntimes out and retries, adding load to an upstream that is already slow; the retries time out too.\nThis is the classic overload spiral, and LLM backends are unusually prone to it because each request\nis expensive and long. Meanwhile infrastructure you forgot about has its own opinions: reverse\nproxies and load balancers ship with idle timeouts of about a minute, so a non-streaming generation\nthat takes 70 s is cut off with a gateway error and the engine keeps generating for nobody.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"stream and never time out\", is not enough either. Streaming turns \"no bytes\nfor N seconds\" into a usable liveness signal, but you still need a bound on total time so a stuck\nstream cannot run until `max_tokens`, and you still need every downstream call in an agent step to\nknow how much of the user's patience remains.","how":"### The timeout stack\nA single call crosses several layers, each with its own timer: the SDK (the OpenAI Python client\ndefaults to a 10-minute timeout and two retries), the HTTP library under it (httpx splits a timeout\ninto connect, read, write and pool phases, each defaulting to five seconds), your own server's\nhandler, the proxy or load balancer in front of it (nginx's `proxy_read_timeout` and an AWS ALB's\nidle timeout both default to 60 s), the provider's gateway, and finally the engine's scheduler, which\nmay hold a request in its admission queue. The effective timeout is the *shortest* of these, and the\none that fires is rarely the one you configured. Retries stack on top: because that SDK retries a\ntimed-out request by default, its worst case is three full timeouts, half an hour, before your code\nsees an error, unless you lower `timeout` or `max_retries`.\n\n### Which timers, and what each one means\n- **Connect timeout.** TCP and TLS handshake. Short (a few seconds); failure here means the host is\n  unreachable, and retrying elsewhere is cheap and safe.\n- **Time to first byte / first token.** Covers queueing plus prefill. Set it from your TTFT p99 with\n  margin (illustratively 10–30 s for long-prompt workloads); when it fires the request has consumed\n  little, so a fallback or retry on another replica is reasonable.\n- **Idle (read) timeout between chunks.** The liveness signal for a stream. A healthy stream emits a\n  token every few tens of milliseconds, so an idle timer of 15–30 s only fires when the upstream is\n  stuck, provided the server sends heartbeats during legitimate pauses such as hidden reasoning.\n- **Total deadline.** The budget for the whole call, and for non-streaming calls the only timer that\n  distinguishes \"slow\" from \"dead\". It must cover the longest *legitimate* response.\n\n### Budget arithmetic\nFor a non-streaming call the deadline has to be `TTFT_p99 + max_tokens × TPOT_p99 + margin`. With\n`max_tokens = 4096` and a TPOT of 30 ms that is already over two minutes before queueing, which is\nwhy non-streaming calls with large `max_tokens` collide with 60-second proxies and why some SDKs\nrefuse very long non-streaming requests and tell you to stream. Derive the number from `max_tokens`\nper call rather than hard-coding one constant, and cap `max_tokens` to what the product needs; a\nsmaller cap is the cheapest timeout fix there is.\n\n### Deadline propagation\nAn agent step that must answer within 60 s and makes four LLM calls and two tool calls cannot give\neach of them 60 s. Carry a deadline (an absolute timestamp) through the call chain, and give every\ndownstream call `remaining = deadline − now`. When `remaining` falls below the minimum useful time\nfor a step, fail fast with a partial result rather than start work that cannot finish. gRPC carries\ndeadlines natively; over HTTP you pass it as a header or in your own context object.\n\n### What happens when a timer fires\nNothing stops upstream unless you make it. A client-side timeout that merely raises an exception\nleaves the engine decoding, the provider billing, and the connection half-open until the server's\nown timers notice. Every timeout must therefore close the connection or abort the request so the\nsignal reaches the engine; that is the cancellation lesson's job, and timeouts are its most common\ntrigger.\n\n### Retrying after a timeout\nA timeout is the most expensive failure to retry: the first attempt may still be running, it signals\noverload more often than a transient glitch, and the retry needs the same long budget again. Retry\nonly when the remaining deadline allows it, with jitter, and only for idempotent requests. Prefer\nfalling back (a smaller or different model, a cached answer, a degraded response) over retrying the\nsame call, and open a circuit breaker when timeouts cluster, so a sick upstream is not hammered.\n\n### Server-side timers if you run the engine\nBound the admission queue: a request that has waited longer than its first-token budget should be\nrejected with a fast 429 or 503 rather than served late. Cap per-request generation time and\n`max_tokens` at the API boundary, and expose queue wait as a metric so you can see the budget being\nspent before clients time out.","example":"Layered timeouts around a streaming call with `httpx`: a short connect timeout, a cumulative\nfirst-token timer, an idle timer that resets on every line (heartbeats included), and a total\ndeadline derived from `max_tokens` and measured TPOT, capped by the caller's absolute deadline so an\nagent can share one budget across several steps. With these numbers a 512-token call gets\n`8 + 512 × 0.05 × 1.5 ≈ 46 s`, and the first-token timer turns a stuck queue into an error after\n15 s instead of 46.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, json, time\nimport httpx\n\nTTFT_P99, TPOT_P99 = 8.0, 0.05            # measured for this model and traffic, in seconds\nFIRST_TOKEN, IDLE = 15.0, 20.0\n\ndef call_budget(max_tokens: int) -> float:\n    return TTFT_P99 + max_tokens * TPOT_P99 * 1.5    # 1.5x margin on decode\n\nasync def stream_completion(client: httpx.AsyncClient, payload: dict, deadline: float) -> str:\n    budget = min(call_budget(payload[\"max_tokens\"]), deadline - time.monotonic())\n    if budget < 2.0:\n        raise TimeoutError(\"not enough budget left to start this call\")\n    first_by = time.monotonic() + FIRST_TOKEN\n    timeout = httpx.Timeout(connect=3.0, read=IDLE + 5, write=10.0, pool=3.0)  # socket backstop\n    text = []\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(budget):                       # total deadline (Python 3.11+)\n            async with client.stream(\"POST\", \"/v1/chat/completions\",\n                                     json={**payload, \"stream\": True}, timeout=timeout) as r:\n                r.raise_for_status()\n                lines = r.aiter_lines()\n                while True:\n                    wait = IDLE if text else min(IDLE, first_by - time.monotonic())\n                    try:\n                        line = await asyncio.wait_for(anext(lines), max(wait, 0.0))\n                    except StopAsyncIteration:\n                        break\n                    if not line.startswith(\"data: \") or line == \"data: [DONE]\":\n                        continue                                  # heartbeats reset IDLE only\n                    delta = json.loads(line[6:])[\"choices\"][0][\"delta\"].get(\"content\")\n                    if delta: text.append(delta)\n    except (TimeoutError, httpx.TimeoutException) as e:\n        # leaving `client.stream` closed the connection: the upstream's only signal to stop decoding\n        phase = \"first-token\" if not text else \"idle-or-total\"\n        raise TimeoutError(f\"{phase} timeout after {len(text)} chunks\") from e\n    return \"\".join(text)\n```","tradeoffs":["A short first-token timeout fails fast and enables cheap fallback, but under a traffic burst it turns a recoverable queue into a wave of errors; the balance flips when callers can wait and nothing else can serve them.","Idle timeouts give a precise liveness signal for streams at the cost of needing heartbeats during legitimate pauses; without heartbeats they are worse than a plain total deadline.","Deriving deadlines from `max_tokens` is accurate but means a generous cap buys a long timeout; tighter caps make timeouts tighter at the cost of truncating rare long answers.","Retrying after a timeout can rescue a transient failure and can also double load on an overloaded upstream; the decision flips on whether remaining budget exists and whether the timeout rate is rising fleet-wide.","Long server-side timeouts maximise the chance a slow request completes and let hung requests hold capacity; shorter ones protect the pool and shed work that would have finished."],"pitfalls":["Inheriting defaults: a 60-second proxy or load balancer idle timeout in front of a non-streaming endpoint; the symptom is gateway errors at exactly 60 s on long generations while the engine keeps running.","Treating the HTTP read timeout as a total timeout; the symptom is a \"timeout\" that never fires on a slow-but-alive stream, since it resets on every chunk.","Timing out without cancelling; the symptom is provider usage and GPU load that stay high after the clients have given up, and tokens billed for answers nobody saw.","One timeout constant for every call regardless of `max_tokens`; the symptom is short calls waiting minutes to fail and long calls being cut off while healthy.","Agent steps that each get the full user budget; the symptom is a 60-second SLO breached by a request whose individual calls all \"succeeded within timeout\".","Retrying timeouts immediately and without a budget check; the symptom is an overload that gets worse as clients time out, the signature of a retry storm."],"when":"Every call to a model, a retriever or a tool needs the layered timers above; the question is only the\nvalues. Derive them from measured percentiles and from `max_tokens`, revisit them when you change\nmodels or prompt lengths, and give agents a single propagated deadline rather than per-call constants.\nStream whenever `max_tokens` is large, so liveness comes from the idle timer instead of a multi-minute\ntotal. Measure timeout rate by layer and by cause (first-token versus idle versus total), and alert\nwhen it rises, because it is usually the first visible sign of upstream overload. In interviews,\nexpect \"how would you set timeouts for an LLM call\" and answer with the stack, the arithmetic and the\ncancel-and-fallback behaviour, not a number.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures connect, first-token, idle and total timers per call from `max_tokens` and measured TPOT, and makes every expiry close the upstream connection.","architect":"Sets the end-to-end latency budget per surface, mandates deadline propagation across services, and decides where fallback replaces retry.","fde":"Finds the 60-second idle timeout in the customer's load balancer or API gateway that is truncating long answers, and gets streaming enabled through it."},"check":{"q":"An agent makes up to six sequential LLM calls and three tool calls per user request, and the\nuser-facing SLO is 60 seconds. How do you set the timeouts, and what happens when the budget runs out?","a":"Give the request one absolute deadline at entry and pass it to every step; each call gets\n`min(its own derived budget, remaining)`. Derive each LLM call's budget from its `max_tokens` and\nmeasured TPOT and stream it so the idle timer (with heartbeats) is the liveness check; give tools\nbudgets from their own latency percentiles. Before starting a step, check that enough budget remains\nto be useful; if not, stop and return the best partial result or a clear \"could not finish\" rather\nthan start a call that will be cut off. Never retry a timed-out step unless the remaining budget\ncovers it, and prefer a cheaper fallback model. Every expiry must cancel the in-flight call so the\nupstream stops. Measure which step consumes the budget in practice and shrink `max_tokens` or move\nwork earlier accordingly."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most useful liveness signal for a streaming LLM response?","options":["The connect timeout, since a connected socket means the server is alive","A single total timeout set generously to cover the longest answer","An idle timeout on the gap between chunks, combined with server heartbeats during legitimate pauses","The time to first token, after which the stream can be trusted to finish"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A healthy stream delivers data continuously, so a stalled gap is the earliest and most specific sign of a dead upstream. A total timeout still bounds the worst case, but it reacts far later; TTFT says nothing about what happens after the first token."},{"q":"Long non-streaming generations fail at exactly 60 seconds with a gateway error, and the GPU stays busy afterwards. What is the likely cause?","options":["The model has a built-in 60-second generation limit","An intermediary's default idle timeout cut the connection, and the engine was never told to abort","The client's connect timeout is too short","`max_tokens` is too low for the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reverse proxies and load balancers commonly default to about a minute of idle time, which a non-streaming response exceeds with nothing sent. The engine keeps decoding because no cancellation reached it; streaming with heartbeats or a longer idle setting plus propagated cancellation fixes both halves."},{"q":"A 90-second generation times out while the upstream is clearly overloaded. What is the safest default behaviour?","options":["Retry immediately with the same parameters to recover from the transient failure","Extend the timeout and wait, since the answer is probably almost done","Cancel the in-flight request upstream, then fall back or fail clearly, retrying only if the remaining budget allows","Switch to a non-streaming call so the response arrives in one piece"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Retrying a long, timed-out call under overload doubles the load that caused the timeout and may duplicate work still running upstream. Cancelling frees capacity, and a cheaper fallback or an honest failure respects the user's budget."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/cancellation","sources":["Dean and Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale","Official docs: Python asyncio task cancellation","Official docs: MDN AbortController"],"why":"LLM requests are expensive, long and often abandoned. Users close tabs, hit stop, or send a new\nmessage before the last answer finishes; agents fan out speculative branches and keep one; timeouts\ngive up on calls that are still running; hedged requests race two providers and only need the\nwinner. In every case the work continues unless something tells it to stop, because a GPU engine\nhas no idea whether anyone is reading.\n\nThe cost is threefold. Money: a provider bills every token decoded until the abort arrives, and a\nself-hosted replica burns GPU time on output that is discarded. Capacity: an abandoned sequence\nholds its KV blocks and its place in the batch, so live requests queue behind ghosts, TTFT rises and\npreemptions start; under a traffic spike with many disconnects this is how a healthy cluster looks\nsaturated. Correctness: in a chat UI the old answer keeps streaming into the thread after the user\nhas moved on, and in an agent a cancelled step may already have fired a tool with side effects.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"let it run, it is only a few hundred tokens\", fails on arithmetic. If a\ntenth of requests are abandoned halfway through 1,000-token answers, the replica spends about 5 %\nof its decode capacity on nothing, and the share climbs with longer outputs and more impatient users.\nTimeouts without cancellation are worse: they guarantee the client gives up while guaranteeing the\nupstream does not.","how":"### The chain, hop by hop\n1. **Trigger.** In the browser an `AbortController` aborts the `fetch`; a stop button, a new message\n   or navigation calls `controller.abort()`. Server-side triggers are timeouts, superseding requests\n   and hedge resolution.\n2. **Transport.** The client closes the connection (or resets the HTTP/2 stream).\n3. **Your server.** The framework learns of the disconnect only when it next touches the socket. A\n   streaming handler finds out at its next write, or by polling a disconnect check; a non-streaming\n   handler that is simply awaiting the model never touches the socket and never finds out.\n4. **Task cancellation.** The handler's task is cancelled (streaming frameworks usually do this on\n   disconnect) or your code cancels it. In Python, `task.cancel()` schedules a `CancelledError` at the\n   task's next `await`; it is cooperative, not a kill, so `finally` blocks run and must not swallow\n   the exception.\n5. **Upstream close.** Cleanup closes the response you were reading from the provider or engine. That\n   close is the only signal the next hop gets; forgetting it leaves the upstream running while your\n   own request looks finished.\n6. **Engine abort.** The serving layer in front of the engine (an OpenAI-compatible server, a\n   provider's gateway) detects its client's disconnect and aborts the request. A continuous-batching\n   engine removes the sequence from the running batch at the next step and releases its KV blocks for\n   other sequences. Without the abort, the sequence runs to end-of-sequence or `max_tokens`.\n\nEach arrow is a place the signal can be lost: a proxy configured to keep the upstream alive after the\nclient leaves, a worker that cannot observe the socket, a `finally` that logs and returns, an SDK\nthat buffers the response instead of streaming it.\n\n### Making disconnects observable\nStreaming is the practical fix because a write per token means the server notices a dead client\nwithin one token. For non-streaming work, run generation as a separate task and race it against a\ndisconnect watcher, or poll the request's disconnect state while waiting. Frameworks expose this\ndifferently (FastAPI's `request.is_disconnected()`, a disconnect message on the ASGI receive\nchannel), and the companion lesson in the FastAPI module covers the mechanics.\n\n### Side effects and accounting\nCancellation interrupts at an `await`, so anything between awaits either happened or did not. For\npure generation that is harmless. For an agent mid tool-call it is not: the tool may have sent the\nemail, written the row or charged the card before the cancel landed. Make tools idempotent or\ntwo-phase, record the run as \"cancelled\" rather than \"failed\", and shield the small pieces of work\nthat must complete (writing the usage record, releasing a lock) so a cancel cannot tear them. For\nbilling, remember that a cancelled stream never delivers its final usage event, while a provider\ngenerally charges for tokens generated up to the moment its gateway sees the abort, which can be\nmore than you received (tokens in flight, buffered at a proxy, or decoded during the\ndisconnect-detection lag). Keep the text you did receive and tokenize it for an estimate, treat that\nas a lower bound, and reconcile against the provider's usage reports rather than streamed chunk\ncounts.\n\n### Patterns built on cancellation\n- **Supersede.** The newest user message cancels the in-flight generation before starting the next,\n  so two answers never interleave and the slot is freed immediately.\n- **Stop button.** Same mechanism, user-triggered; keep the partial text, mark it truncated.\n- **Deadline cancel.** A timeout is only useful if it cancels; the two lessons are halves of one\n  behaviour.\n- **Hedging.** Issue the same request to two replicas or providers after a short delay and cancel the\n  loser when the first token or the full response arrives; it cuts tail latency at the price of\n  duplicate tokens, so it is reserved for latency-critical, cheap-to-duplicate calls.\n- **Speculative prefetch.** Start a likely-next generation early and cancel it if the user goes\n  elsewhere.","example":"The supersede pattern for a chat session: the newest request cancels the previous generation, and\nthe cancelled task's `finally` closes the provider stream, which is the only way the provider learns\nto stop decoding. The `asyncio.wait` keeps the caller from swallowing its own cancellation.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\nclient = AsyncOpenAI()\nMODEL = \"my-model\"\n\nclass ChatSession:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.current: asyncio.Task | None = None\n        self.received: list[str] = []            # a cancelled stream sends no usage event; tokenize\n                                                 # this later for a lower-bound estimate of billed output\n\n    async def _generate(self, messages, on_delta):\n        stream = await client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, stream=True)\n        try:\n            async for chunk in stream:\n                if chunk.choices and chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n                    self.received.append(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n                    on_delta(chunk.choices[0].delta.content)\n        finally:\n            await stream.close()                 # runs on CancelledError too: closing the HTTP response\n                                                 # is the provider's only signal to abort the sequence\n\n    async def submit(self, messages, on_delta) -> asyncio.Task:\n        prev = self.current\n        if prev and not prev.done():\n            prev.cancel()                        # supersede: new message or stop button\n            await asyncio.wait([prev])           # wait for its cleanup without swallowing our own cancel\n        self.current = asyncio.create_task(self._generate(messages, on_delta))\n        return self.current\n\nasync def demo():\n    s = ChatSession()\n    show = lambda t: print(t, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    first = await s.submit([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a long essay about rivers.\"}], show)\n    await asyncio.sleep(1.0)                     # user changes their mind one second in\n    second = await s.submit([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Actually, one sentence on rivers.\"}], show)\n    await second\n    print(\"\\nfirst cancelled:\", first.cancelled(), \"| chars received:\", sum(map(len, s.received)))\n\nasyncio.run(demo())\n```","tradeoffs":["Cancelling on disconnect frees capacity and money immediately but throws away partial work that might have been valuable; for long, expensive jobs the better design is an asynchronous job whose lifetime is independent of the HTTP connection.","Hedged requests cut tail latency and double the tokens spent on every hedged call; they pay off only for short, latency-critical calls where the tail is bad and capacity is available.","Immediate cancellation is cheapest, while a short grace period tolerates mobile network blips and tab switches; the right delay depends on how reconnecting clients resume, which for LLM streams is usually \"they cannot\", so grace periods rarely pay.","Cooperative cancellation is safe for generation and risky around side effects; tools that are idempotent can be cancelled freely, non-idempotent ones need shielding or compensation.","Polling for disconnects in non-streaming handlers adds a little overhead per request and is the only way those handlers ever stop early."],"pitfalls":["No disconnect handling at all; the symptom is GPU utilisation and provider spend that stay flat when users leave, and TTFT that rises during periods of many abandoned requests.","Catching `CancelledError` and continuing (on Python before 3.8 a plain `except Exception` did this silently, since it was not yet a `BaseException`); the symptom is tasks that never finish and `finally` cleanup that never runs.","Closing your own response but not the upstream stream; the symptom is provider usage higher than your logs can account for.","A proxy that keeps the upstream connection alive after the client aborts; the symptom is the serving layer never seeing a disconnect even though clients clearly left.","Non-streaming endpoints assumed to cancel on disconnect; the symptom is a 2-minute generation completing for a client that left after 5 seconds.","Cancelling an agent mid-tool-call with no idempotency; the symptom is duplicated emails or orders when the user retries after hitting stop.","Reporting cancelled requests as errors; the symptom is a noisy error rate that masks real failures and panics on-call."],"when":"Every service that fronts a model needs cancellation on disconnect, timeout and supersede; it is not\noptional once outputs are longer than a sentence or traffic is bursty. Reach for hedging only for\nshort, latency-critical calls with capacity to spare, and for speculative prefetch only when the\nabandoned cost is clearly less than the latency saved. Do not tie long batch jobs to a connection at\nall. Measure the abandonment rate, time from client disconnect to engine abort, and tokens generated\nafter disconnect, and check that the provider's reported usage falls when abandonment rises; if\nit does not, the abort is not reaching them. In interviews,\ntracing the stop signal hop by hop from `AbortController` to KV-block release is the answer that\nshows you have run this in production.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires `AbortController` on the client, closes upstream streams in `finally`, and makes handlers observe disconnects for both streaming and non-streaming paths.","architect":"Decides which calls may be hedged or prefetched, which jobs are detached from connections, and requires idempotent tools so cancelled agent steps cannot cause duplicates.","fde":"Finds the customer proxy or gateway that swallows client aborts, and proves with engine metrics that abandoned requests were burning their GPU capacity."},"check":{"q":"Users hit the stop button constantly, yet GPU utilisation and the provider bill do not move at\nall. Trace where the cancellation is being lost, and how you would prove each hypothesis.","a":"Start at the browser: confirm `controller.abort()` is called and the fetch actually closes\n(network tab shows the request cancelled). Next the server: does the handler observe the disconnect?\nA non-streaming handler awaiting the model never will; a streaming one should see a failed write or\n`is_disconnected()` within a token, so log it. Then task cancellation: check that the task is\ncancelled and that `finally` runs without swallowing `CancelledError`. Then the upstream: verify the\nprovider or engine response is closed, not merely abandoned to the garbage collector, by watching the\nconnection count or the provider's streamed-token usage. Then infrastructure: a proxy configured to\nignore client aborts keeps the upstream alive, visible as the serving layer never logging a\ndisconnect. Finally the engine: its abort count and freed-block metrics should rise with stop-button\npresses; if everything upstream is right and they do not, the serving layer is not calling abort."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a non-streaming endpoint usually keep generating after the client disconnects?","options":["The engine refuses to abort requests that have already started prefill","The handler is awaiting the model and never touches the socket, so nothing observes the disconnect until it tries to write the response","HTTP does not allow a client to close a connection before the response arrives","Non-streaming responses are cached by the proxy and must be completed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A disconnect is only visible to code that reads from or writes to the connection. A streaming handler writes every token and notices quickly; a non-streaming one discovers the dead client only when the finished answer fails to send, long after the work was wasted."},{"q":"In Python asyncio, what does calling `task.cancel()` on a running generation task actually do?","options":["Kills the task immediately and skips any cleanup code","Sends a SIGTERM to the thread running the coroutine","Schedules a `CancelledError` to be raised at the task's next `await`, so `finally` blocks run and can close upstream streams","Marks the task as done and returns its partial result"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cancellation is cooperative: the exception is delivered at the next suspension point, cleanup in `finally` runs, and code must re-raise rather than swallow it. That is exactly what lets the task close the provider stream on its way out."},{"q":"What happens to an aborted sequence in a continuous-batching engine such as vLLM?","options":["Its KV cache is kept until `max_tokens` would have been reached, in case the client reconnects","It finishes decoding in the background and the output is discarded","It is removed from the running batch and its KV blocks are released for other sequences","It is moved to a low-priority queue and completed when the GPU is idle"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The point of propagating cancellation is to reclaim capacity now. The engine drops the sequence at the next scheduling step and frees its memory, which is why abandoned requests only cost capacity when the abort never reaches the engine."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/backpressure","sources":["Little, 1961 — A Proof for the Queuing Formula L = λW","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload; Addressing Cascading Failures)","Official docs: vLLM (engine arguments and metrics)"],"why":"An inference server has a fixed capacity. The running batch is capped by KV-cache memory (`max_num_seqs`\nand free cache blocks in vLLM) and the rate at which it emits tokens is capped by memory bandwidth.\nArrivals do not know this. When the arrival rate λ exceeds the service rate μ for even a few seconds, the\ndifference has to accumulate somewhere.\n\nThe naive design, accept every connection and park it in an unbounded queue, moves the problem rather\nthan solving it. Little's law, L = λW, says the number of requests in the system equals arrival rate\ntimes time-in-system. If the queue is unbounded, W grows without limit, and every queued request\neventually outlives its client's timeout. The client retries, which raises λ further on a system that\nwas already saturated. Meanwhile the GPU keeps prefilling 8k-token prompts for connections nobody is\nlistening to, so *useful* throughput falls exactly when demand is highest. That is the cascading-failure\npattern, and LLM workloads make it worse because each unit of work is expensive and long-lived.\n\n\"Just autoscale\" is the second naive answer. Adding a GPU node means pulling an image, loading tens of\ngigabytes of weights and warming up, which takes minutes; the spike is either over or the service has\nalready collapsed. Backpressure is what keeps the system serving *some* traffic correctly while scaling\ncatches up. The principle: fail fast and selectively at the edge, where rejection costs microseconds,\nrather than slowly and universally in the middle, where every failure has already consumed GPU time.","how":"### Where the pressure originates\nIn a continuous-batching engine the scheduler holds a running batch and a waiting queue. Each decode\nstep it tries to admit waiting requests; admission fails when the sequence count would exceed\n`max_num_seqs` or when no free KV blocks remain. When a *running* request needs a block and none is\nfree, the engine preempts a sequence (swapping or recomputing its cache), which is the first sign of\noverload. Engines expose these as metrics, for instance vLLM's running and waiting request gauges and\nits KV-cache usage percentage. Queue wait time, not GPU utilisation, is the pressure signal: a GPU at\n95% with an empty queue is healthy; at 95% with a queue growing by eight requests a second it is minutes\nfrom collapse.\n\n### Propagating it upstream\nA request path is client → gateway → application → engine, and each hop must do two things: bound its\nown buffer, and translate \"I am full\" into something the previous hop understands. HTTP already has the\nvocabulary: 429 means *this caller* exceeded its allowance, 503 means *the server* is out of capacity;\nboth carry `Retry-After`. Two rules follow from the economics. The rejection decision must be made\nbefore any GPU work starts, because rejecting after prefill wastes the most expensive part of the\nrequest. And the decision must be cheap, ideally a counter check, because under overload you make it\nthousands of times a second.\n\n### Admission policies, simplest first\n1. **Concurrency limit.** A semaphore sized from Little's law: at 4 requests/s and 8 s mean latency you\n   need about 32 in flight; set the limit near the engine's real capacity and reject beyond it. Simple,\n   but one number cannot describe a mix of 500-token and 50,000-token requests.\n2. **Bounded queue with a wait budget.** Allow a short queue, but reject any request that has waited\n   longer than a budget (say 1–2 s for interactive traffic). This is the CoDel insight from network\n   queue management: control queue *delay*, not queue *length*, because length is meaningless when\n   service times vary 100× between requests.\n3. **Token-aware admission.** The real cost is tokens. Admit against a budget of prompt tokens plus\n   `max_tokens`, because one 100k-token document request displaces fifty chat turns and a request\n   count hides that.\n4. **Priority shedding.** When over capacity, drop batch and background work before interactive\n   traffic, because the cost of a dropped request differs by an order of magnitude between them.\n5. **Adaptive limits.** Measure the latency gradient and shrink the limit when it rises, the TCP-Vegas\n   idea used by adaptive concurrency libraries, because any static limit drifts as the prompt mix and\n   the model change.\n\n### Streaming and slow consumers\nStreaming adds pressure in the reverse direction. The server produces tokens faster than a slow client\nreads them, so the application buffers; the engine keeps generating and holding KV memory for a consumer\nthat may have gone away. Bound the per-connection buffer, detect disconnects, and cancel the generation\n(see cancellation), otherwise capacity leaks to dead streams.\n\n### The caller's half of the loop\nA rejection only relieves pressure if the caller slows down. Well-behaved clients honour `Retry-After`,\nback off with jitter, and cap retries with a budget (see retry strategies); a good internal client also\nshrinks its own concurrency when it sees 503s and grows it slowly on success (additive increase,\nmultiplicative decrease, the same rule TCP uses), because that converges on the server's real capacity\nwithout either side knowing the number in advance. If your callers are your own services, enforce this\nin the shared client library; if they are external, assume some will retry immediately and size the\nedge rejection path to absorb it. Three controls are easy to confuse here: *rate limiting* caps one\ncaller's share regardless of server health, *backpressure* is the signal that the server as a whole is\nfull, and *load shedding* is the act of dropping work once that signal fires. A system needs all three.\n\n### Queues as deliberate backpressure\nFor non-interactive work a durable queue with a bounded consumer pool turns rejection into latency:\nproducers enqueue freely, consumers pull at the GPU's pace. This only works if the queue has a maximum\ndepth or age; an unbounded queue is the original problem wearing a different hat.","example":"An admission gate in front of an engine: a semaphore sized from Little's law, a wait budget after which\nthe request is shed with a `503` and `Retry-After`, a disconnect check so no GPU work is done for a\ncaller who left while queued, and the queue-wait histogram that is the real pressure signal.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request, Response\n\napp = FastAPI()\nMAX_CONCURRENT = 32      # Little's law: ~4 req/s × ~8 s latency, trimmed to the engine's real capacity\nWAIT_BUDGET_S = 1.5      # longest an interactive request may queue before we shed it\nsem = asyncio.Semaphore(MAX_CONCURRENT)\n\n@app.post(\"/v1/generate\")\nasync def generate(req: Request):\n    body = await req.json()\n    t0 = time.monotonic()\n    try:\n        await asyncio.wait_for(sem.acquire(), timeout=WAIT_BUDGET_S)\n    except asyncio.TimeoutError:\n        # Shed at the edge: costs us microseconds, and tells the caller exactly what to do.\n        raise HTTPException(503, \"overloaded\", headers={\"Retry-After\": \"2\"})\n    queue_wait_ms = (time.monotonic() - t0) * 1000   # export as a histogram: this IS the pressure signal\n    try:\n        if await req.is_disconnected():               # caller gave up while queued: do no GPU work\n            return Response(status_code=499)          # nginx's \"client closed request\" convention\n        return await call_engine(body)               # vLLM / provider call, with its own timeout\n    finally:\n        sem.release()\n```\n\nThe two numbers to tune are the concurrency limit (start from Little's law, then lower it until the\nengine's waiting queue stays near zero under load) and the wait budget (from the product's latency\nbudget: if users abandon at 5 s and generation takes 3 s, queueing longer than 1.5–2 s is pointless).\nIn production the semaphore is usually token-weighted and per-priority, and the same shape appears\ninside the engine as `--max-num-seqs`.","tradeoffs":["Shedding early converts some requests into fast 503s instead of all requests into slow timeouts; it flips when callers cannot retry (synchronous webhooks, batch jobs with no scheduler), where a longer queue with a strict age limit is kinder.","A static concurrency limit is predictable and trivial to reason about, but it is tuned for one prompt mix; when 50k-token documents start arriving it is either too high (OOM, preemption) or too low (idle GPU), and token-aware or adaptive limits cost that simplicity back.","Queue-delay control keeps latency bounded regardless of request size, but the wait budget is a product decision that must be revisited whenever generation length or the latency SLO changes.","A durable queue hides overload from producers entirely, which is right for pipelines and wrong for chat: once a reply takes longer than a user will wait, finishing it is wasted GPU time, so interactive paths need explicit rejection."],"pitfalls":["Unbounded in-memory queues, which many frameworks give you by default; the symptom is p99 latency climbing for minutes with a flat error rate, then an out-of-memory crash with no 5xx warning before it.","Watching queue length instead of queue time; the symptom is a \"healthy\" queue depth of ten while p99 explodes, because those ten are long-document requests.","Rejecting after expensive work (after retrieval, prompt assembly or, worst, after prefill); the symptom is a GPU at 100% while completed requests per second falls.","Omitting `Retry-After`, or clients ignoring it; the symptom is a sawtooth of load spikes at a fixed interval after each shedding episode as every rejected client retries together.","Wiring load shedding into the load balancer's health check; the symptom is a total outage during a partial overload, because every instance reports unhealthy at once and is removed. Keep liveness separate from load."],"when":"Every boundary that fronts a GPU or a metered provider quota needs explicit backpressure: the gateway in\nfront of your API, the application in front of the engine, and the engine's own scheduler limits. Reach\nfor the simple semaphore-plus-wait-budget first, add token weighting when prompt lengths vary widely, and\nadd priority tiers when batch and interactive traffic share a deployment. Measure queue wait time,\nrejection rate, in-flight tokens and KV-cache usage, and alert on wait time rather than on errors because\nit moves first. In day-to-day work it shows up as the question \"what happens when traffic doubles for\nten minutes\"; in interviews, the expected answer walks from Little's law to bounded queues to\n503-with-Retry-After, and distinguishes that from rate limiting, which protects against a single caller\nrather than against aggregate load.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the admission gate in the API layer, sizes `max_num_seqs` and queue limits on the engine, exports queue-wait histograms, and tests the path by load-testing past capacity.","architect":"Decides where in the chain rejection happens, which traffic classes are shed first, and how the latency SLO translates into a queue wait budget and autoscaling triggers.","fde":"Diagnoses a customer's \"the model got slow at 2 pm\" incident from queue depth and preemption metrics, and negotiates priority tiers between their batch pipeline and their chat product on shared GPUs."},"check":{"q":"Your vLLM-backed API serves 4 requests per second at 8 seconds mean latency. A campaign triples\ntraffic for ten minutes. Walk through what happens with no backpressure, then design the controls you\nwould add and the numbers you would set.","a":"By Little's law steady state needs about 32 concurrent requests; at 12 requests per second it\nneeds 96, so with the engine capped near 32 the queue grows by roughly 8 requests every second. After a\nminute some 480 requests are waiting and a new arrival faces roughly two minutes of queueing (the wait\ngrows by about two seconds every second, since 8 join per second and 4 leave); clients time out at 30 s\nand retry, raising the arrival rate further, while the GPU prefills prompts for abandoned connections.\nUseful throughput falls below the original 4 per second: that is the collapse. With backpressure: keep\nthe engine limit near its true capacity, put a semaphore with a 1–2 s wait budget in front of it, return\n503 with Retry-After for the rest, cancel generation on client disconnect, and shed batch traffic before\ninteractive. Roughly two thirds of requests get a fast rejection, but the third that is served sees\nnormal latency, and queue-wait time becomes the autoscaling trigger. A strong answer also mentions\ntoken-aware admission if prompt lengths vary, and notes that per-tenant rate limiting is a separate\ncontrol for a different problem."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should an admission controller for LLM requests bound queue *time* rather than queue *length*?","options":["Queue length cannot be measured accurately in an asynchronous server","Service times vary by orders of magnitude with prompt and output length, so the same queue length can mean one second or two minutes of waiting","HTTP clients can only communicate time-based limits, not length-based ones","Queue time is cheaper to compute than queue length"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A queue of ten short chat turns drains in a second; ten 50k-token document requests take minutes. Length hides this, while measured wait time directly tracks what the caller experiences, which is why delay-based control is the right signal."},{"q":"A server at aggregate capacity must reject a request from a caller who is well within their own quota. Which response is correct?","options":["429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After","503 Service Unavailable with Retry-After","408 Request Timeout","200 with an empty completion and a warning header"],"answer":1,"explanation":"429 states that the caller exceeded their allowance, which is false here and would send them to the wrong fix. 503 says the server is the bottleneck, and Retry-After tells clients when to come back without a synchronized retry storm."},{"q":"Which is the primary reason an unbounded queue in front of a GPU server is harmful rather than merely wasteful?","options":["It makes the engine's continuous batching less efficient","Queued requests consume KV-cache memory before they are scheduled","The server keeps doing expensive work for requests whose clients have already timed out, while their retries add new load","HTTP keep-alive connections are limited to a fixed number per process"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once wait time exceeds client timeouts, every completed request is wasted and every timed-out client retries, so useful throughput falls as load rises. Waiting requests do not yet hold KV memory; the damage is the wasted GPU time and the feedback loop."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/fallbacks","sources":["Nygard, 2007 — Release It! (circuit breaker and stability patterns)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Addressing Cascading Failures)","Official docs: Anthropic API errors and rate limits"],"why":"Hosted models fail in ways a retry cannot fix: a provider returns 529 overloaded or 429 for minutes\nduring a demand spike, a region has an incident, a deployment pushes elevated 5xx, a model is deprecated,\na content filter refuses, latency triples under load. Self-hosted engines add their own: a GPU node dies,\na rollout ships a bad build, a long-context burst preempts everything. A retry assumes the target will be\nhealthy a second later; during an incident it is not, and three attempts against a 30-second timeout is a\n90-second path to the same error. The alternative to a fallback is showing the user an error, which is\nsometimes right (a medical triage assistant should fail closed) but for most product surfaces a slightly\nworse answer beats none.\n\nThe naive implementation, a `try/except` that calls model B, fails for reasons that only show up in\nproduction. Model B may not support your tool schema, JSON mode or context length, and you discover this\nwhen A is already down. Your evaluation suite never ran against B, so quality in fallback mode is\nunknown. When A fails for all your traffic at once, B's rate limit, which you never provisioned, is\nexceeded within seconds: the fallback storm. A timeout on A may have fired after A executed a side effect\ninside an agent loop, so B duplicates it. And if tokens were already streamed from A, you cannot switch\nmid-sentence. Fallbacks are a designed subsystem, not an exception handler.","how":"### The ladder\nOrder alternatives by behavioural distance from the primary, not by cost. A typical ladder: the same\nmodel on a different endpoint or region (the major models are available through more than one cloud,\nwhich keeps prompts and evals valid); a sibling model from the same family one size down; a different\nvendor's model with a translated request; a non-generative degraded answer (cached response, retrieval\nresults without synthesis, a template); and finally an explicit error with a retry affordance. Each rung\ncosts quality, so the ladder is built to exhaust the cheapest-in-quality options first.\n\n### Trigger classification\nOnly some failures justify a fallback. Transient capacity errors (429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529 (Anthropic's\noverloaded status), connection failures, timeouts before the first token) justify one retry on the same target and then a\nfallback. Request-specific errors (400 for a malformed body, a context-length overflow, an unsupported\nparameter) will fail identically on any equivalent target; the one exception is a context overflow,\nwhich may legitimately route to a model with a larger window. Authentication and billing errors are\nconfiguration faults and should page a human rather than fail over. Latency is also a trigger: if time\nto first token exceeds a budget, cancel and try the next rung, because the user cannot tell the\ndifference between slow and down. A softer variant is a *hedge*: once the primary has been silent past\nroughly its p95 time to first token, start the fallback in parallel, keep whichever produces a first\ntoken first and cancel the other. Because only the slowest few percent of calls spawn a hedge, the extra\nload is small, but each hedge is billed on both targets until the loser is cancelled.\n\n### Circuit breaker per target\nWithout a breaker, every request during an outage pays the primary's full timeout before falling back,\nso latency doubles at exactly the moment the system is under stress. A breaker tracks the failure *rate*\nover a sliding window of recent calls per target, opens when that rate crosses a threshold (with a\nminimum call count, so three failures out of three at 2 a.m. do not trip it), routes straight to the\nfallback while open, and after a cooldown moves to *half-open*, letting a single probe through whose\nresult decides whether to close or reopen. It converts \"try and wait\" into \"skip, because we already\nknow\".\n\nHow much a breaker buys depends on how failures arrive. A slow failure (a 30-second timeout, a stalled\nstream) is what makes \"try the primary first\" expensive, so breakers pay off most there. A fast failure\n(a 529 or 503 returned in tens of milliseconds) costs almost nothing to attempt, so for a *partial*\noutage it is often better to leave the breaker closed and let each request try the primary once, keeping\nthe healthy fraction of capacity in use. Count-of-consecutive-failures breakers, the common naive\nversion, behave badly here: at a 20% independent error rate five failures in a row happen only once every few\nthousand calls, so the breaker almost never opens, while at a 60% rate it flaps.\n\n### Compatibility layer\nThe fallback must receive an equivalent request. That means normalising message formats, translating\ntool schemas, mapping parameter names and ranges, re-checking that the prompt fits the target's context\nwindow using the target's tokenizer, and knowing whether structured output is supported. Gateways\n(self-built, or products such as LiteLLM, OpenRouter or Portkey) exist largely to own this translation.\nPrompts should be evaluated on every rung; where a model needs a variant prompt, version it alongside the\nprimary's.\n\n### One deadline for the whole chain\nGive the request a single deadline, for example 20 seconds, and hand each rung the remainder rather than\nits own timeout. Otherwise chained timeouts add up to a wait no user will tolerate.\n\n### Keeping the backup real\nProvision quota on the fallback deliberately and route a trickle of live traffic (a few percent) to it,\nso its credentials, quota, prompts and evals are continuously verified. An unexercised fallback is an\nuntested one.\n\n### Observability and streaming\nTag every response with the target that served it and the reason for any fallback; alert when the\nfallback rate rises, because quality regressions in fallback mode are invisible otherwise. For streaming,\ndecide before the first byte: a failure after tokens have been emitted either restarts the response\nunder a new message id (so the client can discard the partial one) or surfaces an error with a retry\nbutton.","example":"The chain below tries each target in order, skips any whose breaker is open, gives each the remaining\nshare of one deadline, refuses to fail over on request-specific errors, and labels the response with what\nserved it. Per-target retries live inside `call`, where the SDK already does them.\n\n```python\nimport time\nfrom collections import deque\n\nclass Breaker:\n    \"\"\"Opens when >= `threshold` of the last `window` calls failed; one half-open probe per cooldown.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, window=20, min_calls=10, threshold=0.5, cooldown_s=30):\n        self.results, self.min_calls, self.threshold = deque(maxlen=window), min_calls, threshold\n        self.cooldown_s, self.state, self.open_until = cooldown_s, \"closed\", 0.0\n    def available(self):\n        if self.state == \"open\" and time.monotonic() >= self.open_until:\n            self.state = \"half_open\"; return True      # exactly one probe gets through\n        return self.state == \"closed\"\n    def record(self, ok):\n        if self.state == \"half_open\":                  # the probe's result decides\n            self.state = \"closed\" if ok else \"open\"\n            self.results.clear(); self.open_until = time.monotonic() + self.cooldown_s\n            return\n        self.results.append(ok)\n        n, fails = len(self.results), self.results.count(False)\n        if n >= self.min_calls and fails / n >= self.threshold:\n            self.state, self.open_until = \"open\", time.monotonic() + self.cooldown_s\n\nTRANSIENT = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529, \"timeout\", \"connection\"}\nLADDER = [(\"anthropic\", \"primary-model\"), (\"bedrock\", \"primary-model\"), (\"anthropic\", \"smaller-model\")]\nbreakers = {t: Breaker() for t in LADDER}\n\ndef generate(req, deadline_s=20.0):\n    deadline, last_err = time.monotonic() + deadline_s, None\n    for target in LADDER:\n        remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()\n        if remaining <= 0 or not breakers[target].available():\n            continue\n        try:\n            resp = call(target, adapt(req, target), timeout=min(remaining, 10.0))\n        except ProviderError as e:\n            if e.code not in TRANSIENT:                # malformed request, policy, auth: no rung fixes this\n                breakers[target].record(True); raise   # the target was healthy; our request was not\n            breakers[target].record(False); last_err = e; continue\n        breakers[target].record(True)\n        resp.meta = {\"served_by\": target, \"fallback\": target != LADDER[0]}\n        return resp\n    return degraded_response(req, last_err)            # cached/template answer, or an explicit error\n```\n\nTrace it through an incident. With a 50% threshold over 20 calls, a primary returning 529s on 20% of\nrequests stays closed: each failing request pays one fast 529 and moves to the second endpoint, and the\n80% that succeed keep using the primary. If the primary starts timing out on most calls, the window\ncrosses 50% after at least ten calls, the breaker opens, and for the next 30 seconds every request goes\nstraight to the second endpoint without paying a 10-second timeout; one probe then tests recovery. The\nnumbers are starting points to tune against your own error and latency distributions.","tradeoffs":["Availability rises, but quality in fallback mode is lower and, unless you evaluate every rung, unknown; when the fallback cannot meet the product's accuracy bar, failing closed is the better design.","Breakers cut incident latency sharply but can trip on a noisy error burst and divert healthy traffic; a low threshold and short window react fast but flap, a high threshold and minimum call count are stable but leave users paying timeouts for longer, and the right point moves toward \"react fast\" the slower the target's failures are.","A second vendor removes correlated outage risk but doubles compliance scope (data residency, DPAs), prompt maintenance and eval cost; a second region of the same provider is cheaper when the risk you fear is regional rather than vendor-wide.","Degraded non-LLM answers keep the interface alive, but presenting a cached or templated response as fresh can be worse than an honest error for time-sensitive questions."],"pitfalls":["A fallback that has never served traffic; the symptom is fallback rate and error rate spiking together during the first real incident, because the key expired, the quota was zero or the schema never matched.","Failing over on non-transient errors; the symptom is every request costing two or three calls and still failing, with identical 400s from each target in the trace.","Independent timeouts per rung instead of one shared deadline; the symptom is p99 latency during incidents equal to the sum of all the timeouts.","A fallback storm; the symptom is the secondary provider returning 429 within seconds of the primary's incident, because the whole fleet moved at once onto unprovisioned quota.","Silent model swaps; the symptom is JSON parse errors or refusal-rate changes that correlate with the fallback rate, because output formatting quirks differ between models and the downstream parser was tuned to one.","Falling back after a side effect; the symptom is duplicate tickets or emails whenever the primary times out mid-tool-call, which is an idempotency problem the fallback exposes."],"when":"Add a fallback wherever an LLM call sits on a user-facing path with an availability target the single\nprovider cannot meet alone, and wherever a cheaper degraded answer is genuinely acceptable. Do not add one\nwhere a worse answer is worse than no answer, or where the \"fallback\" would silently change compliance\nposture. Start with the same model on a second endpoint, because that preserves prompts and evals; add a\nsmaller model only after evaluating it; add a non-LLM degraded response as the last rung. Measure fallback\nrate, per-target error rate, latency per rung and quality by served-by tag. In interviews, the expected\ndepth is: classify the trigger, break the circuit, share one deadline, keep the fallback compatible and\nwarm, and explain why streaming and side effects make \"just try B\" unsafe.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the target ladder, breaker state and request adapters, writes evals that run against every rung, and tags traces with the serving target and the fallback reason.","architect":"Owns the policy: which failures fail over, which fail closed, which models are approved as fallbacks, and how much quota is reserved on each backup.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer which degraded behaviour is acceptable for their use case, and verifies that their data-residency rules still hold when traffic moves to a second region or vendor."},"check":{"q":"Your primary provider starts returning 529 overloaded errors for about 20% of requests for half an\nhour. Design the fallback behaviour for a customer-support chat product and explain what you would\ndeliberately avoid doing.","a":"First, classify: 529 is transient and, importantly, fast; it comes back in milliseconds, so\nattempting the primary costs little. At a 20% error rate the primary is still serving most traffic, so I\nwould *not* want the breaker to open and push all load onto the backup; a rate-based breaker with a\nthreshold well above 20% stays closed, and each failing request makes one quick attempt (or one short\njittered retry) and then moves to the next rung. The breaker exists for the case where the primary\ndegrades into timeouts or a majority error rate. Route to the\nsame model on a second endpoint or region first, because prompts and evals carry over; only if that is\nalso degraded, step down to a smaller model that has already been evaluated on support transcripts.\nConfirm the backup has quota for about a fifth of peak traffic, since that is what will move. Give\nthe whole chain one deadline of a few seconds to first token, since chat users abandon quickly. Decide\nbefore streaming starts; a mid-stream failure shows the partial answer with a retry button rather than\nsilently restarting. Tag every reply with served-by and watch quality metrics in fallback mode. Avoid:\nfailing over on 400s, letting the entire fleet shift onto a backup with no pre-provisioned quota, and\nfailing over tool-executing turns without idempotency keys, which would duplicate ticket creation."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why put a circuit breaker in front of the primary model instead of simply catching its errors and calling the fallback?","options":["Breakers are required to satisfy provider terms of service during outages","Without one, every request during an outage waits for the primary's full timeout before falling back, so latency doubles when the system is already under stress","Catching exceptions is unreliable for streaming responses","Breakers reduce the number of distinct fallback targets you need"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A try/except still issues the doomed primary call. A breaker remembers recent failures and skips the primary while open, so fallback requests are fast instead of adding a full timeout to each response; the gain is largest when failures are slow timeouts rather than instant error codes."},{"q":"Which failure should NOT trigger failover to a second provider?","options":["A 503 from the primary provider","A timeout before the first token arrives","A 400 caused by a malformed tool schema in your request","A 529 overloaded error"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A malformed request fails identically everywhere, so failing over doubles cost and latency for nothing; the fix is in your code. The other three are capacity or transient faults where another target can genuinely succeed."},{"q":"What is a \"fallback storm\"?","options":["A loop where the fallback model's errors trigger retries against the primary","The whole fleet shifting onto the backup at once when the primary fails, exceeding quota the backup never had provisioned","Repeated breaker trips caused by a noisy error threshold","Clients retrying with no jitter after a 503"],"answer":1,"explanation":"During a primary outage every request moves simultaneously to the fallback. If its rate limit was sized for a trickle, it fails within seconds, turning one outage into two. Pre-provisioned quota and load shedding prevent it."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/retry-strategies","sources":["Brooker, 2015 — Exponential Backoff and Jitter (AWS Architecture Blog)","Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering (Handling Overload)","Official docs: Anthropic SDK (retries and timeouts)","Dean & Barroso, 2013 — The Tail at Scale"],"why":"Provider APIs fail a small fraction of the time by design: 429 when shared capacity is tight, 529 or 503\nwhen overloaded, 5xx during deployments, connection resets through proxies, and timeouts on long\ngenerations. At a 1% independent failure rate and 100,000 calls a day that is 1,000 user-visible errors;\na single well-placed retry cuts it to about 10, because the probability of two independent failures is\n0.01%. That arithmetic is why every SDK retries by default.\n\nThe arithmetic breaks when failures are correlated, and during an incident they always are. If the\nprovider is saturated, each retry is new load on a system that is already dropping requests. Three\nimmediate retries quadruple the offered load; stack an SDK that retries twice (3 attempts), a service that\nretries three times (4) and a browser that retries three times (4), and one user click becomes up to\n3 × 4 × 4 = 48 attempts. That is the retry storm, and it can turn a 30-second blip into an outage that\nlasts many minutes.\n\nLLM calls raise the stakes further. Each attempt costs seconds and real money, a timed-out call may have\nalready done its work (or, in an agent, executed a tool with side effects), and a streaming response\ncannot be transparently retried once tokens have reached the user. So the design question is not whether\nto retry but exactly which failures, how many times, with what spacing, under what aggregate limit, and\nwith what guarantee that a duplicate is harmless.","how":"### Classify first\nRetry: 408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529 (Anthropic's overloaded status), connection errors and, only\nwhen the operation is idempotent, timeouts. Do not retry: 400 (malformed request, context-length\noverflow), 401 and 403 (credentials), 404 (wrong model id), 413, 422 and content-policy refusals; they\nfail identically next time. A 429 needs a second look: a per-minute rate limit clears in seconds, a\nmonthly quota exhaustion does not, and `Retry-After` tells you which.\n\n### Exponential backoff with jitter\nThe wait before attempt *n* grows geometrically, `min(cap, base × 2ⁿ)`, so a persistent fault quickly\nspreads retries thin. Pure exponential backoff has a flaw: every client that failed at the same instant\nretries at the same instant, so the load arrives in synchronized waves at 1 s, 2 s, 4 s. Jitter breaks\nthe synchrony. With *full jitter* the wait is uniform between 0 and the exponential ceiling; with\n*decorrelated jitter* it is uniform between the base and three times the previous wait, capped.\nBrooker's simulations showed both complete the same work with far fewer total calls than un-jittered\nbackoff, which is why full jitter is a common library default.\n\n### Honour the server's schedule\nIf the response carries `Retry-After`, use it instead of your own schedule: the server knows when\ncapacity returns, and waiting less is wasted load that may count against you. The header may be a\nnumber of seconds or an HTTP date, so parse both, and still cap it: a `Retry-After` longer than the\nrequest's remaining deadline means \"give up or fall back now\", not \"sleep\". Providers also return\nremaining-quota and reset headers; an outbound rate limiter can read them to slow down *before* the\nfirst 429.\n\n### Caps and budgets\nPer request: a maximum attempt count (two or three) and a total deadline that includes the backoff\nsleeps, so a 30-second provider timeout cannot become a two-minute wait. Per client: a retry budget,\nretries allowed only while they stay below roughly 10% of recent requests (the figure the SRE book uses).\nThe per-request cap bounds the worst case for one call; the budget bounds aggregate amplification during\nan incident to about 1.1× instead of 3×. A common implementation is itself a token bucket: every\nsuccessful request deposits 0.1 tokens, every retry spends one, and retries stop when the bucket is\nempty. In normal operation the bucket stays full and nothing changes; in an outage, where nothing\nsucceeds, it drains after a few retries and the client degrades to one attempt per request, which is\nexactly the behaviour you want from a fleet hammering a saturated provider.\n\nWorked numbers make the point. A fleet sends 200 requests per second; the provider goes fully down.\nWith three attempts per request and no budget, it receives 600 requests per second while it is trying\nto recover. With a 10% budget it receives about 220. As the provider recovers, every request it rejects\nfrom the first fleet comes back twice more, so partial recovery is met with up to triple load and can\nstay pinned in overload; the second fleet's offered load is near its real demand from the start, and\nsuccesses refill the budget only as the provider actually heals.\n\n### Retry at one layer\nKnow what the SDK does. The official Anthropic and OpenAI SDKs retry twice by default with exponential\nbackoff and honour `Retry-After` (check the current defaults for your version). Either disable that and\nown the policy, or let it be the only retrying layer; two layers multiply.\n\n### LLM-specific rules\n- Prefer a tight time-to-first-token timeout plus a retry over a long total timeout plus a retry, because\n  stalled requests rarely recover and the user is waiting.\n- Never retry a stream after the first token has been forwarded; restart it as a new message id so the\n  client can discard the partial one.\n- Give every retried operation an idempotency key, so a retried tool call does not send a second email.\n- Prompt caching makes retried prefill cheaper, since the retry reads the prefix the first attempt wrote,\n  provided the first attempt got far enough to write it (on Anthropic an entry becomes readable once the\n  first response begins).\n- A hedged request (start a second attempt once the first has exceeded the p95 latency and keep whichever\n  finishes first) cuts tail latency for a few percent of extra load, since only the slowest 5% of calls\n  spawn a hedge; firing it immediately would double the load. It needs cancellation of the loser to be\n  worth it (Dean and Barroso's \"tail at scale\" pattern).\n- A wrong answer is not a transient failure. Validation-driven regeneration is a separate loop with its\n  own bound and usually a changed prompt or temperature.","example":"The helper below decides by status code, prefers the server's `Retry-After`, otherwise sleeps with full\njitter under a cap, refuses to exceed the request's deadline, and consults a process-wide retry budget so\nthat an incident cannot multiply load. In an async service replace `time.sleep` with `await asyncio.sleep`.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nRETRYABLE = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, 529}\nBASE_S, CAP_S, MAX_ATTEMPTS, DEADLINE_S = 0.5, 8.0, 3, 30.0\n\nclass RetryBudget:\n    \"\"\"Process-wide: retries may not exceed `ratio` of recent requests (use a sliding window in prod).\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, ratio=0.1): self.ratio, self.requests, self.retries = ratio, 0, 0\n    def allow(self): return self.retries < self.ratio * max(self.requests, 10)\n\nbudget = RetryBudget()\n\ndef call_with_retry(send):\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + DEADLINE_S\n    budget.requests += 1\n    for attempt in range(MAX_ATTEMPTS):\n        try:\n            return send()                                   # raises ApiError(status, retry_after)\n        except ApiError as e:\n            last_try = attempt == MAX_ATTEMPTS - 1\n            if e.status not in RETRYABLE or last_try or not budget.allow():\n                raise\n            if e.retry_after is not None:                   # the server knows when capacity returns\n                delay = min(float(e.retry_after), CAP_S * 4)\n            else:\n                delay = random.uniform(0, min(CAP_S, BASE_S * 2 ** attempt))   # full jitter\n            if time.monotonic() + delay > deadline:\n                raise                                       # a retry that cannot finish in time is waste\n            budget.retries += 1\n            time.sleep(delay)\n```","tradeoffs":["Retries raise success rates when failures are independent but amplify load when they are correlated; the retry budget is what makes the trade safe, and without it the first incident proves the point.","A higher backoff cap is gentler on the provider but worse for the user; for interactive features cap at one or two short retries and then fall back, for batch pipelines cap in minutes and let the queue absorb it.","Hedged requests cut tail latency but add load (a few percent when fired at the p95, up to double when fired immediately) and need cancellation; they pay off only when time-to-first-token variance is high and the call is cheap relative to the user's time.","Retrying after timeouts recovers from stalls but risks duplicated effects; without idempotency keys the safe choice is to surface the timeout rather than retry."],"pitfalls":["Retrying 400s and context-length errors; the symptom is the identical error three times in a row in the logs and triple the latency on every such request.","No jitter; the symptom is load spikes at 1, 2 and 4 seconds after an incident starts, visible as a sawtooth on the provider's error graph.","Stacked retries across SDK, service and client; the symptom is one user action producing 20 or more provider calls in a single trace.","Ignoring `Retry-After`; the symptom is long streaks of 429s whose remaining-quota headers never recover because your retries keep spending the refill.","`time.sleep` inside an async handler; the symptom is every in-flight request stalling whenever one request backs off.","Retrying inside an open stream; the symptom is duplicated paragraphs appearing in the user interface."],"when":"Every outbound LLM call needs an explicit retry policy, even if that policy is \"the SDK's two attempts and\nnothing else\". Choose numbers from the latency budget: an interactive path gets one or two retries with a\nsmall cap and then a fallback; a background job gets more attempts, a larger cap and a queue to wait in.\nPair retries with idempotency keys whenever the call has side effects, with a circuit breaker when the\ndependency can be down for minutes, and with a rate limiter so retries never push you over quota. Measure\nretry rate, success-after-retry rate and the amplification factor (provider calls per user request). In\ninterviews, the expected answer names classification, exponential backoff with full jitter,\n`Retry-After`, per-request caps plus a global budget, and explains why layering retries is dangerous.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures or replaces SDK retries, implements jittered backoff and a budget, and makes sure async code never blocks the event loop while waiting.","architect":"Sets the policy per traffic class, decides which layer owns retries, and defines the amplification factor the system may impose on providers during incidents.","fde":"Traces a customer's \"the API is flaky\" complaint to stacked retries in their integration and tunes their client against the provider's Retry-After and quota headers."},"check":{"q":"You are shipping a chat feature that calls a hosted LLM through an SDK that already retries twice.\nDescribe the retry policy you would deploy end to end and justify each number.","a":"I would make the SDK the only retrying layer for transport-level failures, keep its default of two\nattempts, and set its timeout from the product's latency budget rather than the long default, since a\nchat user leaves after a few seconds. Retries apply only to 408, 429, 5xx, 529 and connection errors; a\n400 or a context-length error goes straight to the caller. Backoff is exponential with full jitter and\nhonours Retry-After. Above the SDK I add a circuit breaker and a fallback rather than more retries, so an\noutage costs one short wait and then a different target, and a process-wide retry budget of about 10% so\nthat an incident cannot triple our load on the provider. Every turn carries an idempotency key, which\nmakes retrying a timed-out tool call safe, and streaming responses are never retried after the first\ntoken; a failure there becomes a new message. I would monitor the amplification factor to prove the\npolicy holds."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why add random jitter to exponential backoff?","options":["It shortens the average wait so users see lower latency","It desynchronizes clients that failed at the same moment, so their retries do not arrive in simultaneous waves","It is required for the server's Retry-After header to be respected","It prevents the exponential delay from exceeding the cap"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without jitter every client that failed together retries together at 1, 2 and 4 seconds, recreating the overload. Spreading each wait uniformly over the window flattens those waves; Brooker's simulations showed it also reduces total calls."},{"q":"Which of these should be retried against the same endpoint without changing the request?","options":["A 503 Service Unavailable","A 400 reporting that the prompt exceeds the context window","A 401 Unauthorized","A 422 reporting an invalid tool schema"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 503 is a capacity signal that will likely clear. The other three describe defects in the request or credentials that will fail identically on every attempt, so retrying only adds latency and cost."},{"q":"What does a client-wide retry budget provide that a per-request maximum attempt count does not?","options":["A guarantee that no single request waits longer than the deadline","Protection against retrying non-idempotent operations","A bound on aggregate load amplification when every request is failing at once","Automatic detection of which errors are transient"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Three attempts per request still means 3× load when all requests fail together. A budget limits retries to a fraction of recent traffic, so during an incident the extra load stays near 10% instead of 200%."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/rate-limiting","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic API rate limits","Official docs: OpenAI API rate limits","IETF HTTPAPI working group — RateLimit header fields for HTTP (draft)"],"why":"The resource behind an LLM call is shared, finite and expensive. A hosted provider gives your organisation\na quota, typically requests per minute and tokens per minute per model, and when it is exceeded the 429s\nhit every caller in the organisation, not just the one that caused it. A self-hosted GPU has a fixed token\nbudget per second. Without an inbound limiter, one customer's retry loop or one agent stuck in a tool\ncycle exhausts the shared allowance, every other customer sees errors, and the bill arrives anyway.\n\n\"Let the provider's 429 be our rate limiter\" is the naive design and it fails on four counts. The blast\nradius is the whole organisation. It is reactive: by the time the 429 arrives, the requests are already\nqueued and the latency already spent. It removes your ability to prioritise, because the provider does\nnot know which of your tenants matter. And it interacts badly with retries, since every rejected request\ncomes back as another.\n\n\"Limit requests per minute like any web API\" is the second naive design. One request can carry 200\ntokens or 200,000; a request limit is both gamed (pack more work per call) and useless for protecting the\nresource that actually runs out, which is tokens. Rate limiting for LLMs therefore has to be cost-aware,\nhas to estimate costs before they are known, and has to run in two directions at once.","how":"### Units\nProviders limit requests per minute, tokens per minute (sometimes input and output tokens separately,\nsometimes per day as well) and occasionally concurrency; the exact names and tiers vary by vendor and are\npublished in their docs. Your inbound limiter should mirror this: per-tenant requests, per-tenant tokens\nand, for agents, steps or dollars per task.\n\n### Algorithms, and why the token bucket wins here\n- **Token bucket.** A bucket of capacity *B* refills at *r* units per second; a request costing *c* is\n  admitted if the bucket holds at least *c*, which it then removes. It permits bursts up to *B* while\n  enforcing a long-run average of *r*, and its state is two numbers (level, last refill time). LLM\n  traffic is bursty and cost-weighted, which is exactly what this models; the \"tokens\" in the name map\n  directly onto LLM tokens if you set the units that way.\n- **Leaky bucket.** Requests drain at a fixed rate, smoothing output; equivalent in enforcement terms\n  (the GCRA formulation) but it reshapes bursts into delay rather than admitting them.\n- **Fixed window.** A counter per minute. Simple, but it allows 2× the limit across a window boundary,\n  since a caller can spend the full allowance at 0:59 and again at 1:00.\n- **Sliding window log.** Store every request's timestamp; exact, but memory grows with traffic.\n- **Sliding window counter.** Weight the previous window's count by its overlap with the current one;\n  constant memory, approximate, good enough for most inbound limits.\n\n### Reserve, then settle\nA request's cost is input tokens, which you can count or estimate before sending, plus output tokens,\nwhich you only learn when generation ends but which are bounded by `max_tokens`. The limiter therefore\n*reserves* input plus an output estimate at admission and *settles* when the response arrives, refunding\nthe difference. Reserving the full `max_tokens` never overshoots but under-uses quota when clients set\ngenerous limits; reserving an expected output length uses quota well but occasionally overshoots and\ntakes a provider 429. Which is right depends on how expensive an overshoot is for you. If you reserve an\nestimate, settlement must also *debit* when the actual exceeds it, letting the bucket go into debt that\nlater refill pays off; refunding only in one direction turns every underestimate into silent overspend.\nEither way, clients who set `max_tokens` far above their needs shrink everyone's headroom. Check how your\nprovider counts before sizing reservations: some count cached prompt tokens differently toward their\ninput-token limits, and the exact accounting is vendor-specific.\n\nThe numbers interact. In a bucket with 80,000 tokens of capacity, a request reserving a 20,000-token prompt plus\n`max_tokens` 4,000 takes 30% of the burst at once; if it actually generates 600 tokens, settlement\nreturns 3,400. Three such requests arriving together hold 72,000 of the 80,000 and the fourth waits for\nrefill (or for the first three to settle and refund), which is the limiter doing its job. A single 100,000-token request, by contrast, can never fit; the\nlimiter must reject it with a non-retryable error or the bucket must be sized above the largest request\nyou intend to accept.\n\n### Distributed enforcement\nSeveral application replicas share one provider quota, so the limiter's state must be shared, or the\nquota statically partitioned. Shared state lives in Redis with the check-and-decrement done atomically\nin a Lua script, because two replicas that read, decide and then write will both admit the last unit.\nStatic partitioning (each of three replicas gets a third) needs no coordination but wastes headroom\nwhenever load is uneven. A central gateway process is the third option and the usual home for\nper-tenant policy.\n\n### The response contract and the feedback loop\nInbound rejections return 429 with `Retry-After` and remaining-limit headers (a standard set of header\nnames is being defined by the IETF; providers currently use their own). Outbound, read the provider's\nremaining-quota and reset headers and slow down before the first 429, decreasing the local rate when\nheadroom is thin. Keep 429 for \"you exceeded your allowance\" and 503 for \"we are out of capacity\";\nclients fix them differently.\n\n### Fairness and layering\nSize per-tenant buckets from the plan, a global bucket from the provider quota minus a safety margin\n(running at around 80% leaves room for retries and bursts), and let high-priority traffic borrow from\nthe global bucket before low-priority traffic does. Multiple keys, workspaces or regions multiply quota\nwhen a single bucket is not enough.","example":"A cost-aware token bucket with a reservation step. Capacity is ten seconds of refill so short bursts are\nabsorbed, the refill rate is 80% of an illustrative provider limit, and unused reservations are refunded\nonce the real output length is known. In production the bucket lives in Redis with `try_reserve` as an\natomic script.\n\n```python\nimport time, threading\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Cost-aware limiter: `capacity` units, refilled continuously at `rate` units/second.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, capacity: float, rate: float):\n        self.capacity, self.rate = capacity, rate\n        self.level, self.updated = capacity, time.monotonic()\n        self.lock = threading.Lock()\n    def _refill(self):\n        now = time.monotonic()\n        self.level = min(self.capacity, self.level + (now - self.updated) * self.rate)\n        self.updated = now\n    def try_reserve(self, cost: float) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Reserve `cost` now. Returns 0 if admitted, else seconds until it would fit.\"\"\"\n        if cost > self.capacity:              # would never fit: reject outright, do not say \"retry later\"\n            raise ValueError(\"request larger than bucket capacity\")\n        with self.lock:\n            self._refill()\n            if self.level >= cost:\n                self.level -= cost\n                return 0.0\n            return (cost - self.level) / self.rate\n    def settle(self, reserved: float, actual: float):\n        with self.lock:                       # refund unused quota, or debit an overshoot (level may go\n            self.level = min(self.capacity, self.level + reserved - actual)   # negative = a debt)\n\n# Illustrative provider limit of 400k tokens/minute; run at 80% to leave headroom for retries.\nTPM = 400_000 * 0.8\ntpm_bucket = TokenBucket(capacity=TPM / 6, rate=TPM / 60)     # 10 s of burst, ~5,333 tokens/s\n\ndef call_llm(prompt_tokens: int, max_tokens: int):\n    reserved = prompt_tokens + max_tokens                  # safe estimate; expected output is the alternative\n    wait = tpm_bucket.try_reserve(reserved)\n    if wait > 0:\n        raise RateLimited(retry_after=round(wait, 1))      # inbound: 429 + Retry-After; outbound: sleep\n    resp = provider.generate(max_tokens=max_tokens)\n    tpm_bucket.settle(reserved, prompt_tokens + resp.usage.output_tokens)\n    return resp\n```","tradeoffs":["Token buckets admit bursts, which users like, but a burst that fits your bucket may still exceed the provider's own windowing; keep burst capacity below what the upstream tolerates.","Reserving `max_tokens` never overshoots but strands quota when clients over-specify; reserving an expected output uses quota fully but accepts occasional provider 429s, so pick by how costly an upstream rejection is.","A shared Redis limiter is exact across replicas at the price of a round trip and a dependency; static partitioning is free and sufficient when replicas are few and load is even.","Strict per-tenant limits guarantee fairness but block legitimate spikes; letting tenants borrow unused global headroom improves utilisation at the cost of weaker isolation."],"pitfalls":["Limiting requests only; the symptom is provider 429s while your requests-per-minute dashboard is green, because long prompts exhausted the token limit.","Fixed windows; the symptom is bursts of upstream 429s at minute boundaries as callers double-spend across the edge.","Non-atomic check-then-decrement across replicas; the symptom is overshoot that scales with replica count and vanishes in single-instance tests.","Never refunding reservations; the symptom is sustained throughput far below quota while the limiter reports itself full.","Bucket capacity smaller than the largest legitimate request; the symptom is a few long-document requests receiving `Retry-After` indefinitely while everything else flows, because they can never fit.","Limiting after expensive work (retrieval, prompt assembly); the symptom is CPU and vector-store load that does not fall when requests are rejected.","Counting tokens with the wrong tokenizer; the symptom is systematic under- or over-reservation that appears the day a new model is added.","Treating 429 and 503 as interchangeable; the symptom is clients that back off per-caller when the problem is global, or that hammer a saturated server believing they are under quota."],"when":"Put an inbound limiter in front of every LLM endpoint that serves more than one tenant or more than one\napplication, and an outbound limiter in front of every provider key that more than one process uses.\nStart with per-tenant request and token buckets plus one global token bucket per provider model; add\npriority borrowing and per-agent step budgets when agents appear. Measure rejection rate per tenant,\nquota utilisation against the provider's limit, reservation-to-actual ratio and upstream 429 rate; the\nlast should be near zero if the outbound limiter works. In interviews, expect to be asked why requests\nper minute is insufficient, how to limit when cost is unknown up front, and how to make a limiter correct\nacross replicas.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the bucket in Redis with an atomic script, wires reserve and settle around every provider call, and returns 429 with Retry-After from the API layer.","architect":"Allocates provider quota across tenants, priorities and regions, decides the safety margin, and defines what each plan tier is entitled to in tokens, not just requests.","fde":"Explains to a customer why their nightly batch job starved the chat assistant, and configures per-workload buckets or separate keys so the two no longer share one allowance."},"check":{"q":"You have a single provider key limited to one million tokens per minute, three application\nreplicas and forty tenants of very different sizes. Design the rate limiting and explain each choice.","a":"Two layers. Outbound: one global token bucket per provider model, held in Redis so all three\nreplicas share it, with capacity set to perhaps ten seconds of refill and the rate at about 80% of the\nlimit to leave headroom for retries. Each call reserves prompt tokens plus an output estimate and refunds\nthe difference on completion, because output length is unknown until the response ends. Inbound: a\nper-tenant token bucket sized from the tenant's plan, enforced before retrieval or prompt assembly so\nrejected requests cost nothing. Interactive traffic may borrow from global headroom before batch traffic\ndoes. Rejections return 429 with Retry-After and limit headers; a global shortfall returns 503 instead so\nclients back off correctly. I would read the provider's remaining-quota headers to slow down before the\nfirst upstream 429, and monitor upstream 429 rate, per-tenant rejection rate and reservation accuracy. If\none tenant genuinely needs most of the quota, I would give them a separate key rather than let them\ndominate the shared bucket."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is a requests-per-minute limit insufficient for LLM traffic?","options":["Providers do not publish request limits, only token limits","Requests vary in cost by orders of magnitude, so the real constraint, tokens per minute, can be exhausted by a few large requests","Request counts cannot be shared across replicas","Token limits are easier to compute than request counts"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A single 150k-token request costs as much as hundreds of short ones. Limiting requests alone leaves the token quota unprotected, which is why provider 429s appear while request dashboards look healthy."},{"q":"What is the main weakness of a fixed-window counter?","options":["It requires storing a timestamp for every request","A caller can spend a full allowance at the end of one window and again at the start of the next, producing a 2× burst","It cannot be implemented atomically in Redis","It does not allow any bursting at all"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fixed windows reset abruptly, so two full allowances can land within seconds across the boundary. Sliding windows and token buckets spread enforcement continuously and avoid the edge."},{"q":"Why does an LLM token limiter reserve at admission and settle at completion?","options":["Because provider APIs require clients to pre-declare token usage","To make the limiter work without a shared store","Because output token count is unknown until generation finishes, so the limiter must charge an estimate first and correct it afterwards","To allow retries to reuse the original reservation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Input tokens can be counted up front but output tokens cannot; `max_tokens` only bounds them. Reserving an estimate prevents overshoot while settling refunds unused quota so throughput is not stranded."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/idempotency","sources":["Official docs: Stripe API (idempotent requests)","IETF HTTPAPI working group — The Idempotency-Key HTTP Header Field (draft)"],"why":"A timeout is ambiguous. The request may never have arrived, may still be running, or may have completed\nafter the client stopped listening. The client cannot tell which, so its only options are to give up or\nto retry, and giving up is poor availability. Exactly-once delivery is impossible in general across a\nnetwork; what distributed systems achieve instead is at-least-once delivery combined with idempotent\nprocessing, which produces effectively-once results. That is why every durable queue, webhook and payment\nAPI is built around deduplication rather than around preventing duplicates.\n\nLLM systems have three properties that make this urgent. Generation is expensive: a retried 30-second,\n10k-token call doubles cost and latency, and if the first call actually completed you paid twice for one\nanswer. Generation is non-deterministic: the second attempt produces different text, so a user who\nclicked once may see two different replies to the same message. And agents execute tools with side\neffects: a retried step that calls `create_ticket` or `send_email` without protection does it twice,\nwhich is the failure that turns a flaky network into a customer-visible incident.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail predictably. \"Never retry\" sacrifices availability. \"Dedupe by content\"\nbreaks when identical content is legitimately repeated (\"send another reminder\"). \"Rely on a unique\ndatabase constraint\" works only when a natural key exists, which an LLM call rarely has. Idempotency keys\nsupply the missing key explicitly.","how":"### The key\nThe client generates a unique key, usually a UUID, per *logical operation* and reuses it on every retry\nof that operation. The key is scoped to the caller (tenant, API key) so collisions between callers are\nimpossible, and it expires after a retention window; Stripe, the canonical example, keeps keys for 24\nhours. The distinction between operation and attempt is the whole idea: a new key means \"do it again\",\nthe same key means \"make sure it happened once\".\n\n### The server algorithm\n1. Fingerprint the request body (a hash of its canonical serialisation).\n2. *Claim* the key: atomically insert `(key, fingerprint, status=in_progress)`. If the insert conflicts:\n   a different fingerprint means the key was reused for a different request, which is a client bug and\n   returns 422; `status=completed` means replay the stored response, same status code and body, with no\n   work done; `status=in_progress` means a duplicate is racing the original, so return 409 or wait for\n   the first to finish. Returning 409 rather than running a second copy is why the claim must be the\n   first write.\n3. Execute the operation.\n4. Store the outcome atomically with `status=completed`. Transient failures should release the claim so\n   a retry can run; permanent failures (4xx) can be stored and replayed.\n\nThe claim needs a lease. If the process crashes between steps 3 and 4 the key stays `in_progress`\nforever, so the row carries a short expiry after which a retry may take over. The lease length is a\ngenuine tension: it must outlast the slowest legitimate execution (a long generation or a slow tool),\nor a retry will take over a key whose first attempt is still running and you get the duplicate you were\npreventing; but every second of lease is a second a crashed attempt blocks recovery. Long operations\ntherefore renew the lease periodically (a heartbeat), and the completing write checks that it still\nholds the lease, for example by matching an attempt id stored in the row, before recording the outcome. This is also the honest\nlimit of the pattern: unless the side effect and the key store share one transaction, there is a window\nin which the effect happened but was not recorded, and the retry repeats it. Outbox patterns and\nreconciliation jobs exist to close that window.\n\n### Idempotent by design\nOften you can avoid the key store by making the operation naturally idempotent. Derive resource ids\ndeterministically (`job_id = hash(tenant, document_id, prompt_version)`) and insert with\n`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`. Prefer set semantics (\"the summary of document X is S\") to append or increment\nsemantics (\"add a summary\"). Use conditional writes with version numbers so a stale duplicate is rejected.\n\n### Where it lands in an LLM system\n- **Chat turns.** The client generates a message id when the user presses send; the server appends the\n  user turn and the assistant reply under that id only if absent, and stores the generated text under\n  it. A double click or a retried request returns the same reply instead of a second, different one, and\n  the second generation is never billed.\n- **Agent tool calls.** The key is derived from the run id plus the step or tool-call id and passed to\n  the tool. The tool implementation dedupes on it, and if it calls an external API that accepts an\n  idempotency header, it forwards the key. A regenerated model turn produces new tool-call ids, so the\n  derivation must include something stable about the step, not only the model's id.\n- **Queues and webhooks.** Consumers record processed message or event ids (the inbox pattern) because\n  deliveries are at-least-once by contract.\n- **Provider calls.** Most generation endpoints do not accept an idempotency key themselves (check the\n  specific provider), so your layer owns it: coalesce concurrent duplicates onto one in-flight call, and\n  replay the stored completion to late retries.\n\n### Streaming\nPartial output complicates replay. Persist the final text under the key when the stream completes; a\nretry that arrives mid-stream either attaches to the in-flight generation or restarts under the same\nmessage id so the client can discard the partial render.","example":"A minimal idempotency layer: claim first, fingerprint check, replay on completion, 409 on a racing\nduplicate, release on transient failure so the client can retry. In production the dictionary is a Redis\n`SET NX PX` or a table with a unique index, and `claim` is a single atomic operation.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, json, time\n\nclass IdempotencyStore:\n    \"\"\"In production: Redis SET NX PX, or a table with a unique index on key.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, ttl_s=86_400, lease_s=120):\n        self.rows, self.ttl_s, self.lease_s = {}, ttl_s, lease_s\n    def claim(self, key, fingerprint):\n        row = self.rows.get(key)\n        if row and row[\"expires\"] > time.monotonic():\n            if row[\"fp\"] != fingerprint:\n                return \"mismatch\", None\n            return row[\"status\"], row.get(\"response\")      # \"in_progress\" or \"done\"\n        self.rows[key] = {\"fp\": fingerprint, \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n                          \"expires\": time.monotonic() + self.lease_s}   # short lease while running\n        return \"claimed\", None\n    def complete(self, key, response):\n        self.rows[key].update(status=\"done\", response=response, expires=time.monotonic() + self.ttl_s)\n    def release(self, key):\n        self.rows.pop(key, None)\n\nstore = IdempotencyStore()\n\ndef handle_generate(idem_key: str, tenant: str, body: dict):\n    key = f\"{tenant}:{idem_key}\"                              # scope keys per caller\n    fp = hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()\n    status, saved = store.claim(key, fp)\n    if status == \"mismatch\":    return 422, {\"error\": \"idempotency key reused with a different body\"}\n    if status == \"done\":        return 200, saved             # replay: no second generation, no second bill\n    if status == \"in_progress\": return 409, {\"error\": \"already in flight; retry shortly\"}\n    try:\n        resp = llm.generate(**body)       # expensive, non-deterministic; must finish within lease_s\n    except TransientError:\n        store.release(key)                                    # let the client retry with the same key\n        raise                             # a permanent error could be stored and replayed instead\n    store.complete(key, resp)\n    return 200, resp\n```","tradeoffs":["A key store adds a write and a read to every request and a dependency on the store; when a deterministic natural id exists, an upsert achieves the same guarantee for free.","Replaying the stored response gives consistency but means a retry can never get a better answer; \"regenerate\" must therefore be a new key, not a retry.","Returning 409 to a racing duplicate is simple and safe; coalescing it onto the in-flight call gives a better experience but holds connections and needs more state.","Stored responses double as a cache, but they are now retained user content with privacy and retention obligations, so the TTL is a policy decision as well as a technical one."],"pitfalls":["Generating the key inside the retry loop so each attempt gets a fresh one; the symptom is duplicates persisting despite \"idempotency\" being implemented.","Check-then-insert instead of an atomic claim; the symptom is duplicates that appear only under concurrency or load tests and never in local runs.","No fingerprint check; the symptom is a client bug that reuses keys silently returning one request's answer to an unrelated request.","Storing transient failures as the final outcome; the symptom is a momentary 503 becoming permanent for that key until it expires.","Agent tool calls keyed only on the model's tool-call id; the symptom is duplicate tickets after a regenerated turn, because the regenerated call carries a new id.","A lease shorter than the slowest generation or tool call; the symptom is duplicates that appear only on long requests, with two attempts for the same key overlapping in the trace.","Assuming temperature zero makes generation idempotent; the symptom is subtly different replayed outputs, since determinism is not guaranteed across hardware and batch composition."],"when":"Require an idempotency key on every endpoint that creates something, costs money or triggers a side\neffect, which in an LLM product means message creation, job submission and every tool with an external\neffect. Skip the key store where a natural deterministic id makes the write an upsert, and where the\noperation is a pure read. Pair it with the retry policy: retries are only safe because of this. Measure\nreplay rate (how often a key is reused) and 409 rate; a high replay rate points at an overly aggressive\nclient, a nonzero duplicate-effect rate points at a window in the claim-execute-store sequence. In\ninterviews, the expected answer defines idempotency precisely, explains why exactly-once is impossible\nand at-least-once plus idempotency is the substitute, and walks through claim, fingerprint, replay and\nlease.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Adds the key header to the API, implements the atomic claim and replay in Redis or Postgres, and threads run and step ids into every agent tool call.","architect":"Decides which operations must be idempotent, where the key store lives relative to the side effect, and how reconciliation closes the gap the store cannot.","fde":"Audits a customer's agent integration for tools that email, charge or create records without keys, and proves the fix with a chaos test that injects timeouts mid-step."},"check":{"q":"An agent step calls a `create_invoice` tool and the LLM request times out after the tool has\nalready run. How do you make the whole system safe to retry end to end?","a":"Treat each layer separately. The user's turn carries a client-generated message id, so the retried\nturn replays or attaches to the in-flight run instead of starting a second one. Inside the run, each\ntool call gets an idempotency key derived from the run id and a stable step identifier, not only the\nmodel's tool-call id, which changes if the turn is regenerated. The `create_invoice` implementation\nclaims that key atomically before acting, forwards it as the idempotency header to the billing API if\nthat API supports one, and stores the invoice id under the key; a retried step then returns the existing\ninvoice instead of creating another. The LLM call itself is wrapped so a retry after timeout coalesces\nonto the in-flight generation or replays the stored completion. Finally, because the key store and the\nbilling system are not one transaction, a reconciliation job compares invoices against recorded steps to\ncatch the rare window where the effect happened but the record did not."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which operation is idempotent by construction, with no key store needed?","options":["Append the model's reply to the conversation","Set the summary of document 42 to the generated text","Increment the tenant's token counter by the response usage","Send the generated digest email to the user"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Setting a value to the same thing twice leaves one state; appending, incrementing and sending accumulate with each repeat. Designing writes as sets instead of appends removes the need for deduplication. If the text is regenerated on the retry the final value may differ, but there is still exactly one summary."},{"q":"A client sends a request with idempotency key K, times out, and retries with K while the first attempt is still generating. What should the server do?","options":["Start a second generation, since the first may have failed","Detect the in-progress claim on K and return 409 (or attach the retry to the in-flight result) instead of generating again","Return the stored response for K, which is empty","Reject the retry with 422 because the key was reused"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The atomic claim exists precisely to catch this race: the key is in progress, so a second execution would duplicate cost and effects. 422 is for a key reused with a different body, which this is not."},{"q":"Why should the server store a fingerprint of the request body alongside the idempotency key?","options":["To allow the key to be shorter than a UUID","To let the server compress the stored response","To detect a key being reused for a different request, which is a client bug that would otherwise return the wrong stored response","To make the key unique across tenants"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Keys are generated by clients, and clients have bugs. Without the fingerprint a reused key silently replays a response to an unrelated request; with it the server can reject the mismatch explicitly."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/caching","sources":["Official docs: Anthropic prompt caching","Official docs: OpenAI prompt caching","Zheng et al., 2024 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs (RadixAttention)","Bang, 2023 — GPTCache: An Open-Source Semantic Cache for LLM Applications"],"why":"The cost structure forces it. Every call pays for prefill in proportion to prompt length, and in agent and\nRAG systems most of the prompt is identical from call to call: the system prompt, tool schemas, few-shot\nexamples, a long document. A 40-turn agent session with a 12k-token prefix re-processes roughly half a\nmillion tokens of bytes the model has already seen, and at long contexts prefill dominates time to first\ntoken. Separately, many products see repeated questions (\"what is your refund policy\"), where the whole\ngeneration repeats.\n\nThe naive answer, \"cache responses like any HTTP response\", fails in three ways. Exact hits are rare in\nconversational traffic, because prompts contain user-specific and time-specific content, so the hit rate\nrounds to zero. When a hit does occur, non-determinism and personalisation mean the cached text may be\nwrong for the new context: a cached answer about *this* customer's order is a data leak for the next one.\nAnd response caches do nothing for the dominant cost in agent loops, where every turn is new but the\nprefix is identical.\n\nPrompt caching exists for the third problem: it attacks prefill compute without touching the answer.\nSemantic caching exists for the first: it trades exactness for hit rate. Each introduces a specific\nfailure mode, no speedup for one and wrong answers for the other, which is why the layers must be\nunderstood separately before being combined.","how":"### Layer 1: exact-match response cache\nKey: a hash of everything that affects the output: model id and version, system prompt, messages, tool\ndefinitions, sampling parameters, and any retrieved context. Hit: return the stored response. It is most\nvaluable in deterministic pipelines (classification, extraction, embedding of the same chunk) where\nidentical inputs really recur. Because the key includes the prompt template, bump a template version to\ninvalidate on change. Product semantics decide whether a cached answer is acceptable at temperature above\nzero; a \"regenerate\" action must bypass it.\n\n### Layer 2: semantic cache\nGPTCache is a well-known open-source implementation; the data flow is the same in any of them:\n1. **Scope.** Build a namespace from tenant, corpus or knowledge-base version, prompt version and\n   model, *because* a similar question asked against a different tenant's data or an older policy must\n   never hit. Lookups only search inside the namespace.\n2. **Embed.** Embed the normalised query, usually the latest user message with any context that changes\n   the answer folded in. Embedding only the last message is what makes \"and for business accounts?\"\n   match an unrelated earlier question.\n3. **Search.** Nearest-neighbour lookup over stored queries in the namespace; take the best match and\n   its similarity.\n4. **Decide.** Hit if similarity ≥ τ. The threshold is a precision/recall knob: \"how do I cancel my\n   order\" and \"how do I cancel my subscription\" embed close together and have different answers, so a\n   threshold tuned only for hit rate ships wrong answers. A cheap verifier call (\"does this stored answer\n   answer this question?\") can raise precision at some latency cost.\n5. **Fill and expire.** On a miss, generate, then store query, embedding and answer with a TTL, and\n   delete the namespace (or bump its version) when the underlying knowledge changes, because the cache\n   does not know the policy page was edited.\n\nMeasurement must track hit *precision* (the fraction of sampled hits a human or judge rates correct)\nalongside hit rate, because the failure mode is a confident wrong answer that no error metric sees. It\nfits FAQ-like, high-repeat, low-personalisation traffic, and fits chat with history or user-specific\ncontext poorly.\n\n### Layer 3: prompt caching (prefix KV reuse)\nThe engine keeps the key and value tensors computed for a prompt prefix and, when a new request's token\nsequence begins with the same prefix, skips prefill for those tokens and processes only the suffix.\nProviders expose this as a billing and latency feature; self-hosted engines expose it as a scheduler\nfeature (vLLM's automatic prefix caching by block hash, SGLang's RadixAttention tree with LRU eviction).\n\nThe rules follow from the mechanism. Matching is on the exact token sequence in order, so the request is\nrendered as tools, then system, then messages, and any change at position *i* invalidates everything\nafter it; static content goes first and volatile content (timestamps, user names, per-request retrieved\ndocuments) goes last. The cache is per model and isolated per organisation or workspace. Entries have a\ntime to live, and hits are not guaranteed under load, so the only ground truth is the usage report: on\nAnthropic, `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` alongside the uncached\n`input_tokens`; OpenAI reports cached tokens inside its usage details. An entry becomes readable only\nonce the writing request has begun responding, so N parallel identical requests all pay full price.\n\nVendor specifics at the time of writing, which change: Anthropic uses explicit `cache_control`\nbreakpoints (up to four per request) with a default five-minute TTL refreshed on every read and an\noptional one-hour TTL; cache writes cost more than ordinary input (1.25× for five minutes, 2× for one\nhour) and reads cost a fraction of input (around 0.1×); the minimum cacheable prefix is\nmodel-dependent, on the order of one to a few thousand tokens, and shorter prefixes silently do not\ncache. OpenAI caches automatically for prompts above a minimum length and discounts cached input\ntokens. On both, caching reduces input cost and time to first token; it does nothing for output tokens or\ndecode speed.\n\n### Which layer for which traffic\nFAQ bot: semantic plus exact. Extraction or classification pipeline: exact. Agent loops, multi-turn chat\nand RAG with a large static context: prompt caching. Embedding generation: exact. Most real systems run\nprompt caching always and add a response-level cache only where repeats are measured.","example":"Explicit prompt caching on the Anthropic API, with the static manual placed before the breakpoint and the\nvolatile question after it; the usage fields confirm whether the second call read the prefix. A worked\ncost scenario follows the code.\n\n```python\nimport anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\nMANUAL = open(\"policy_manual.md\").read()          # ~20k tokens, identical on every request\n\ndef ask(question: str) -> str:\n    resp = client.messages.create(\n        model=\"claude-opus-5-5\",\n        max_tokens=1024,\n        system=[\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Answer questions using only the policy manual below.\"},\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": MANUAL,\n             \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},   # breakpoint: everything up to here is cacheable\n        ],\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}],   # volatile content after the breakpoint\n    )\n    u = resp.usage\n    print(f\"uncached={u.input_tokens} cache_write={u.cache_creation_input_tokens} \"\n          f\"cache_read={u.cache_read_input_tokens} output={u.output_tokens}\")\n    return next(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == \"text\")\n\nask(\"What is the refund window?\")      # first call: cache_write ≈ 20k, cache_read = 0\nask(\"Who can approve an exception?\")   # within the TTL: cache_read ≈ 20k, uncached ≈ the question\n```\n\nWorked scenario with illustrative prices. Take a 20,000-token manual, a 200-token question, a 400-token\nanswer, and 1,000 questions a day arriving more often than every five minutes, so the entry stays warm.\nAssume 3 per million input tokens, 3.75 per million for cache writes (1.25×), 0.30 per million for cache\nreads (0.1×) and 15 per million output tokens. Without caching, daily input cost is\n1,000 × 20,200 × 3 / 10⁶ ≈ 60.6. With caching it is one write (20,000 × 3.75 / 10⁶ ≈ 0.08), 1,000 reads\n(1,000 × 20,000 × 0.30 / 10⁶ = 6.0) and the uncached questions (1,000 × 200 × 3 / 10⁶ = 0.6), about\n6.7, a nine-fold reduction on input. Output cost, 1,000 × 400 × 15 / 10⁶ = 6.0, is unchanged, so the\ntotal falls from about 66.6 to about 12.7. Time to first token drops by roughly the time it took to\nprefill 20,000 tokens. The break-even is instructive: at 1.25× write and 0.1× read, a prefix reused even\nonce within the TTL is already cheaper than not caching (1.35× versus 2×).","tradeoffs":["An exact cache can never return content that was wrong for its key, but its hit rate on free-form conversation is close to zero; it earns its keep in pipelines and embedding jobs, not in chat.","A semantic cache can reach high hit rates on FAQ traffic, but every hit is a bet that similarity implies same answer; it flips to a liability when answers are personalised, when the corpus changes often, or when a wrong answer is costly.","Prompt caching makes reads cheap but writes cost more than plain input, so a prefix used once per TTL costs more than no caching; with a 1.25× write and 0.1× read the break-even is a single reuse, but a one-hour TTL at a 2× write needs more.","Prefix ordering constraints fight prompt-engineering habits such as putting the date or the user's name at the top; every byte of volatile content moved earlier shortens the cacheable prefix.","On self-hosted engines a resident prefix cache occupies KV memory that could hold running sequences, so cache retention competes with batch size under load."],"pitfalls":["A timestamp, request id or user name early in the system prompt; the symptom is `cache_read_input_tokens` stuck at zero while the bill is unchanged or slightly higher, because every request pays the write premium for an entry nothing ever reads.","A prefix shorter than the model's minimum; the symptom is no cache-creation tokens ever reported despite the breakpoint.","Tool definitions rendered in nondeterministic order, or a tool list that varies per request; the symptom is intermittent hits that correlate with which feature flags were on.","A semantic cache without tenant scoping; the symptom is answers that reference another customer's documents, which is a security incident, not a quality bug.","A similarity threshold tuned for hit rate only; the symptom is support tickets saying the assistant answered a different question.","Caching responses at temperature above zero without a bypass on \"regenerate\"; the symptom is regenerate returning identical text.","Reporting cache hit rate instead of cost and latency deltas; the symptom is a dashboard at 90% hits while the bill barely moves, because the hits were on short prefixes.","Expecting decode to speed up; the symptom is unchanged tokens per second after enabling caching, which is correct, since only prefill is skipped."],"when":"Turn prompt caching on wherever a prompt has a stable prefix above the model's minimum: agent loops, long\nsystem prompts, document question answering, few-shot classifiers. Add an exact-match cache in\ndeterministic pipelines and for embeddings. Add a semantic cache only for repeat-heavy,\nlow-personalisation traffic, with tenant scoping, explicit invalidation and a measured hit precision.\nMeasure cache-read tokens as a fraction of total input, time to first token before and after, cost per\nrequest, and for semantic caches both hit rate and hit precision. Day to day it shows up as \"why did the\nbill double after we added the date to the system prompt\"; in interviews, the expected depth is\ndistinguishing the three layers, explaining why prompt caching needs ordered, byte-identical prefixes,\nand naming the semantic cache's wrong-answer risk.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Orders the prompt static-first, places breakpoints, asserts in integration tests that a second identical request reports cache reads, and keys response caches on every input that affects output.","architect":"Decides which layers each product surface gets, sets tenant isolation and retention rules for cached content, and models the cost and latency impact against provider pricing.","fde":"Finds the one dynamic line a customer put at the top of their system prompt, restores their cache hits, and explains why their semantic cache must be partitioned per business unit."},"check":{"q":"An agent product makes about 40 model calls per session over a 12k-token system-and-tools prefix\nfollowed by a growing conversation. Design its caching and state what each layer buys and cannot buy.","a":"Prompt caching is the main lever. Render tools and system prompt first and keep them byte-stable;\nput a breakpoint at the end of that 12k prefix so every call reads it instead of prefilling it, and let\nthe conversation tail be cached incrementally so each turn writes only its own delta. That removes most\ninput cost and most time to first token, but it does nothing for output tokens or decode speed, and it\nneeds the session to stay inside the TTL or to be re-warmed. An exact-match response cache buys little\nhere, since every turn is new, except for deterministic sub-calls such as embeddings or classification\ninside tools. A semantic cache is inappropriate for the main loop because answers depend on conversation\nstate and would be wrong on a near-miss hit. I would verify with the usage fields, assert cache reads in\nan integration test, and watch the bill for regressions when someone adds a dynamic line to the system\nprompt."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does provider-side prompt caching reduce?","options":["Output token cost and decode latency","Input token cost and time to first token for the cached prefix","The number of model calls needed per conversation","The size of the context window needed for the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The provider reuses the KV cache for the prefix so prefill is skipped and cached input is billed at a discount. Generation of the answer still happens in full, so output cost and decode speed are unchanged."},{"q":"Why does changing one word at the start of the system prompt eliminate prompt-cache hits?","options":["Because the provider hashes the whole request and any change invalidates the hash","Because the cache matches the exact token sequence in order, so a change at position i invalidates every cached position after it","Because system prompts are never cached, only messages","Because changing the prompt resets the five-minute TTL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"KV entries for position i depend on all earlier tokens, so matching is a prefix match on the token sequence. An early change means no later position can be reused, which is why volatile content belongs at the end."},{"q":"What is the primary risk of a semantic cache?","options":["It increases time to first token for cache misses","It cannot be scoped per tenant","A query similar in embedding space but different in meaning receives a confident, wrong cached answer","It requires the model to support prompt caching"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embedding similarity is not answer equivalence. \"Cancel my order\" and \"cancel my subscription\" sit close together and need different answers, so hit precision must be measured and the threshold tuned for correctness, not hit rate."}]},{"key":"serving-inference/engines","sources":["Kwon et al., 2023 — Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","Zheng et al., 2024 — SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","Official docs: TensorRT-LLM","Official docs: llama.cpp and Ollama"],"why":"A plain `model.generate()` loop in a training framework serves one request at a time, allocates KV cache\ncontiguously at maximum length, and runs generic attention kernels. The result is single-digit GPU\nutilisation and throughput tens of times below what the hardware can do, because decode is\nmemory-bandwidth-bound and only batching amortises the weight reads. Serving well requires a long list of\nsystems work: continuous batching so new requests join between decode steps, paged KV cache so memory is\nnot lost to fragmentation, fused attention kernels, quantized weight kernels, prefix caching, speculative\ndecoding, constrained decoding for structured output, tensor parallelism across GPUs, a streaming HTTP\nAPI, metrics and graceful draining. Each of these is a research-paper-sized problem; engines package them\nso an application team does not rebuild them.\n\nThe other end of the spectrum has a different problem. Running a 7B model on a laptop or an edge box\nneeds memory-mapped quantized weights, CPU vector instructions, Apple Metal or a consumer GPU, and a\nbinary with no Python dependency tree. That is llama.cpp's reason to exist, and Ollama's reason is to\nmake it a one-line install with a model registry.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail predictably. \"Write our own server on Transformers\" loses on throughput and\nmemory. \"Only use a hosted API\" fails when data cannot leave the premises, when per-token prices exceed\nowning GPUs at volume, or when you need a fine-tuned or open-weight model the provider does not host.\nEngines are the layer that makes self-hosting viable.","how":"### Shared machinery\nAll the datacenter engines implement the same core ideas: a scheduler that batches at token granularity\n(continuous or in-flight batching), a block-based KV cache with a page table (PagedAttention or an\nequivalent), prefix sharing across requests, quantized weight formats (AWQ, GPTQ, FP8 and relatives), an\nOpenAI-compatible HTTP server with streaming, and grammar-constrained decoding. They differ in emphasis,\nhardware reach, model format and how much work it takes to run them.\n\n### A request's path through a datacenter engine\n1. **Startup sizing.** The engine loads weights, runs a profiling pass to measure peak activation\n   memory, and turns whatever remains of its memory allowance into a fixed pool of KV blocks. In vLLM\n   the allowance is `--gpu-memory-utilization` × GPU memory, so the KV pool is that minus weights minus\n   activations; the startup log reports the resulting capacity in tokens. Everything later is bounded by\n   this pool, *because* KV memory, not compute, is what limits concurrency.\n2. **Admission.** The HTTP layer applies the chat template, tokenizes, and hands the request to the\n   scheduler's waiting queue. Applying the template inside the engine is why a wrong template corrupts\n   output silently: the model sees a format it was never trained on.\n3. **Scheduling each step.** Every iteration the scheduler picks which waiting requests to prefill and\n   which running ones to decode, subject to the sequence cap and free blocks. Prefill is often split\n   into chunks so a 30k-token prompt does not stall every running decode for a whole step.\n4. **Prefix lookup.** Before prefilling, the engine checks whether the prompt's leading blocks are\n   already cached and reuses them, so only the new suffix is computed.\n5. **Decode and stream.** Each step emits one token per running sequence (more with speculative\n   decoding), applies any grammar mask for structured output, detokenizes incrementally and streams.\n6. **Finish or preempt.** On a stop condition the blocks return to the pool. If a running sequence needs\n   a block and none is free, the engine preempts some sequence and recomputes or swaps its cache later,\n   which shows up as latency spikes rather than errors.\n\nThe engines differ mainly in steps 3 to 5: scheduler policy, how prefixes are matched, and how fast the\nkernels are, which is why they must be compared on your traffic rather than on a single prompt.\n\n### vLLM\nThe engine that introduced PagedAttention. Python-first, loads Hugging Face checkpoints directly,\nsupports the widest range of models and several hardware backends (NVIDIA first, AMD and others\nfollowing), tensor and pipeline parallelism, automatic prefix caching, speculative decoding, multi-LoRA\nserving and guided decoding. `vllm serve <model>` gives an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; the flags that\nmatter most are `--max-model-len`, `--gpu-memory-utilization`, `--max-num-seqs` and\n`--tensor-parallel-size`. It is the default choice for production serving on NVIDIA hardware because of\nbreadth and community pace.\n\n### SGLang\nFrom the LMSYS group. Its distinctive piece is RadixAttention: the KV cache is organised as a radix tree\nover token sequences, so any request sharing a prefix with any cached sequence reuses it automatically,\nwith LRU eviction. That makes it strong for multi-turn chat, agents and few-shot workloads where prefixes\nrepeat heavily. It also ships a frontend language for structured programs (parallel calls, constrained\ngeneration) and fast constrained decoding. API and deployment model are similar to vLLM; teams usually\nbenchmark both on their own traffic.\n\n### TGI (Text Generation Inference)\nHugging Face's server: a Rust router in front of Python model shards, continuous batching, paged\nattention, several quantization backends, token streaming, and an OpenAI-style Messages API. Its draw is\nintegration with the Hub and Inference Endpoints; if your fleet already lives in the Hugging Face\necosystem it is the shortest path. Its feature pace on the newest serving tricks has generally trailed\nvLLM and SGLang, so check the project's current maintenance status and roadmap before choosing it for a\nnew deployment.\n\n### TensorRT-LLM\nNVIDIA's engine. Its distinctive workflow compiles models ahead of time into TensorRT engines specialised\nfor a GPU architecture and a configuration (maximum batch size, sequence length, parallelism), which lets\nit fuse kernels aggressively and use FP8 on Hopper-class parts (recent releases also add a PyTorch-based\nbackend that loads checkpoints directly, narrowing the operational gap, but the compiled path remains the\nreason to choose it); it implements in-flight batching and paged KV\ncache and is typically served behind Triton Inference Server (NVIDIA's NIM containers build on the same\nstack). It has the highest performance ceiling on NVIDIA hardware and the highest operational cost: a\nbuild step per model and GPU type, NVIDIA-only, and rebuilds when limits change.\n\n### llama.cpp\nA C/C++ runtime built around the GGUF weight format and its family of k-quant formats (Q4_K_M and\nrelatives). It runs on CPUs with SIMD, on Apple Silicon through Metal, and on GPUs through CUDA, Vulkan\nand others, and can offload a chosen number of layers to the GPU (`-ngl`) while keeping the rest in\nsystem RAM. `llama-server` exposes an OpenAI-compatible API. It is designed for one or a few users on\ncommodity hardware; it has batching, but not the many-user throughput machinery of the datacenter engines.\n\n### Ollama\nA packaging layer over llama.cpp and its ggml library (newer releases run some model families on\nOllama's own ggml-based engine, but the design and limits are the same): a model registry (`ollama pull`), a Modelfile for prompts and\nparameters, a REST API on port 11434 with an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and automatic GPU detection. It\nis the standard developer-laptop and prototyping runtime. Defaults are single-user; environment variables\nsuch as `OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL` and the per-model context length need changing before it serves a team,\nand it inherits llama.cpp's throughput ceiling.\n\n### Side by side\n| Engine | Target | Concurrency machinery | Hardware | Weight format |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| vLLM | datacenter | continuous batching, paged KV, prefix cache | NVIDIA, AMD, others | HF checkpoints; AWQ/GPTQ/FP8 |\n| SGLang | datacenter | as vLLM, plus RadixAttention | NVIDIA, AMD | HF checkpoints; quantized variants |\n| TGI | datacenter, HF ecosystem | continuous batching, paged attention | NVIDIA, AMD, others | HF checkpoints; several quant backends |\n| TensorRT-LLM | datacenter, peak performance | in-flight batching, paged KV | NVIDIA only | compiled TensorRT engines |\n| llama.cpp | laptop, edge, CPU | small-scale batching | CPU, Apple Silicon, consumer GPUs | GGUF |\n| Ollama | developer machine | single-user defaults | as llama.cpp | GGUF via registry |","example":"The same client code runs against a datacenter engine and a laptop runtime because both speak the OpenAI\nchat API; only the base URL and model name change. The vLLM flags set the context ceiling, the share of\nGPU memory handed to weights plus KV cache, the batch cap and prefix caching.\n\n```bash\n# Datacenter: vLLM serving an 8B instruct model, OpenAI-compatible API on :8000\nvllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct --max-model-len 8192 \\\n  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.90 --max-num-seqs 128 --enable-prefix-caching\n# Laptop: Ollama (llama.cpp underneath), OpenAI-compatible API on :11434\nollama pull llama3.1:8b && ollama serve\n```\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nENGINES = {\"vllm\":   (\"http://localhost:8000/v1\",  \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\"),\n           \"ollama\": (\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", \"llama3.1:8b\")}\n\ndef stream(engine: str, prompt: str):\n    base_url, model = ENGINES[engine]\n    client = OpenAI(base_url=base_url, api_key=\"not-needed\")   # local engines ignore the key\n    resp = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=model, max_tokens=128, stream=True,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n    )\n    for chunk in resp:\n        delta = chunk.choices[0].delta.content\n        if delta:\n            print(delta, end=\"\", flush=True)\n    print()\n\nstream(\"vllm\", \"Explain continuous batching in two sentences.\")\nstream(\"ollama\", \"Explain continuous batching in two sentences.\")\n# Same code, two engines: what differs is throughput under concurrency, not the API.\n```\n\nWhat do those vLLM flags buy on one 80 GB GPU? Llama-3.1-8B has 32 layers, 8 KV heads and head\ndimension 128, so in bf16 each token's cache is 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB. The memory\nallowance is 0.90 × 80 = 72 GB; weights take about 16 GB; allow a few GB for activations and CUDA\ngraphs (illustrative), leaving roughly 52 GB of KV pool, or about 400,000 tokens. That is only about 48\nsequences at the full 8,192-token context, so `--max-num-seqs 128` is reachable only if the average\nlive sequence is around 3,000 tokens or less; beyond that, the scheduler holds requests in the waiting\nqueue or preempts. Raising `--max-model-len` to 32k does not change the pool, it only lets fewer, longer\nsequences use it.","tradeoffs":["vLLM's breadth (models, hardware, features) comes with a fast-moving codebase; pinning versions and re-benchmarking on upgrade is part of owning it, whereas a compiled TensorRT-LLM engine is frozen and predictable until you need to change it.","TensorRT-LLM's peak throughput is bought with an ahead-of-time build per model, GPU type and configuration; it pays off for a few high-volume models on a homogeneous NVIDIA fleet and not for a long tail of models or mixed hardware.","SGLang's radix-tree prefix sharing wins when traffic has heavy prefix reuse (agents, multi-turn, shared system prompts); on single-shot traffic with unique prompts the advantage over vLLM shrinks to whatever the current kernels give.","llama.cpp and Ollama run anywhere with no Python stack, but their throughput under many concurrent users is far below a GPU engine; they are the right tool for one user per box and the wrong tool for a shared service.","TGI's ecosystem fit (Hub, Inference Endpoints) saves integration work, but adopting it means accepting Hugging Face's cadence for serving optimisations."],"pitfalls":["Benchmarking a single request and choosing llama.cpp or Ollama for a multi-user service; the symptom is latency that climbs steeply as users are added, because batching is limited to a handful of parallel slots rather than the hundreds of sequences a datacenter scheduler juggles.","Setting `--max-model-len` and `--gpu-memory-utilization` without doing the KV-cache arithmetic; the symptom is an OOM at startup or a scheduler that admits only a handful of sequences.","Mixing weight formats; the symptom is a GGUF file that the datacenter engines load only experimentally or not at all, or an AWQ checkpoint llama.cpp cannot read, discovered on deploy day.","Assuming \"OpenAI-compatible\" means identical behaviour; the symptom is tool calls and JSON mode that parse differently across engines and models, because each implements function calling with its own parser and chat template.","Running Ollama with its default context length; the symptom is long prompts silently truncated, since the runtime applies its own context window unless the model or request overrides it.","Serving a base model with an instruct chat template, or the reverse; the symptom is rambling or never-terminating output.","A TensorRT engine built on one GPU type deployed to another; the symptom is a load failure on the new nodes after a hardware refresh."],"when":"Pick by three questions. Where does it run: developer laptop or edge means llama.cpp or Ollama; a shared\nGPU service means one of the datacenter engines. How much concurrency and what traffic shape: unique\nsingle-shot prompts favour vLLM, heavy prefix reuse and structured programs favour SGLang, a few very\nhigh-volume models on a homogeneous NVIDIA fleet justify TensorRT-LLM, a Hugging Face-centric platform\npoints at TGI. How much operational effort can you spend: compiled engines need a build pipeline, vLLM\nand SGLang need version discipline, Ollama needs almost nothing. Measure tokens per second at your target\nconcurrency, time to first token at p95, and GPU memory headroom; never choose on a single-request\nbenchmark. In interviews, be ready to explain what continuous batching and paged KV cache do, why\nllama.cpp and vLLM are not competitors, and what the TensorRT-LLM build step buys and costs.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Runs the engine, tunes context length, memory utilisation and batch caps, enables prefix caching and quantization, and benchmarks candidates on recorded production traffic.","architect":"Chooses the engine family per deployment target, decides whether the TensorRT-LLM build pipeline is worth its rigidity, and standardises on an OpenAI-compatible contract so engines stay swappable.","fde":"Stands up vLLM or SGLang inside a customer's VPC, or Ollama on an analyst's workstation, and translates their \"it is slow\" into the batching and memory numbers that explain it."},"check":{"q":"A customer prototyped a support assistant on Ollama on a workstation and wants to roll it out to\n300 agents on two NVIDIA GPUs. What do you change, and what do you keep?","a":"Keep the model family, the prompts and the OpenAI-compatible client code, since those transfer.\nChange the runtime: Ollama's llama.cpp core serves one or a few users well but lacks the large-batch\nscheduler and paged KV-cache machinery that 300 concurrent users need, so latency would grow with every\nadded user.\nMove to vLLM or SGLang on the GPUs; SGLang if the support prompts share a large system prefix, because\nRadixAttention reuses it automatically, otherwise vLLM for breadth. Switch from the GGUF file to the\nHugging Face checkpoint, quantized with AWQ or FP8 if memory demands it, and do the KV-cache arithmetic\nto set the context ceiling and memory utilisation so the scheduler can hold enough sequences. Put the two\nGPUs behind one server with tensor parallelism or run two replicas behind a load balancer, depending on\nmodel size. Benchmark on recorded traffic at target concurrency, verify tool calling still parses, and\nadd backpressure and metrics in front. TensorRT-LLM is a later optimisation if the model and fleet stay\nfixed."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which engine's distinctive workflow compiles the model ahead of time for a specific GPU architecture and configuration?","options":["vLLM","SGLang","TensorRT-LLM","TGI"],"answer":2,"explanation":"TensorRT-LLM builds a TensorRT engine specialised to the GPU type, batch and sequence limits, which enables aggressive kernel fusion but means a rebuild when hardware or limits change. The others load checkpoints at startup and need no per-GPU build step."},{"q":"What is the relationship between Ollama and llama.cpp?","options":["They are independent implementations of the same GGUF specification","Ollama packages llama.cpp with a model registry, a REST API and defaults for local use","llama.cpp is a GPU-only fork of Ollama","Ollama is the datacenter version of llama.cpp with continuous batching"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ollama is built on llama.cpp and its ggml library; it adds pulling models, a Modelfile, an HTTP API and device detection. It inherits llama.cpp's strengths on commodity hardware and its limits under many concurrent users."},{"q":"Which workload benefits most from SGLang's RadixAttention relative to a standard paged KV cache?","options":["Single-shot requests with unique prompts","Multi-turn chat and agent loops where many requests share long prefixes","Offline batch embedding of documents","CPU-only inference on edge devices"],"answer":1,"explanation":"RadixAttention stores the KV cache as a radix tree over token sequences, so any request whose prefix matches a cached one reuses it without recomputation. vLLM's block-hash prefix caching also reuses shared prefixes, so the gap is one of degree; it is largest in multi-turn and agent traffic, where branching conversations share long, irregular prefixes."}]}]});
